('9440aaa3-a930-44b8-97b1-2fe245e80801', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d', 'pyq', 'Business Planning & Entrepreneurship Management - October 2025', 'Practice PYQs and Ace your Endsems.', '', 2025, 'https://h36y6svq.us-east.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cue-pyqs/objects/1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d%2F2025%2Ff39e66c7-bdcb-4b42-97c7-d3ab078d67bb-business-planning-and-entrepreneurship-management-oct2025.pdf?v=ad38024c7467feebf05c5ec7758da5e0', '1ba3fabc-a2c8-4c58-a388-cb07c2a4d70d/2025/f39e66c7-bdcb-4b42-97c7-d3ab078d67bb-business-planning-and-entrepreneurship-management-oct2025.pdf', 1, true, 'Regular Examination', 'Business_Planning_and_Entrepreneurship_Management_Oct2025.pdf', 60090, 'f39e66c7-bdcb-4b42-97c7-d3ab078d67bb', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-17 07:35:34.633207+00', '2026-09-17 12:26:44.844251+00'),
('e6eda6b6-a7af-48b4-b17b-9686508cbddb', '5c93c785-bc3e-4489-969c-9ec5f21c174a', 'pyq', 'Eco ll- October 2024', '', '', 2024, 'https://h36y6svq.us-east.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cue-pyqs/objects/5c93c785-bc3e-4489-969c-9ec5f21c174a%2F2024%2F5b08ca41-bfde-4c9c-b868-47645752f4e5-principles-of-economics-ii-oct2024.pdf?v=09428cf64ee0c9aad8f2611df45a3e06', '5c93c785-bc3e-4489-969c-9ec5f21c174a/2024/5b08ca41-bfde-4c9c-b868-47645752f4e5-principles-of-economics-ii-oct2024.pdf', 2, true, 'Regular Examination', 'Principles_of_Economics_II_Oct2024.pdf', 3557715, '5b08ca41-bfde-4c9c-b868-47645752f4e5', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-17 18:34:26.458536+00', '2026-09-17 18:35:59.670814+00'),
('d6348681-f477-4c19-ae6a-a8764bd6d68c', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'pyq', 'EDM - October 2024', '', '', 2024, 'https://h36y6svq.us-east.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/cue-pyqs/objects/4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11%2F2024%2Fd6749521-e7ca-476a-9bec-45fd90d26a33-equity-and-debt-markets-oct2024.pdf?v=cf35c8e3ab23fb5bd9dd8f87530bd298', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11/2024/d6749521-e7ca-476a-9bec-45fd90d26a33-equity-and-debt-markets-oct2024.pdf', 2, true, 'Regular Examination', 'Equity_and_Debt_Markets_Oct2024.pdf', 4430616, 'd6749521-e7ca-476a-9bec-45fd90d26a33', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '2026-09-17 19:10:56.707507+00', '2026-09-17 19:10:56.707507+00'),
('0f67486d-f258-46ee-a3a1-6904c9319349', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Define an Equity Share.', '', 'An equity share represents part-ownership of a company. A holder of equity shares ("equity shareholder") contributes capital and becomes a part-owner with: A residual claim on profits (dividend, if declared) Voting rights in company decisions A residual claim on assets on winding up (after all other claims are settled) Equity shares are also called ordinary shares; holders bear the highest risk but also have the highest potential return among a company''s capital providers. Exam Tip: Always mention "ownership," "residual claim," and "voting rights." Common Trap: Don''t confuse equity shares with debentures/bonds — equity = ownership + risk; debt = loan + fixed return, no ownership.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'a2aabef7-6d37-4560-b100-28902f5cd163', 'dc365377-2a45-4a2b-9c54-b6feaa2aec58', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u1-1-q-1", "text": "Define an Equity Share.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u1-1-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "An equity share represents part-ownership of a company. A holder of equity shares (\"equity shareholder\") contributes capital and becomes a part-owner with:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u1-1-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["A residual claim on profits (dividend, if declared)", "Voting rights in company decisions", "A residual claim on assets on winding up (after all other claims are settled)"]}, {"id": "edm-u1-1-a-3", "text": "Equity shares are also called ordinary shares; holders bear the highest risk but also have the highest potential return among a company''s capital providers. Exam Tip: Always mention \"ownership,\" \"residual claim,\" and \"voting rights.\" Common Trap: Don''t confuse equity shares with debentures/bonds — equity = ownership + risk; debt = loan + fixed return, no ownership.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-09 17:19:00.009132+00', '2026-09-19 18:36:49.771269+00'),
('bc550873-e171-4543-84c7-4a9185b6fedf', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'What is the Primary Market? Explain its functions and methods of raising capital.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Foundation for Oct 2024 Q2A and Q2P', 'Meaning: The primary market (New Issues Market) is where a company issues new securities directly to investors, and the money goes to the issuing company.
Functions: (1) Origination: advice on issue size, pricing and timing. (2) Underwriting: guarantee to buy unsubscribed shares. (3) Distribution: selling the securities to investors.
Methods of raising capital: Public issue (IPO / FPO), Rights issue, Private placement (preferential allotment, QIP) and Offer for Sale (OFS).
Participants: Issuer, merchant bankers (BRLM), underwriters, registrar, bankers to the issue, syndicate members, depositories, stock exchanges, SEBI and investors.
Regulation: SEBI (ICDR) Regulations 2018, Companies Act 2013 and SCRA 1956.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c1-q-1", "text": "What is the Primary Market? Explain its functions and methods of raising capital.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c1-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: The primary market (New Issues Market) is where a company issues new securities directly to investors, and the money goes to the issuing company.", "Functions: (1) Origination: advice on issue size, pricing and timing. (2) Underwriting: guarantee to buy unsubscribed shares. (3) Distribution: selling the securities to investors.", "Methods of raising capital: Public issue (IPO / FPO), Rights issue, Private placement (preferential allotment, QIP) and Offer for Sale (OFS).", "Participants: Issuer, merchant bankers (BRLM), underwriters, registrar, bankers to the issue, syndicate members, depositories, stock exchanges, SEBI and investors.", "Regulation: SEBI (ICDR) Regulations 2018, Companies Act 2013 and SCRA 1956."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('2d7b95ab-5679-4c89-bc4e-3968cd18e479', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between Primary Market and Secondary Market.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2P (10m)', 'Basis | Primary Market | Secondary Market
Meaning | Market for new securities | Market for already issued securities
Also called | New Issues Market (NIM) | Stock market / aftermarket
Parties | Company and investors | Investors and investors (through brokers)
Flow of funds | To the company | To the selling investor (not to the company)
Price | Fixed by issuer and merchant banker (fixed price / price band) | Determined by demand and supply
Frequency | A security is sold only once | Sold repeatedly
Intermediaries | Merchant bankers, underwriters, registrar, bankers to issue | Stockbrokers, sub-brokers, depositories, clearing corporations
Location | No fixed place | Organised exchange (NSE, BSE)
Objective | Capital formation | Liquidity, marketability and price discovery
Instruments | IPO, FPO, rights issue, private placement | Trading of listed shares, F&O
Regulation | SEBI ICDR Regulations 2018 and Companies Act | SCRA 1956 and SEBI regulations for exchanges
Risk | Pricing and subscription risk | Price volatility

Interdependence: The primary market creates securities and the secondary market gives them liquidity. Without a secondary market, investors would hesitate to subscribe in the primary market.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c2-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between Primary Market and Secondary Market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c2-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Primary Market", "Secondary Market"], ["Meaning", "Market for new securities", "Market for already issued securities"], ["Also called", "New Issues Market (NIM)", "Stock market / aftermarket"], ["Parties", "Company and investors", "Investors and investors (through brokers)"], ["Flow of funds", "To the company", "To the selling investor (not to the company)"], ["Price", "Fixed by issuer and merchant banker (fixed price / price band)", "Determined by demand and supply"], ["Frequency", "A security is sold only once", "Sold repeatedly"], ["Intermediaries", "Merchant bankers, underwriters, registrar, bankers to issue", "Stockbrokers, sub-brokers, depositories, clearing corporations"], ["Location", "No fixed place", "Organised exchange (NSE, BSE)"], ["Objective", "Capital formation", "Liquidity, marketability and price discovery"], ["Instruments", "IPO, FPO, rights issue, private placement", "Trading of listed shares, F&O"], ["Regulation", "SEBI ICDR Regulations 2018 and Companies Act", "SCRA 1956 and SEBI regulations for exchanges"], ["Risk", "Pricing and subscription risk", "Price volatility"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c2-a-2", "text": "Interdependence: The primary market creates securities and the secondary market gives them liquidity. Without a secondary market, investors would hesitate to subscribe in the primary market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('596bdfc1-1ae9-4e03-aac0-233c03f79535', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Define IPO. Explain its meaning, types and features.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2A (10m)', 'Definition: An Initial Public Offer (IPO) is the first offer of securities by an unlisted company to the public for subscription, with the intention of getting listed on a stock exchange. Under SEBI ICDR, it also includes an offer for sale of existing holders'' securities in an unlisted issuer.
Types (by nature of the offer):
Fresh issue: new shares are issued and the money goes to the company.
Offer for Sale (OFS): existing shareholders (promoters, PE/VC investors) sell their shares and the money goes to the sellers.
Most IPOs combine both.
FPO (Follow-on Public Offer): a further public offer by an already listed company.
Features: Made to the general public. Converts a private company into a public listed company. Needs SEBI vetting and an offer document (DRHP/RHP/prospectus). Uses a merchant banker. Price is set by fixed price or book building. Requires a minimum subscription of 90% (verify). Listing follows within T+3 days of issue closing.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c3-q-1", "text": "Define IPO. Explain its meaning, types and features.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c3-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Definition: An Initial Public Offer (IPO) is the first offer of securities by an unlisted company to the public for subscription, with the intention of getting listed on a stock exchange. Under SEBI ICDR, it also includes an offer for sale of existing holders'' securities in an unlisted issuer.", "Types (by nature of the offer):", "Fresh issue: new shares are issued and the money goes to the company.", "Offer for Sale (OFS): existing shareholders (promoters, PE/VC investors) sell their shares and the money goes to the sellers.", "Most IPOs combine both.", "FPO (Follow-on Public Offer): a further public offer by an already listed company.", "Features: Made to the general public. Converts a private company into a public listed company. Needs SEBI vetting and an offer document (DRHP/RHP/prospectus). Uses a merchant banker. Price is set by fixed price or book building. Requires a minimum subscription of 90% (verify). Listing follows within T+3 days of issue closing."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('a74f9206-a323-49fe-8064-c3b9a8ddfe3e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the process of an IPO.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2A ("process" part)', 'Decision and approvals: Board and shareholder approval (special resolution). Check eligibility under SEBI ICDR.
Appointment of intermediaries: Book Running Lead Manager (merchant banker), registrar, underwriters, bankers to the issue, syndicate members, legal counsel and auditors.
Due diligence and DRHP: The merchant banker prepares the Draft Red Herring Prospectus (DRHP) and files it with SEBI and the exchanges. It is put in the public domain for comments. SEBI issues observations, and the exchange gives in-principle listing approval.
RHP and price band: After approvals, the Red Herring Prospectus is filed with the Registrar of Companies, and the price band is announced.
Marketing: Roadshows and investor meetings. Anchor investors are allotted shares one working day before the issue opens.
Issue opens: Bidding stays open for 3 to 10 working days. Investors apply through ASBA/UPI, and the funds are only blocked in their bank account.
Closing and allotment: The price is finalised (cut-off price in book building). The registrar and the exchange finalise the basis of allotment. Shares are credited to demat accounts, and unsuccessful applicants'' funds are unblocked.
Listing: Shares are listed and trading begins on the exchanges (T+3 from issue closing).
Post-listing: Stabilisation (green shoe option, where applicable) and compliance with the lock-ins.

Exam tip: Present the steps as a numbered flow or a diagram (2025 instructions ask for neat diagrams wherever necessary).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c4-q-1", "text": "Explain the process of an IPO.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c4-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Decision and approvals: Board and shareholder approval (special resolution). Check eligibility under SEBI ICDR.", "Appointment of intermediaries: Book Running Lead Manager (merchant banker), registrar, underwriters, bankers to the issue, syndicate members, legal counsel and auditors.", "Due diligence and DRHP: The merchant banker prepares the Draft Red Herring Prospectus (DRHP) and files it with SEBI and the exchanges. It is put in the public domain for comments. SEBI issues observations, and the exchange gives in-principle listing approval.", "RHP and price band: After approvals, the Red Herring Prospectus is filed with the Registrar of Companies, and the price band is announced.", "Marketing: Roadshows and investor meetings. Anchor investors are allotted shares one working day before the issue opens.", "Issue opens: Bidding stays open for 3 to 10 working days. Investors apply through ASBA/UPI, and the funds are only blocked in their bank account.", "Closing and allotment: The price is finalised (cut-off price in book building). The registrar and the exchange finalise the basis of allotment. Shares are credited to demat accounts, and unsuccessful applicants'' funds are unblocked.", "Listing: Shares are listed and trading begins on the exchanges (T+3 from issue closing).", "Post-listing: Stabilisation (green shoe option, where applicable) and compliance with the lock-ins."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c4-a-2", "text": "Exam tip: Present the steps as a numbered flow or a diagram (2025 instructions ask for neat diagrams wherever necessary).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('8a9ebffe-e7b7-4348-97d7-5ce0758b4267', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the Merits and Demerits of an IPO.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2A', ' | To the Company | To Investors
Merits | Raises large capital, with no repayment obligation. Better visibility and brand image. Liquidity for promoters and early investors (exit). Higher valuation and credibility. Allows employee ESOPs to be valued and traded. Easier access to future funds (FPO, rights, QIP). Improves governance through disclosure | Opportunity to buy into growing companies. Regulated, transparent offer (SEBI vetting, disclosures). Possible listing gains. Liquidity after listing. Small applications are allowed (ASBA/UPI). Reservation for retail investors
Demerits | Heavy costs (merchant banker, legal, marketing, listing fees). Disclosure and compliance burden. Dilution of promoters'' control. Pressure on quarterly performance. Time-consuming process. Market-timing risk (issue may fail if markets fall). Loss of privacy | Possibility of overpricing and listing-day losses. Volatility after listing. Information asymmetry, since retail investors depend on the offer document. Allotment is not guaranteed in oversubscribed issues. Money is blocked during the bidding period', NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c5-q-1", "text": "Discuss the Merits and Demerits of an IPO.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c5-a-1", "rows": [["", "To the Company", "To Investors"], ["Merits", "Raises large capital, with no repayment obligation. Better visibility and brand image. Liquidity for promoters and early investors (exit). Higher valuation and credibility. Allows employee ESOPs to be valued and traded. Easier access to future funds (FPO, rights, QIP). Improves governance through disclosure", "Opportunity to buy into growing companies. Regulated, transparent offer (SEBI vetting, disclosures). Possible listing gains. Liquidity after listing. Small applications are allowed (ASBA/UPI). Reservation for retail investors"], ["Demerits", "Heavy costs (merchant banker, legal, marketing, listing fees). Disclosure and compliance burden. Dilution of promoters'' control. Pressure on quarterly performance. Time-consuming process. Market-timing risk (issue may fail if markets fall). Loss of privacy", "Possibility of overpricing and listing-day losses. Volatility after listing. Information asymmetry, since retail investors depend on the offer document. Allotment is not guaranteed in oversubscribed issues. Money is blocked during the bidding period"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('de5f5226-4073-4e53-991d-ea543c560049', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'What are the methods followed for an IPO?', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q2.2 (Fixed Price Issue is "a method of IPO")', 'Fixed Price Issue: The issuer and merchant banker decide the price before the issue opens, and it is disclosed in the offer document.
Book Building Issue: Only a price band (floor and cap) is disclosed, and the final price is discovered from investor bids.
Types of issue by nature (applicable to both methods): Fresh issue and Offer for Sale.
Other ways of issuing securities (contrast, not IPO methods): Rights issue, private placement/preferential allotment, QIP, bonus issue.
Trend: Large IPOs almost always use book building. Fixed price is mostly seen in smaller issues, including SME issues.

Exam tip: Any question on "methods" should be answered with both methods, and a mini-comparison table (Card 10) earns extra marks.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 6, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c6-q-1", "text": "What are the methods followed for an IPO?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c6-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Fixed Price Issue: The issuer and merchant banker decide the price before the issue opens, and it is disclosed in the offer document.", "Book Building Issue: Only a price band (floor and cap) is disclosed, and the final price is discovered from investor bids.", "Types of issue by nature (applicable to both methods): Fresh issue and Offer for Sale.", "Other ways of issuing securities (contrast, not IPO methods): Rights issue, private placement/preferential allotment, QIP, bonus issue.", "Trend: Large IPOs almost always use book building. Fixed price is mostly seen in smaller issues, including SME issues."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c6-a-2", "text": "Exam tip: Any question on \"methods\" should be answered with both methods, and a mini-comparison table (Card 10) earns extra marks.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('8ea6bb59-fb12-4961-a27c-1aed7cca6345', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Enlist the characteristics of a Fixed Price Issue as a method of IPO.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q2.2 (7.5m)', 'Price fixed in advance: the issuer and merchant banker fix the price before the issue opens.
Price disclosed in the offer document: the price is mentioned in the prospectus, so investors know the exact price and amount payable.
Fixed quantity: the number of shares offered is known.
Full payment: the entire issue price is normally payable at the time of application, and funds are blocked through ASBA/UPI.
No price discovery: demand does not influence the price. The number of shares subscribed is known only as the issue progresses and after it closes.
Reservation of categories: retail, NII and QIB categories are allocated as per SEBI ICDR.
Proportionate allotment: if oversubscribed, shares are allotted on a pro-rata basis, and excess money is refunded or unblocked.
Risk lies with the issuer: if the price is too high, the issue may be undersubscribed. If it is too low, the company loses funds.
Simplicity and low cost: fewer marketing and bidding formalities, which makes it suitable for small and SME issues.
Minimum subscription and listing rules apply: 90% minimum subscription (verify) and listing within T+3.
Basis of issue price: the company must give the justification for the price in the offer document.

Exam tip: 7.5 marks = a 2-line definition + 8 crisp points with one-line explanations.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c7-q-1", "text": "Enlist the characteristics of a Fixed Price Issue as a method of IPO.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c7-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Price fixed in advance: the issuer and merchant banker fix the price before the issue opens.", "Price disclosed in the offer document: the price is mentioned in the prospectus, so investors know the exact price and amount payable.", "Fixed quantity: the number of shares offered is known.", "Full payment: the entire issue price is normally payable at the time of application, and funds are blocked through ASBA/UPI.", "No price discovery: demand does not influence the price. The number of shares subscribed is known only as the issue progresses and after it closes.", "Reservation of categories: retail, NII and QIB categories are allocated as per SEBI ICDR.", "Proportionate allotment: if oversubscribed, shares are allotted on a pro-rata basis, and excess money is refunded or unblocked.", "Risk lies with the issuer: if the price is too high, the issue may be undersubscribed. If it is too low, the company loses funds.", "Simplicity and low cost: fewer marketing and bidding formalities, which makes it suitable for small and SME issues.", "Minimum subscription and listing rules apply: 90% minimum subscription (verify) and listing within T+3.", "Basis of issue price: the company must give the justification for the price in the offer document."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c7-a-2", "text": "Exam tip: 7.5 marks = a 2-line definition + 8 crisp points with one-line explanations.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('8334f3c9-0251-43b3-b218-1cadd122e52e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the process, merits and demerits of a Fixed Price Issue.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q2.2 (companion form)', 'Process: Merchant banker values the company and recommends a price. Issuer fixes the price. Prospectus is filed with RoC. Issue opens for 3–10 working days. Investors apply with full payment. The registrar finalises allotment (pro rata if oversubscribed). Refunds and demat credit follow, and the shares list.
Merits: Simple and easy to understand. Certainty of price for investors. Lower cost and less marketing effort. Suitable for small issues. No dependence on institutional bidding.
Demerits: No true price discovery, so mis-pricing is likely. Demand is not visible while the price is set. Higher risk of undersubscription. Investors'' money is blocked for the full price. Not favoured by institutional investors. Big issuers prefer book building.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c8-q-1", "text": "Explain the process, merits and demerits of a Fixed Price Issue.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c8-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Process: Merchant banker values the company and recommends a price. Issuer fixes the price. Prospectus is filed with RoC. Issue opens for 3–10 working days. Investors apply with full payment. The registrar finalises allotment (pro rata if oversubscribed). Refunds and demat credit follow, and the shares list.", "Merits: Simple and easy to understand. Certainty of price for investors. Lower cost and less marketing effort. Suitable for small issues. No dependence on institutional bidding.", "Demerits: No true price discovery, so mis-pricing is likely. Demand is not visible while the price is set. Higher risk of undersubscription. Investors'' money is blocked for the full price. Not favoured by institutional investors. Big issuers prefer book building."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('08dac1bc-041c-4dc8-9184-4236b5c386f0', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Book Building process.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus; the natural companion of 2025 Q2.2', 'Meaning: A price-discovery mechanism in which the issuer announces a price band, and investors bid for shares within that band. The final issue price is discovered from the demand at each price.
Steps:

Appointment of the merchant banker (BRLM). Preparation and filing of the DRHP.
SEBI observations. RHP filed with RoC with floor price and cap price (cap should not exceed 120% of the floor, verify).
Announcement of the price band (at least 2 working days before opening).
Anchor investors are allotted shares one working day before opening.
Bidding for 3–10 working days through the exchange''s electronic bidding system (bids can be revised or cancelled during the period). Demand is visible at each price.
Price discovery: the cut-off price is the highest price at which the entire issue is subscribed.
Allotment at the uniform cut-off price to all successful bidders. Excess money is unblocked.
Listing (T+3).

Why it is popular: Real-time demand information, price close to market value, and strong institutional participation.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 9, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c9-q-1", "text": "Explain the Book Building process.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c9-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: A price-discovery mechanism in which the issuer announces a price band, and investors bid for shares within that band. The final issue price is discovered from the demand at each price.", "Steps:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c9-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Appointment of the merchant banker (BRLM). Preparation and filing of the DRHP.", "SEBI observations. RHP filed with RoC with floor price and cap price (cap should not exceed 120% of the floor, verify).", "Announcement of the price band (at least 2 working days before opening).", "Anchor investors are allotted shares one working day before opening.", "Bidding for 3–10 working days through the exchange''s electronic bidding system (bids can be revised or cancelled during the period). Demand is visible at each price.", "Price discovery: the cut-off price is the highest price at which the entire issue is subscribed.", "Allotment at the uniform cut-off price to all successful bidders. Excess money is unblocked.", "Listing (T+3)."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c9-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Why it is popular: Real-time demand information, price close to market value, and strong institutional participation."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('1b8fdad1-6be4-4ee9-8d35-45727ea6f127', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Sensex vs Nifty, and a simple index numerical.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus (indices, numerical potential is low but possible)', 'Basis | Sensex | Nifty 50
Exchange | BSE | NSE
Stocks | 30 | 50
Base | 1978–79 = 100 | Nov 1995 = 1000
Launched | 1986 | 1996
Method | Free-float market cap | Free-float market cap

Formula: Index = (Current free-float market cap ÷ Base market cap) × Base index value. Free-float market cap = Shares outstanding × Price × Free-float factor.

Example 1: A company has 50 crore shares priced at ₹200. Promoters hold 60%.

Free-float factor = 40% = 0.40.
FF market cap = 50 crore × 200 × 0.40 = ₹4,000 crore (check: total ₹10,000 crore × 0.4 ✓).

Example 2: Base market cap = ₹3,000 crore, base value = 1,000, current FF market cap = ₹4,500 crore.

Index = (4,500 ÷ 3,000) × 1,000 = 1,500.
If it rises to 1,560: change = 60 points = 4% (60 ÷ 1,500).

Example 3 (price-weighted): Prices ₹100, ₹200, ₹300 (divisor 3): index = 600 ÷ 3 = 200. New prices ₹110, ₹190, ₹330: 630 ÷ 3 = 210 (+5%).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 51, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c51-q-1", "text": "Sensex vs Nifty, and a simple index numerical.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c51-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Sensex", "Nifty 50"], ["Exchange", "BSE", "NSE"], ["Stocks", "30", "50"], ["Base", "1978–79 = 100", "Nov 1995 = 1000"], ["Launched", "1986", "1996"], ["Method", "Free-float market cap", "Free-float market cap"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c51-a-2", "text": "Formula: Index = (Current free-float market cap ÷ Base market cap) × Base index value. Free-float market cap = Shares outstanding × Price × Free-float factor.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c51-a-3", "text": "Example 1: A company has 50 crore shares priced at ₹200. Promoters hold 60%.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c51-a-4", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Free-float factor = 40% = 0.40.", "FF market cap = 50 crore × 200 × 0.40 = ₹4,000 crore (check: total ₹10,000 crore × 0.4 ✓)."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c51-a-5", "text": "Example 2: Base market cap = ₹3,000 crore, base value = 1,000, current FF market cap = ₹4,500 crore.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c51-a-6", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Index = (4,500 ÷ 3,000) × 1,000 = 1,500.", "If it rises to 1,560: change = 60 points = 4% (60 ÷ 1,500)."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c51-a-7", "text": "Example 3 (price-weighted): Prices ₹100, ₹200, ₹300 (divisor 3): index = 600 ÷ 3 = 200. New prices ₹110, ₹190, ₹330: 630 ÷ 3 = 210 (+5%).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('7f8ba3e7-ce2f-42fe-aaef-4358d707646f', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between Fixed Price Issue and Book Building Issue.', 'Priority: Tier 1 to Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Natural variation of 2025 Q2.2', 'Basis | Fixed Price Issue | Book Building Issue
Price | Fixed before the issue and disclosed | Price band disclosed; final price discovered from bids
Offer document | Prospectus | RHP (then prospectus after closing)
Price discovery | No | Yes, through bids
Demand information | Known only after or as the issue progresses | Visible during bidding at each price
Payment | Full issue price at application | Bid at any price in the band, and the cut-off price is paid
Investor bidding | One price, no bids | Bids can be revised or cancelled during the issue
Anchor investors | Not normally used | Used
Risk | Mis-pricing risk on the issuer | Reduced mis-pricing risk
Cost and complexity | Lower | Higher (roadshows, syndicate, technology)
Suitability | Small, SME issues | Large issues and institutional participation', NULL, NULL, NULL, 10, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c10-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between Fixed Price Issue and Book Building Issue.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c10-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Fixed Price Issue", "Book Building Issue"], ["Price", "Fixed before the issue and disclosed", "Price band disclosed; final price discovered from bids"], ["Offer document", "Prospectus", "RHP (then prospectus after closing)"], ["Price discovery", "No", "Yes, through bids"], ["Demand information", "Known only after or as the issue progresses", "Visible during bidding at each price"], ["Payment", "Full issue price at application", "Bid at any price in the band, and the cut-off price is paid"], ["Investor bidding", "One price, no bids", "Bids can be revised or cancelled during the issue"], ["Anchor investors", "Not normally used", "Used"], ["Risk", "Mis-pricing risk on the issuer", "Reduced mis-pricing risk"], ["Cost and complexity", "Lower", "Higher (roadshows, syndicate, technology)"], ["Suitability", "Small, SME issues", "Large issues and institutional participation"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('50a9f1a6-ea5d-4cd2-86d5-c69cd8357eea', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Key IPO terms (glossary).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Supports Oct 2024 Q2A and 2025 Q2.2', 'Price band: range between the floor price (minimum) and cap price (maximum) within which investors bid.
Cut-off price: the final price at which shares are allotted (the highest price at which the entire issue is subscribed). Retail investors can bid "at cut-off".
Lot size: minimum number of shares that can be applied for. Application value is typically ₹14,000–15,000 (verify).
Anchor investor: a QIB who applies for a minimum of ₹10 crore and is allotted shares a day before opening. Up to 60% of the QIB portion may go to anchors. Lock-in: 50% for 30 days and 50% for 90 days (verify).
ASBA (Application Supported by Blocked Amount): the amount stays in the investor''s bank account and is blocked. UPI-based ASBA is available for retail investors.
Green Shoe Option (GSO): an over-allotment option (up to 15% of the issue) to stabilise the post-listing price.
Basis of allotment: how oversubscribed shares are distributed among applicants.
Oversubscription/subscription multiple: total demand ÷ shares offered.
Grey market premium (GMP): unofficial, unregulated indicator of expected listing price. It should not be relied on.
Promoter lock-in: minimum promoter contribution (20% of post-issue capital) is locked in for 18 months, and the excess for 6 months (verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 11, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c11-q-1", "text": "Key IPO terms (glossary).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c11-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Price band: range between the floor price (minimum) and cap price (maximum) within which investors bid.", "Cut-off price: the final price at which shares are allotted (the highest price at which the entire issue is subscribed). Retail investors can bid \"at cut-off\".", "Lot size: minimum number of shares that can be applied for. Application value is typically ₹14,000–15,000 (verify).", "Anchor investor: a QIB who applies for a minimum of ₹10 crore and is allotted shares a day before opening. Up to 60% of the QIB portion may go to anchors. Lock-in: 50% for 30 days and 50% for 90 days (verify).", "ASBA (Application Supported by Blocked Amount): the amount stays in the investor''s bank account and is blocked. UPI-based ASBA is available for retail investors.", "Green Shoe Option (GSO): an over-allotment option (up to 15% of the issue) to stabilise the post-listing price.", "Basis of allotment: how oversubscribed shares are distributed among applicants.", "Oversubscription/subscription multiple: total demand ÷ shares offered.", "Grey market premium (GMP): unofficial, unregulated indicator of expected listing price. It should not be relied on.", "Promoter lock-in: minimum promoter contribution (20% of post-issue capital) is locked in for 18 months, and the excess for 6 months (verify)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('4c704d39-f948-4c3a-b2d4-d2e62955d602', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain investor categories and reservation in a book-built IPO.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus (supports IPO numericals)', 'QIB (Qualified Institutional Buyers): mutual funds, insurers, banks, FPIs.
NII (Non-Institutional Investors, also HNIs): individuals and entities bidding for more than ₹2 lakh.
RII (Retail Individual Investors): individuals applying for up to ₹2 lakh.
Typical reservation (profit-track issuers, SEBI ICDR Reg 6(1)): QIB not more than 50%, NII not less than 15%, retail not less than 35% of the net offer.
Loss-making/new-age companies (Reg 6(2)): QIB not less than 75%, NII not more than 15%, retail not more than 10%.
Within QIB: up to 60% for anchor investors, and 5% of the remaining QIB portion for mutual funds.
Employees and existing shareholders may have small reserved portions.
(verify the latest ICDR percentages and limits)', NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c12-q-1", "text": "Explain investor categories and reservation in a book-built IPO.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c12-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["QIB (Qualified Institutional Buyers): mutual funds, insurers, banks, FPIs.", "NII (Non-Institutional Investors, also HNIs): individuals and entities bidding for more than ₹2 lakh.", "RII (Retail Individual Investors): individuals applying for up to ₹2 lakh.", "Typical reservation (profit-track issuers, SEBI ICDR Reg 6(1)): QIB not more than 50%, NII not less than 15%, retail not less than 35% of the net offer.", "Loss-making/new-age companies (Reg 6(2)): QIB not less than 75%, NII not more than 15%, retail not more than 10%.", "Within QIB: up to 60% for anchor investors, and 5% of the remaining QIB portion for mutual funds.", "Employees and existing shareholders may have small reserved portions.", "(verify the latest ICDR percentages and limits)"]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('84af348d-9273-48ae-9913-a0cb0fa165b7', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Define Merchant Banker. Explain its features.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2Q (9m)', 'Definition (SEBI Merchant Bankers Regulations, 1992): any person engaged in the business of issue management, either by making arrangements regarding selling, buying or subscribing to securities, or by acting as manager, consultant, adviser or rendering corporate advisory service in relation to such issue management.
Features:

SEBI registration is compulsory to act as a merchant banker.
Must be a body corporate with a minimum net worth (₹5 crore, verify).
Specialised, fee-based service provider.
Multi-functional: issue management, underwriting, advisory, portfolio management, loan syndication, etc.
Intermediary between the issuer and investors.
Acts on a professional and advisory basis, applying due diligence.
Regulated by SEBI, with a code of conduct and inspection powers.
Carries liability for disclosures in the offer document.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 13, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c13-q-1", "text": "Define Merchant Banker. Explain its features.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c13-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Definition (SEBI Merchant Bankers Regulations, 1992): any person engaged in the business of issue management, either by making arrangements regarding selling, buying or subscribing to securities, or by acting as manager, consultant, adviser or rendering corporate advisory service in relation to such issue management.", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c13-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["SEBI registration is compulsory to act as a merchant banker.", "Must be a body corporate with a minimum net worth (₹5 crore, verify).", "Specialised, fee-based service provider.", "Multi-functional: issue management, underwriting, advisory, portfolio management, loan syndication, etc.", "Intermediary between the issuer and investors.", "Acts on a professional and advisory basis, applying due diligence.", "Regulated by SEBI, with a code of conduct and inspection powers.", "Carries liability for disclosures in the offer document."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('c7c0ae02-b30d-4665-8bd5-a0ed88d0a1a6', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the types of Merchant Bankers.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2Q ("Types" part)', 'Answer both classifications, because the syllabus and textbooks differ.

(a) By function/nature of service

Issue managers / Lead managers (BRLMs) who manage public and rights issues.
Underwriters who guarantee subscription.
Portfolio managers who manage investments for clients.
Corporate advisors/consultants for mergers, restructuring and valuation.
Co-managers, bankers to the issue and registrars who support the issue.

(b) By ownership

Bank-sponsored: e.g. SBI Caps, ICICI Securities Primary Dealership arms.
Financial-institution owned: e.g. IDBI Capital.
Private/independent firms: e.g. Kotak, JM Financial, Axis Capital.
Foreign investment banks: e.g. Citi, Goldman Sachs, Morgan Stanley.

(c) SEBI categories: Historically four categories (I–IV) were defined by permitted activities. After the 2011 reform, only Category I merchant bankers exist (verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 14, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c14-q-1", "text": "Explain the types of Merchant Bankers.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c14-a-1", "text": "Answer both classifications, because the syllabus and textbooks differ.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c14-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "(a) By function/nature of service", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c14-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Issue managers / Lead managers (BRLMs) who manage public and rights issues.", "Underwriters who guarantee subscription.", "Portfolio managers who manage investments for clients.", "Corporate advisors/consultants for mergers, restructuring and valuation.", "Co-managers, bankers to the issue and registrars who support the issue."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c14-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "(b) By ownership", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c14-a-5", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Bank-sponsored: e.g. SBI Caps, ICICI Securities Primary Dealership arms.", "Financial-institution owned: e.g. IDBI Capital.", "Private/independent firms: e.g. Kotak, JM Financial, Axis Capital.", "Foreign investment banks: e.g. Citi, Goldman Sachs, Morgan Stanley."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c14-a-6", "text": "(c) SEBI categories: Historically four categories (I–IV) were defined by permitted activities. After the 2011 reform, only Category I merchant bankers exist (verify).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('462f1032-ffe3-41e3-b3b9-fa2d5eeb8c7b', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the role of the Merchant Banker in price fixation.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2Q; syllabus "Role of Merchant Bankers in fixing the price"', 'SEBI does not fix the price (free pricing since 1992). The issuer decides the price in consultation with the merchant banker, who plays the central role.

Valuation of the company: uses P/E multiple, Price/Book, EV/EBITDA, DCF and NAV, benchmarked against listed peers.
Financial and business due diligence: growth prospects, earnings quality, industry outlook and risks.
Market and investor feedback: roadshows, meetings with institutions and analysing market conditions.
Choosing the pricing method and structure: recommends fixed price (single price, often with an IPO discount to ensure subscription) or book building (price band and later the cut-off price).
Advises on issue size and mix of fresh issue and OFS.
Balances interests: the issuer wants maximum proceeds, while investors want a fair price. The banker aims for full subscription and a healthy post-listing performance.
Disclosure of the "Basis for Issue Price": qualitative and quantitative factors (EPS, P/E, RoNW, NAV, peer comparison) in the offer document.
Price band management: recommends the band and, if needed, revisions.
Coordination with underwriters and syndicate, and support for stabilisation after listing.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 15, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c15-q-1", "text": "Explain the role of the Merchant Banker in price fixation.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c15-a-1", "text": "SEBI does not fix the price (free pricing since 1992). The issuer decides the price in consultation with the merchant banker, who plays the central role.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c15-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Valuation of the company: uses P/E multiple, Price/Book, EV/EBITDA, DCF and NAV, benchmarked against listed peers.", "Financial and business due diligence: growth prospects, earnings quality, industry outlook and risks.", "Market and investor feedback: roadshows, meetings with institutions and analysing market conditions.", "Choosing the pricing method and structure: recommends fixed price (single price, often with an IPO discount to ensure subscription) or book building (price band and later the cut-off price).", "Advises on issue size and mix of fresh issue and OFS.", "Balances interests: the issuer wants maximum proceeds, while investors want a fair price. The banker aims for full subscription and a healthy post-listing performance.", "Disclosure of the \"Basis for Issue Price\": qualitative and quantitative factors (EPS, P/E, RoNW, NAV, peer comparison) in the offer document.", "Price band management: recommends the band and, if needed, revisions.", "Coordination with underwriters and syndicate, and support for stabilisation after listing."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('805407e2-4788-4b30-bd19-c59655341dfe', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the responsibilities of a Merchant Banker.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2Q ("Responsibilities")', 'Due diligence of the issuer, and submission of a due diligence certificate to SEBI.
Ensure adequate and accurate disclosures in the DRHP/RHP/prospectus.
File documents with SEBI, exchanges and RoC within the prescribed timelines.
Ensure compliance with SEBI ICDR, Companies Act 2013 and LODR.
Coordinate intermediaries: registrar, underwriters, bankers to issue and syndicate members.
Ensure timely allotment, refunds/unblocking and listing, and address investor complaints until the process is complete.
Give fair and justified pricing advice.
Maintain records (5 years, verify), comply with the Code of Conduct (Schedule III), avoid conflicts of interest and keep clients'' information confidential.
Face SEBI action (warning, suspension, cancellation of registration or penalties) for lapses.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c16-q-1", "text": "Explain the responsibilities of a Merchant Banker.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c16-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Due diligence of the issuer, and submission of a due diligence certificate to SEBI.", "Ensure adequate and accurate disclosures in the DRHP/RHP/prospectus.", "File documents with SEBI, exchanges and RoC within the prescribed timelines.", "Ensure compliance with SEBI ICDR, Companies Act 2013 and LODR.", "Coordinate intermediaries: registrar, underwriters, bankers to issue and syndicate members.", "Ensure timely allotment, refunds/unblocking and listing, and address investor complaints until the process is complete.", "Give fair and justified pricing advice.", "Maintain records (5 years, verify), comply with the Code of Conduct (Schedule III), avoid conflicts of interest and keep clients'' information confidential.", "Face SEBI action (warning, suspension, cancellation of registration or penalties) for lapses."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('92d8bfc9-4afa-45f8-bdfc-f610c9dd1cdd', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'What is a Red Herring Prospectus (RHP)? Explain its unique features.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Red Herring Prospectus — unique features"', 'Meaning (Companies Act 2013, Sec 32): A prospectus that does not include complete particulars of the quantum or price of the securities offered. It is used mainly in book building.
Why "red herring": The cover carries a red-coloured disclaimer that the document is not final and is subject to completion.
Unique features:

Gives a price band rather than the final price and amount.
Filed with the RoC at least 3 days before the issue opens.
Carries the same liability as a prospectus for misstatements.
Any variations between the RHP and the final prospectus must be highlighted.
After the issue closes, the final prospectus with the price, total capital raised and other details is filed with RoC (and SEBI/exchanges).
Enables book building, since the price is discovered after bidding.
Preceded by the DRHP (filed with SEBI).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 17, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c17-q-1", "text": "What is a Red Herring Prospectus (RHP)? Explain its unique features.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c17-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning (Companies Act 2013, Sec 32): A prospectus that does not include complete particulars of the quantum or price of the securities offered. It is used mainly in book building.", "Why \"red herring\": The cover carries a red-coloured disclaimer that the document is not final and is subject to completion.", "Unique features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c17-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Gives a price band rather than the final price and amount.", "Filed with the RoC at least 3 days before the issue opens.", "Carries the same liability as a prospectus for misstatements.", "Any variations between the RHP and the final prospectus must be highlighted.", "After the issue closes, the final prospectus with the price, total capital raised and other details is filed with RoC (and SEBI/exchanges).", "Enables book building, since the price is discovered after bidding.", "Preceded by the DRHP (filed with SEBI)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('6bad075a-c250-49da-8796-95ed1453d6a8', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between DRHP, RHP and Prospectus.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus; supports Card 17', 'Basis | DRHP | RHP | Prospectus
Filed with | SEBI and exchanges | RoC | RoC (also SEBI/exchanges)
Stage | Before the issue; for SEBI''s observations | Just before the issue opens | After the issue closes (final)
Price and quantum | Not final; indicative or blank | Price band, no final price | Final price and total capital
Purpose | Public comments and SEBI review | Bidding and price discovery | Legal, final disclosure', NULL, NULL, NULL, 18, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c18-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between DRHP, RHP and Prospectus.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c18-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "DRHP", "RHP", "Prospectus"], ["Filed with", "SEBI and exchanges", "RoC", "RoC (also SEBI/exchanges)"], ["Stage", "Before the issue; for SEBI''s observations", "Just before the issue opens", "After the issue closes (final)"], ["Price and quantum", "Not final; indicative or blank", "Price band, no final price", "Final price and total capital"], ["Purpose", "Public comments and SEBI review", "Bidding and price discovery", "Legal, final disclosure"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('bdc36e84-9e98-406e-aaaf-fb046b6c8ec6', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain Sweat Equity Shares (meaning, conditions and features).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Numerical on Sweat Equity" (not asked in these two papers)', 'Meaning (Companies Act 2013, Sec 54): Equity shares issued to employees or directors at a discount or for consideration other than cash, for providing know-how, rights in the nature of intellectual property, or value additions.
Conditions (Rule 8, Companies (Share Capital and Debentures) Rules 2014; verify):

Authorised by a special resolution.
Only for the class of equity shares already issued.
Valuation by a registered valuer (for IPR/know-how).
Limits: up to 15% of paid-up equity capital in a year or an issue value of ₹5 crore, whichever is higher, and a total of 25% at any time (higher limit of 50% for start-ups up to 10 years old).
Lock-in of 3 years from the date of allotment.

Accounting: The fair value of the sweat equity is charged as an employee compensation expense (or recognised as an intangible asset if it qualifies as know-how/IPR).
Purpose: Rewards and retains talent without cash outflow.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 19, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c19-q-1", "text": "Explain Sweat Equity Shares (meaning, conditions and features).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c19-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning (Companies Act 2013, Sec 54): Equity shares issued to employees or directors at a discount or for consideration other than cash, for providing know-how, rights in the nature of intellectual property, or value additions.", "Conditions (Rule 8, Companies (Share Capital and Debentures) Rules 2014; verify):"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c19-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Authorised by a special resolution.", "Only for the class of equity shares already issued.", "Valuation by a registered valuer (for IPR/know-how).", "Limits: up to 15% of paid-up equity capital in a year or an issue value of ₹5 crore, whichever is higher, and a total of 25% at any time (higher limit of 50% for start-ups up to 10 years old).", "Lock-in of 3 years from the date of allotment."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c19-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Accounting: The fair value of the sweat equity is charged as an employee compensation expense (or recognised as an intangible asset if it qualifies as know-how/IPR).", "Purpose: Rewards and retains talent without cash outflow."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('fb4dfeac-d46a-4792-b5bb-9a1867bfc10d', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain ESOP (meaning, features and terminology).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2B (ESOP numerical)', 'Meaning: An Employee Stock Option Plan gives employees the option (not the obligation) to buy the company''s shares at a pre-fixed exercise price after a vesting period.
Legal basis: Companies Act 2013 Sec 62(1)(b) with a special resolution. For listed companies, SEBI (Share Based Employee Benefits and Sweat Equity) Regulations 2021.
Terminology:
Grant date: date the option is granted.
Vesting period: period between grant and when the employee becomes entitled to exercise (minimum 1 year under SEBI rules).
Exercise price: price paid by the employee.
Exercise period: window in which vested options can be exercised.
Lapse: options that are not exercised in the exercise period expire.
Not allowed for: promoters/promoter group and independent directors (start-up exceptions apply).
Accounting methods: Intrinsic value method (market price on the grant date − exercise price) or fair value method (option pricing models such as Black-Scholes). Ind AS 102 requires the fair value method.
Purpose: Retention, motivation and aligning employee interests with shareholders.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 20, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c20-q-1", "text": "Explain ESOP (meaning, features and terminology).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c20-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: An Employee Stock Option Plan gives employees the option (not the obligation) to buy the company''s shares at a pre-fixed exercise price after a vesting period.", "Legal basis: Companies Act 2013 Sec 62(1)(b) with a special resolution. For listed companies, SEBI (Share Based Employee Benefits and Sweat Equity) Regulations 2021.", "Terminology:", "Grant date: date the option is granted.", "Vesting period: period between grant and when the employee becomes entitled to exercise (minimum 1 year under SEBI rules).", "Exercise price: price paid by the employee.", "Exercise period: window in which vested options can be exercised.", "Lapse: options that are not exercised in the exercise period expire.", "Not allowed for: promoters/promoter group and independent directors (start-up exceptions apply).", "Accounting methods: Intrinsic value method (market price on the grant date − exercise price) or fair value method (option pricing models such as Black-Scholes). Ind AS 102 requires the fair value method.", "Purpose: Retention, motivation and aligning employee interests with shareholders."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('988a745c-3e75-44d4-a57a-6106d0c1e86a', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain Rights Issue (meaning, features and procedure).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q2.3 (numerical); theory questions are possible', 'Meaning: An offer of new shares to existing shareholders in proportion to their holdings, usually at a price below the market price (Companies Act 2013, Sec 62(1)(a)).
Features:

Offered only to existing shareholders (pre-emptive right).
Offered in a ratio (e.g. 1 share for every 4 held).
Priced at a discount to market price.
The right can be renounced (sold) in favour of others (rights entitlements trade on the exchange).
Unsubscribed shares may be placed by the Board with others, if the offer letter allows.
No prospectus. A Letter of Offer is used. Investors apply through ASBA.

Procedure: Board approval → record date and ratio → Letter of Offer → offer period (about 7–30 days, verify) → subscription/renunciation → allotment of shares → listing.
Merits: Cheaper than a public issue. Control is retained. Existing shareholders are protected from dilution.
Demerits: Limited to the existing shareholder base, so it may not raise very large amounts. Shareholders who cannot pay lose their proportionate ownership. It signals a need for funds.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 21, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c21-q-1", "text": "Explain Rights Issue (meaning, features and procedure).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c21-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: An offer of new shares to existing shareholders in proportion to their holdings, usually at a price below the market price (Companies Act 2013, Sec 62(1)(a)).", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c21-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Offered only to existing shareholders (pre-emptive right).", "Offered in a ratio (e.g. 1 share for every 4 held).", "Priced at a discount to market price.", "The right can be renounced (sold) in favour of others (rights entitlements trade on the exchange).", "Unsubscribed shares may be placed by the Board with others, if the offer letter allows.", "No prospectus. A Letter of Offer is used. Investors apply through ASBA."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c21-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Procedure: Board approval → record date and ratio → Letter of Offer → offer period (about 7–30 days, verify) → subscription/renunciation → allotment of shares → listing.", "Merits: Cheaper than a public issue. Control is retained. Existing shareholders are protected from dilution.", "Demerits: Limited to the existing shareholder base, so it may not raise very large amounts. Shareholders who cannot pay lose their proportionate ownership. It signals a need for funds."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('f18e18e1-6cc4-4ba3-8c51-cdedb02febc3', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Two quick comparisons: ESOP vs Sweat Equity, and Rights Issue vs Public Issue.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus; a possible short-note or comparison question', 'Basis | ESOP | Sweat Equity
Nature | An option to buy shares in future | Shares are issued directly
Price | Exercise price (often below market) | At a discount or for non-cash consideration (know-how)
Beneficiaries | Employees (not promoters/independent directors) | Employees and directors
Vesting/lock-in | Minimum 1-year vesting | 3-year lock-in after allotment
Accounting | Expense over the vesting period with ESOP Outstanding A/c | Fair value charged as expense/intangible on issue

Basis | Rights Issue | Public Issue (IPO/FPO)
Offered to | Existing shareholders | General public
Document | Letter of Offer | Prospectus/RHP
Price | At a discount to market | Fixed price or book building
Cost | Lower | Higher
Control | Preserved | Diluted', NULL, NULL, NULL, 22, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c22-q-1", "text": "Two quick comparisons: ESOP vs Sweat Equity, and Rights Issue vs Public Issue.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c22-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "ESOP", "Sweat Equity"], ["Nature", "An option to buy shares in future", "Shares are issued directly"], ["Price", "Exercise price (often below market)", "At a discount or for non-cash consideration (know-how)"], ["Beneficiaries", "Employees (not promoters/independent directors)", "Employees and directors"], ["Vesting/lock-in", "Minimum 1-year vesting", "3-year lock-in after allotment"], ["Accounting", "Expense over the vesting period with ESOP Outstanding A/c", "Fair value charged as expense/intangible on issue"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c22-a-2", "rows": [["Basis", "Rights Issue", "Public Issue (IPO/FPO)"], ["Offered to", "Existing shareholders", "General public"], ["Document", "Letter of Offer", "Prospectus/RHP"], ["Price", "At a discount to market", "Fixed price or book building"], ["Cost", "Lower", "Higher"], ["Control", "Preserved", "Diluted"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('8723557b-cb4e-4cf9-a24a-5575f2f3e889', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'SME IPOs (overview).', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "SME IPOs and Start-up Listings (Overview)"', 'Meaning: Small and Medium Enterprises can raise funds through IPOs on dedicated exchange platforms: BSE SME and NSE Emerge (both launched in 2012). Governed by Chapter IX of SEBI ICDR 2018.
Features:

Post-issue paid-up capital up to ₹25 crore (verify).
Simpler compliance and lower costs than the main board.
Market makers are mandatory to provide liquidity (two-way quotes) for a minimum period (3 years, verify).
Higher minimum application size (lakhs of rupees) and a lot size limit, so the retail investor base is smaller.
Promoters'' minimum contribution has a longer lock-in.
Can migrate to the main board after meeting the criteria.

Benefits: Access to capital, visibility and easier compliance.
Concerns: Low liquidity, higher volatility and manipulation risk. SEBI has tightened the norms recently (verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 23, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c23-q-1", "text": "SME IPOs (overview).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c23-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Small and Medium Enterprises can raise funds through IPOs on dedicated exchange platforms: BSE SME and NSE Emerge (both launched in 2012). Governed by Chapter IX of SEBI ICDR 2018.", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c23-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Post-issue paid-up capital up to ₹25 crore (verify).", "Simpler compliance and lower costs than the main board.", "Market makers are mandatory to provide liquidity (two-way quotes) for a minimum period (3 years, verify).", "Higher minimum application size (lakhs of rupees) and a lot size limit, so the retail investor base is smaller.", "Promoters'' minimum contribution has a longer lock-in.", "Can migrate to the main board after meeting the criteria."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c23-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Benefits: Access to capital, visibility and easier compliance.", "Concerns: Low liquidity, higher volatility and manipulation risk. SEBI has tightened the norms recently (verify)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('814c8f5c-6737-40b4-895f-b654ec50358d', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Start-up listings (overview).', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Why special: Start-ups are often loss-making, with intangible assets, so they don''t meet the profit-track criteria for the main board.
Routes:

Main-board IPO under Reg 6(2) of SEBI ICDR: allowed for issuers without profit track record, with 75% reserved for QIBs (many new-age tech IPOs used this).
Innovators Growth Platform (IGP): SEBI''s platform for tech/IP-intensive start-ups with pre-existing institutional investors (verify eligibility).
SME platforms (BSE SME/NSE Emerge) for small start-ups.

Supportive measures: Superior Voting Rights (SR) shares for tech companies, and a relaxed rule allowing founders to hold ESOPs post-IPO (verify).
Benefits: Funds for growth, exit for early investors, and visibility.
Concerns: Valuation risk, earnings volatility and governance risk.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 24, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '8aa04e75-c21e-4194-8361-93823fdc65c7', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c24-q-1", "text": "Start-up listings (overview).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c24-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Why special: Start-ups are often loss-making, with intangible assets, so they don''t meet the profit-track criteria for the main board.", "Routes:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c24-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Main-board IPO under Reg 6(2) of SEBI ICDR: allowed for issuers without profit track record, with 75% reserved for QIBs (many new-age tech IPOs used this).", "Innovators Growth Platform (IGP): SEBI''s platform for tech/IP-intensive start-ups with pre-existing institutional investors (verify eligibility).", "SME platforms (BSE SME/NSE Emerge) for small start-ups."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c24-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Supportive measures: Superior Voting Rights (SR) shares for tech companies, and a relaxed rule allowing founders to hold ESOPs post-IPO (verify).", "Benefits: Funds for growth, exit for early investors, and visibility.", "Concerns: Valuation risk, earnings volatility and governance risk."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('0d844393-db26-407b-a53a-95c68ab1faa0', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Online Trading Platforms.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Online Trading Platforms"', 'Meaning: Internet-based platforms provided by brokers that let investors trade in shares, derivatives and mutual funds directly. SEBI permitted internet-based trading in 2000.
Examples: Zerodha (Kite), Groww, Upstox, Angel One, ICICI Direct, HDFC Securities.
Features: Real-time quotes and charts. Order types (market, limit, stop-loss, after-market, GTT). IPO and mutual fund applications. Margin facilities. Research reports and alerts.
Advantages: Convenience and speed, low brokerage, transparency and self-directed investing.
Disadvantages / risks: Cyber-fraud, technical outages, impulsive trading, and mis-selling or unverified advice.
Safeguards: SEBI-registered brokers, two-factor authentication, cyber-security framework, segregation of client funds and "SEBI Check" to verify intermediaries.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 53, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c53-q-1", "text": "Online Trading Platforms.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c53-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Internet-based platforms provided by brokers that let investors trade in shares, derivatives and mutual funds directly. SEBI permitted internet-based trading in 2000.", "Examples: Zerodha (Kite), Groww, Upstox, Angel One, ICICI Direct, HDFC Securities.", "Features: Real-time quotes and charts. Order types (market, limit, stop-loss, after-market, GTT). IPO and mutual fund applications. Margin facilities. Research reports and alerts.", "Advantages: Convenience and speed, low brokerage, transparency and self-directed investing.", "Disadvantages / risks: Cyber-fraud, technical outages, impulsive trading, and mis-selling or unverified advice.", "Safeguards: SEBI-registered brokers, two-factor authentication, cyber-security framework, segregation of client funds and \"SEBI Check\" to verify intermediaries."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('7df1bd30-4c9c-4274-a7a2-c4fd59684e6a', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Master method for share-issue journal entries (applies to Rights, ESOP and Sweat Equity).', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2B and 2025 Q2.3 (both are entry-type numericals)', 'Key formula (all share issues):

Total amount = Number of shares × Issue price
Equity Share Capital = Number of shares × Face Value (FV)
Securities Premium = Number of shares × (Issue price − FV)
Total Debit = Total Credit. This should always hold.

Variables: Face value = nominal value per share (₹10/₹20). Issue price = FV + premium. Premium = the excess over FV.

Steps:

Identify the type (rights / ESOP / sweat equity).
Note how many shares are actually issued (subscribed/exercised/allotted). Ignore lapsed or unsubscribed.
Calculate cash received, capital and premium.
Write the entry with correct heads, using narration.
Verify: total Dr = total Cr, and share capital = shares × FV.

Typical university format: "Illustrate/Pass journal entries" with the number of shares, FV, issue price and possibly partial subscription or a vesting/lapse fact.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 25, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c25-q-1", "text": "Master method for share-issue journal entries (applies to Rights, ESOP and Sweat Equity).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c25-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Key formula (all share issues):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c25-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Total amount = Number of shares × Issue price", "Equity Share Capital = Number of shares × Face Value (FV)", "Securities Premium = Number of shares × (Issue price − FV)", "Total Debit = Total Credit. This should always hold."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c25-a-3", "text": "Variables: Face value = nominal value per share (₹10/₹20). Issue price = FV + premium. Premium = the excess over FV.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c25-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "Steps:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c25-a-5", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Identify the type (rights / ESOP / sweat equity).", "Note how many shares are actually issued (subscribed/exercised/allotted). Ignore lapsed or unsubscribed.", "Calculate cash received, capital and premium.", "Write the entry with correct heads, using narration.", "Verify: total Dr = total Cr, and share capital = shares × FV."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c25-a-6", "text": "Typical university format: \"Illustrate/Pass journal entries\" with the number of shares, FV, issue price and possibly partial subscription or a vesting/lapse fact.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('c9e36143-28c5-480f-a80b-b6a15610ab81', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Rights Issue Case 1 (PYQ solved).', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q2.3 Case 1', 'Question: Gemini Ltd undertakes a rights issue of 50,000 shares at ₹60 (premium). FV = ₹20, premium = ₹40. Illustrate the entries.

Given Data: Shares = 50,000. Issue price = ₹60. FV = ₹20. Premium = ₹40.

Formula/Concept: Cash = shares × issue price. Capital = shares × FV. Premium = shares × premium.

Step-by-step:

Amount received = 50,000 × 60 = ₹30,00,000
Share capital = 50,000 × 20 = ₹10,00,000
Securities premium = 50,000 × 40 = ₹20,00,000

Journal entries:

Particulars |  | Dr (₹) | Cr (₹)
Bank A/c | Dr | 30,00,000 | 
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Rights Issue Application A/c |  |  | 30,00,000
(Being application money received on 50,000 shares at ₹60) |  |  | 
Rights Issue Application A/c | Dr | 30,00,000 | 
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c |  |  | 10,00,000
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c |  |  | 20,00,000
(Being shares allotted at ₹60, FV ₹20, premium ₹40) |  |  | 

Final answer: Equity Share Capital ₹10,00,000; Securities Premium ₹20,00,000; Bank ₹30,00,000. Check: 10,00,000 + 20,00,000 = 30,00,000 ✓. Exam tip: A compact one-line version (Bank Dr to Share Capital and Securities Premium) is also accepted, so follow your teacher''s style. Marks are for the correct figures and heads.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 26, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c26-q-1", "text": "Rights Issue Case 1 (PYQ solved).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c26-a-1", "text": "Question: Gemini Ltd undertakes a rights issue of 50,000 shares at ₹60 (premium). FV = ₹20, premium = ₹40. Illustrate the entries.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c26-a-2", "text": "Given Data: Shares = 50,000. Issue price = ₹60. FV = ₹20. Premium = ₹40.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c26-a-3", "text": "Formula/Concept: Cash = shares × issue price. Capital = shares × FV. Premium = shares × premium.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c26-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "Step-by-step:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c26-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Amount received = 50,000 × 60 = ₹30,00,000", "Share capital = 50,000 × 20 = ₹10,00,000", "Securities premium = 50,000 × 40 = ₹20,00,000"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c26-a-6", "bold": true, "text": "Journal entries:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c26-a-7", "rows": [["Particulars", "", "Dr (₹)", "Cr (₹)"], ["Bank A/c", "Dr", "30,00,000", ""], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Rights Issue Application A/c", "", "", "30,00,000"], ["(Being application money received on 50,000 shares at ₹60)", "", "", ""], ["Rights Issue Application A/c", "Dr", "30,00,000", ""], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c", "", "", "10,00,000"], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c", "", "", "20,00,000"], ["(Being shares allotted at ₹60, FV ₹20, premium ₹40)", "", "", ""]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c26-a-8", "text": "Final answer: Equity Share Capital ₹10,00,000; Securities Premium ₹20,00,000; Bank ₹30,00,000. Check: 10,00,000 + 20,00,000 = 30,00,000 ✓. Exam tip: A compact one-line version (Bank Dr to Share Capital and Securities Premium) is also accepted, so follow your teacher''s style. Marks are for the correct figures and heads.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('cd298e0a-2672-47df-90cb-3994777c7afa', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Rights Issue Case 2: part subscription (PYQ solved).', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q2.3 Case 2', 'Question: Gemini Ltd offered 80,000 shares, and only 72,000 were subscribed. FV = ₹10, issue price = ₹20. Show the entries.

Given Data: Offered = 80,000. Subscribed = 72,000. Issue price = ₹20. FV = ₹10. Premium = ₹10.

Formula/Concept: Use only the subscribed shares (72,000) for all entries.

Step-by-step:

Amount received = 72,000 × 20 = ₹14,40,000
Share capital = 72,000 × 10 = ₹7,20,000
Securities premium = 72,000 × 10 = ₹7,20,000
Unsubscribed = 8,000 shares (10%). No entry is passed for them.

Journal entries:

Particulars |  | Dr (₹) | Cr (₹)
Bank A/c | Dr | 14,40,000 | 
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Rights Issue Application A/c |  |  | 14,40,000
(Being application money received on 72,000 shares at ₹20) |  |  | 
Rights Issue Application A/c | Dr | 14,40,000 | 
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c |  |  | 7,20,000
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c |  |  | 7,20,000
(Being 72,000 shares allotted; 8,000 shares remain unsubscribed) |  |  | 

Final answer: Capital ₹7,20,000; Premium ₹7,20,000; Bank ₹14,40,000. Check: 7,20,000 + 7,20,000 = 14,40,000 ✓. The subscription is 72,000/80,000 = 90%, which meets the usual 90% minimum (verify). Exam tip: State that the 8,000 unsubscribed shares lapse. If the offer letter authorises the Board, they may be placed with others (Sec 62(1)(a)), and that would need a further entry.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 27, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c27-q-1", "text": "Rights Issue Case 2: part subscription (PYQ solved).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c27-a-1", "text": "Question: Gemini Ltd offered 80,000 shares, and only 72,000 were subscribed. FV = ₹10, issue price = ₹20. Show the entries.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c27-a-2", "text": "Given Data: Offered = 80,000. Subscribed = 72,000. Issue price = ₹20. FV = ₹10. Premium = ₹10.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c27-a-3", "text": "Formula/Concept: Use only the subscribed shares (72,000) for all entries.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c27-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "Step-by-step:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c27-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Amount received = 72,000 × 20 = ₹14,40,000", "Share capital = 72,000 × 10 = ₹7,20,000", "Securities premium = 72,000 × 10 = ₹7,20,000", "Unsubscribed = 8,000 shares (10%). No entry is passed for them."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c27-a-6", "bold": true, "text": "Journal entries:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c27-a-7", "rows": [["Particulars", "", "Dr (₹)", "Cr (₹)"], ["Bank A/c", "Dr", "14,40,000", ""], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Rights Issue Application A/c", "", "", "14,40,000"], ["(Being application money received on 72,000 shares at ₹20)", "", "", ""], ["Rights Issue Application A/c", "Dr", "14,40,000", ""], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c", "", "", "7,20,000"], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c", "", "", "7,20,000"], ["(Being 72,000 shares allotted; 8,000 shares remain unsubscribed)", "", "", ""]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c27-a-8", "text": "Final answer: Capital ₹7,20,000; Premium ₹7,20,000; Bank ₹14,40,000. Check: 7,20,000 + 7,20,000 = 14,40,000 ✓. The subscription is 72,000/80,000 = 90%, which meets the usual 90% minimum (verify). Exam tip: State that the 8,000 unsubscribed shares lapse. If the offer letter authorises the Board, they may be placed with others (Sec 62(1)(a)), and that would need a further entry.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('7a07f65d-bae7-43f7-83d4-a700752b248e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Rights Issue: alternative formulations (ratio and instalments).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Variations of 2025 Q2.3', 'Variation A: ratio given instead of number of shares.

Company has 4,00,000 equity shares of ₹10 each. Rights issue 1 for 5 at ₹25.

Rights shares = 4,00,000 × 1/5 = 80,000.
Proceeds = 80,000 × 25 = ₹20,00,000. Capital = 80,000 × 10 = ₹8,00,000. Premium = 80,000 × 15 = ₹12,00,000. (Check: 8,00,000 + 12,00,000 = 20,00,000 ✓.)

Variation B: payable in instalments.

10,000 shares of ₹10 at ₹15. Payable: application ₹5, allotment ₹7 (including premium ₹5), call ₹3. (Check: 5 + 7 + 3 = 15 ✓.)

Stage | Entry | Amount (₹)
Application | Bank Dr, To Rights Application A/c. Then Rights Application A/c Dr, To Equity Share Capital | 50,000
Allotment | Rights Allotment A/c Dr 70,000, To Equity Share Capital 20,000, To Securities Premium 50,000. Then Bank Dr, To Allotment A/c | 70,000
Call | Call A/c Dr, To Equity Share Capital. Then Bank Dr, To Call A/c | 30,000

Check: Capital = 50,000 + 20,000 + 30,000 = 1,00,000 = 10,000 × 10 ✓. Premium = 50,000 = 10,000 × 5 ✓. Total cash = 1,50,000 = 10,000 × 15 ✓.

Variation C: part subscription with instalments: apply the same table to the subscribed shares only.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 28, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c28-q-1", "text": "Rights Issue: alternative formulations (ratio and instalments).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c28-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Variation A: ratio given instead of number of shares.", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c28-a-2", "text": "Company has 4,00,000 equity shares of ₹10 each. Rights issue 1 for 5 at ₹25.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c28-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Rights shares = 4,00,000 × 1/5 = 80,000.", "Proceeds = 80,000 × 25 = ₹20,00,000. Capital = 80,000 × 10 = ₹8,00,000. Premium = 80,000 × 15 = ₹12,00,000. (Check: 8,00,000 + 12,00,000 = 20,00,000 ✓.)"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c28-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "Variation B: payable in instalments.", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c28-a-5", "text": "10,000 shares of ₹10 at ₹15. Payable: application ₹5, allotment ₹7 (including premium ₹5), call ₹3. (Check: 5 + 7 + 3 = 15 ✓.)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c28-a-6", "rows": [["Stage", "Entry", "Amount (₹)"], ["Application", "Bank Dr, To Rights Application A/c. Then Rights Application A/c Dr, To Equity Share Capital", "50,000"], ["Allotment", "Rights Allotment A/c Dr 70,000, To Equity Share Capital 20,000, To Securities Premium 50,000. Then Bank Dr, To Allotment A/c", "70,000"], ["Call", "Call A/c Dr, To Equity Share Capital. Then Bank Dr, To Call A/c", "30,000"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c28-a-7", "text": "Check: Capital = 50,000 + 20,000 + 30,000 = 1,00,000 = 10,000 × 10 ✓. Premium = 50,000 = 10,000 × 5 ✓. Total cash = 1,50,000 = 10,000 × 15 ✓.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c28-a-8", "text": "Variation C: part subscription with instalments: apply the same table to the subscribed shares only.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('5a5d57c3-cac2-4c10-80b9-fbd52e509a6b', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Rights Issue: Theoretical Ex-Rights Price (TERP) and Value of a Right.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: A possible variation (not yet asked)', 'Formulas (for n new shares for every N held; Pm = market price; Ps = subscription price):

TERP = (N × Pm + n × Ps) ÷ (N + n)
Value of a right (per new share) = TERP − Ps
Value of a right (per existing share held) = (Pm − TERP)

Question: A company offers 1 share for every 4 held at ₹120. Market price is ₹160. Find the TERP and the value of a right.

Solution:

N = 4, n = 1, Pm = 160, Ps = 120.
TERP = (4 × 160 + 1 × 120) ÷ 5 = (640 + 120) ÷ 5 = 760 ÷ 5 = ₹152.
Value of a right per new share = 152 − 120 = ₹32.
Value per existing share held = 160 − 152 = ₹8 (= 32 ÷ 4 ✓).

Verification (investor holds 400 shares):

Before: 400 × 160 = ₹64,000.
Entitled to 100 new shares, cost 100 × 120 = ₹12,000. After: 500 × 152 = ₹76,000. Net wealth = 76,000 − 12,000 = ₹64,000 (unchanged ✓).
If the investor sells the rights instead: 100 × 32 = ₹3,200, and the shares fall to 400 × 152 = ₹60,800. Total = ₹64,000 ✓.

Exam tip: A rights issue does not create or destroy wealth for a shareholder who acts on it. Use this as your check.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 29, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c29-q-1", "text": "Rights Issue: Theoretical Ex-Rights Price (TERP) and Value of a Right.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c29-a-1", "text": "Formulas (for n new shares for every N held; Pm = market price; Ps = subscription price):", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c29-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["TERP = (N × Pm + n × Ps) ÷ (N + n)", "Value of a right (per new share) = TERP − Ps", "Value of a right (per existing share held) = (Pm − TERP)"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c29-a-3", "text": "Question: A company offers 1 share for every 4 held at ₹120. Market price is ₹160. Find the TERP and the value of a right.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c29-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "Solution:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c29-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["N = 4, n = 1, Pm = 160, Ps = 120.", "TERP = (4 × 160 + 1 × 120) ÷ 5 = (640 + 120) ÷ 5 = 760 ÷ 5 = ₹152.", "Value of a right per new share = 152 − 120 = ₹32.", "Value per existing share held = 160 − 152 = ₹8 (= 32 ÷ 4 ✓)."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c29-a-6", "bold": true, "text": "Verification (investor holds 400 shares):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c29-a-7", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Before: 400 × 160 = ₹64,000.", "Entitled to 100 new shares, cost 100 × 120 = ₹12,000. After: 500 × 152 = ₹76,000. Net wealth = 76,000 − 12,000 = ₹64,000 (unchanged ✓).", "If the investor sells the rights instead: 100 × 32 = ₹3,200, and the shares fall to 400 × 152 = ₹60,800. Total = ₹64,000 ✓."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c29-a-8", "text": "Exam tip: A rights issue does not create or destroy wealth for a shareholder who acts on it. Use this as your check.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('2fea1f3f-a111-4183-a2e0-280322eaca94', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'ESOP accounting: rules and procedure.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2B', 'Formula (intrinsic value method):

Intrinsic value per option = Market price on grant date − Exercise price
Total compensation = Options granted × Intrinsic value
Spread the expense evenly over the vesting period.
(If the question gives the fair value of the option, use that instead. Ind AS 102 companies use fair value.)

Entries:

On grant/vesting (each year): Employee Compensation Expense A/c Dr, To ESOP Outstanding A/c.
On exercise: Bank A/c Dr (options exercised × exercise price) + ESOP Outstanding A/c Dr (options exercised × intrinsic value), To Equity Share Capital (options exercised × FV), To Securities Premium (balancing figure).
On lapse: for options that lapse before vesting, reverse the expense. For vested options that lapse unexercised, transfer ESOP Outstanding to General Reserve (ICAI Guidance Note). Some textbooks reverse through the expense account, so follow your class method.
At year end: transfer Employee Compensation Expense to the Profit & Loss A/c.

Securities premium per share = Exercise price + Intrinsic value − FV.

Variables: Exercise price = price the employee pays. ESOP Outstanding = equity-like reserve that holds the expense until exercise. FV = nominal value of a share.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 30, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c30-q-1", "text": "ESOP accounting: rules and procedure.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c30-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Formula (intrinsic value method):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c30-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Intrinsic value per option = Market price on grant date − Exercise price", "Total compensation = Options granted × Intrinsic value", "Spread the expense evenly over the vesting period.", "(If the question gives the fair value of the option, use that instead. Ind AS 102 companies use fair value.)"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c30-a-3", "bold": true, "text": "Entries:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c30-a-4", "type": "numberList", "items": ["On grant/vesting (each year): Employee Compensation Expense A/c Dr, To ESOP Outstanding A/c.", "On exercise: Bank A/c Dr (options exercised × exercise price) + ESOP Outstanding A/c Dr (options exercised × intrinsic value), To Equity Share Capital (options exercised × FV), To Securities Premium (balancing figure).", "On lapse: for options that lapse before vesting, reverse the expense. For vested options that lapse unexercised, transfer ESOP Outstanding to General Reserve (ICAI Guidance Note). Some textbooks reverse through the expense account, so follow your class method.", "At year end: transfer Employee Compensation Expense to the Profit & Loss A/c."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c30-a-5", "text": "Securities premium per share = Exercise price + Intrinsic value − FV.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c30-a-6", "text": "Variables: Exercise price = price the employee pays. ESOP Outstanding = equity-like reserve that holds the expense until exercise. FV = nominal value of a share.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('70b6a1b0-b01a-42f6-9e77-3949a87101e1', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'ESOP numerical: Oct 2024 PYQ solved.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q2B (9m)', 'Question: Shares have a face value of ₹10. On 1 Jan 2011 the company granted 50,000 ESOPs at ₹70 when the market price was ₹160. Options were exercisable between 15 Mar and 15 Apr 2011. Employees exercised 47,000; the rest lapsed. Books close on 31 Dec 2011. Pass the entries.

Given Data: Options = 50,000. Exercise price = ₹70. Market price at grant = ₹160. FV = ₹10. Exercised = 47,000. Lapsed = 3,000.

Concept: Intrinsic value method. The vesting period (1 Jan to 15 Mar 2011) ends within the year, so the entire expense falls in 2011.

Step-by-step:

Intrinsic value per option = 160 − 70 = ₹90.
Total compensation = 50,000 × 90 = ₹45,00,000.
Cash on exercise = 47,000 × 70 = ₹32,90,000.
ESOP Outstanding used = 47,000 × 90 = ₹42,30,000.
Share capital = 47,000 × 10 = ₹4,70,000.
Securities premium = 47,000 × (70 + 90 − 10) = 47,000 × 150 = ₹70,50,000.
Lapsed = 3,000 × 90 = ₹2,70,000.

Journal entries:

Date | Particulars |  | Dr (₹) | Cr (₹)
1 Jan–15 Mar 2011 | Employee Compensation Expense A/c | Dr | 45,00,000 | 
 | &nbsp;&nbsp;To ESOP Outstanding A/c |  |  | 45,00,000
 | (Being compensation on 50,000 options at ₹90 charged over the vesting period) |  |  | 
15 Mar–15 Apr 2011 | Bank A/c (47,000 × 70) | Dr | 32,90,000 | 
 | ESOP Outstanding A/c (47,000 × 90) | Dr | 42,30,000 | 
 | &nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c (47,000 × 10) |  |  | 4,70,000
 | &nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c (47,000 × 150) |  |  | 70,50,000
 | (Being 47,000 options exercised and shares allotted) |  |  | 
After 15 Apr 2011 | ESOP Outstanding A/c (3,000 × 90) | Dr | 2,70,000 | 
 | &nbsp;&nbsp;To General Reserve A/c |  |  | 2,70,000
 | (Being 3,000 vested options lapsed unexercised) |  |  | 
31 Dec 2011 | Profit & Loss A/c | Dr | 45,00,000 | 
 | &nbsp;&nbsp;To Employee Compensation Expense A/c |  |  | 45,00,000
 | (Being expense transferred to P&L at year end) |  |  | 

Checks:

Exercise entry: Dr 32,90,000 + 42,30,000 = 75,20,000. Cr 4,70,000 + 70,50,000 = 75,20,000 ✓.
ESOP Outstanding: 45,00,000 − 42,30,000 − 2,70,000 = 0 ✓.

Exam tip: If your teacher reverses the lapsed amount through the Employee Compensation Expense account instead of General Reserve, then ESOP Outstanding Dr 2,70,000 To Employee Compensation Expense 2,70,000, and the P&L transfer becomes ₹42,30,000. Note that SEBI''s minimum vesting period is 1 year, but exam problems often ignore it.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 31, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c31-q-1", "text": "ESOP numerical: Oct 2024 PYQ solved.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-1", "text": "Question: Shares have a face value of ₹10. On 1 Jan 2011 the company granted 50,000 ESOPs at ₹70 when the market price was ₹160. Options were exercisable between 15 Mar and 15 Apr 2011. Employees exercised 47,000; the rest lapsed. Books close on 31 Dec 2011. Pass the entries.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-2", "text": "Given Data: Options = 50,000. Exercise price = ₹70. Market price at grant = ₹160. FV = ₹10. Exercised = 47,000. Lapsed = 3,000.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-3", "text": "Concept: Intrinsic value method. The vesting period (1 Jan to 15 Mar 2011) ends within the year, so the entire expense falls in 2011.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "Step-by-step:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Intrinsic value per option = 160 − 70 = ₹90.", "Total compensation = 50,000 × 90 = ₹45,00,000.", "Cash on exercise = 47,000 × 70 = ₹32,90,000.", "ESOP Outstanding used = 47,000 × 90 = ₹42,30,000.", "Share capital = 47,000 × 10 = ₹4,70,000.", "Securities premium = 47,000 × (70 + 90 − 10) = 47,000 × 150 = ₹70,50,000.", "Lapsed = 3,000 × 90 = ₹2,70,000."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-6", "bold": true, "text": "Journal entries:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-7", "rows": [["Date", "Particulars", "", "Dr (₹)", "Cr (₹)"], ["1 Jan–15 Mar 2011", "Employee Compensation Expense A/c", "Dr", "45,00,000", ""], ["", "&nbsp;&nbsp;To ESOP Outstanding A/c", "", "", "45,00,000"], ["", "(Being compensation on 50,000 options at ₹90 charged over the vesting period)", "", "", ""], ["15 Mar–15 Apr 2011", "Bank A/c (47,000 × 70)", "Dr", "32,90,000", ""], ["", "ESOP Outstanding A/c (47,000 × 90)", "Dr", "42,30,000", ""], ["", "&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c (47,000 × 10)", "", "", "4,70,000"], ["", "&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c (47,000 × 150)", "", "", "70,50,000"], ["", "(Being 47,000 options exercised and shares allotted)", "", "", ""], ["After 15 Apr 2011", "ESOP Outstanding A/c (3,000 × 90)", "Dr", "2,70,000", ""], ["", "&nbsp;&nbsp;To General Reserve A/c", "", "", "2,70,000"], ["", "(Being 3,000 vested options lapsed unexercised)", "", "", ""], ["31 Dec 2011", "Profit & Loss A/c", "Dr", "45,00,000", ""], ["", "&nbsp;&nbsp;To Employee Compensation Expense A/c", "", "", "45,00,000"], ["", "(Being expense transferred to P&L at year end)", "", "", ""]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-8", "bold": true, "text": "Checks:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-9", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Exercise entry: Dr 32,90,000 + 42,30,000 = 75,20,000. Cr 4,70,000 + 70,50,000 = 75,20,000 ✓.", "ESOP Outstanding: 45,00,000 − 42,30,000 − 2,70,000 = 0 ✓."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c31-a-10", "text": "Exam tip: If your teacher reverses the lapsed amount through the Employee Compensation Expense account instead of General Reserve, then ESOP Outstanding Dr 2,70,000 To Employee Compensation Expense 2,70,000, and the P&L transfer becomes ₹42,30,000. Note that SEBI''s minimum vesting period is 1 year, but exam problems often ignore it.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('a2c2fcc7-820a-440d-93c6-7afa6fbf0f52', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'ESOP: alternative formulations (multi-year vesting and forfeiture).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Variations of Oct 2024 Q2B', 'Question: On 1 April 2022 a company grants 10,000 options at ₹50 (market price ₹80, FV ₹10). Vesting period is 2 years. All options are exercised in the third year. Pass the entries.

Steps:

Intrinsic value = 80 − 50 = ₹30. Total = 10,000 × 30 = ₹3,00,000.
Annual expense = 3,00,000 ÷ 2 = ₹1,50,000 per year.

Time | Entry | Amount (₹)
End of Year 1 | Employee Compensation Expense Dr, To ESOP Outstanding. Then P&L Dr, To Employee Compensation Expense | 1,50,000
End of Year 2 | Same entries as Year 1 | 1,50,000
Exercise | Bank Dr 5,00,000 (10,000 × 50) + ESOP Outstanding Dr 3,00,000, To Equity Share Capital 1,00,000 (10,000 × 10), To Securities Premium 7,00,000 (10,000 × 70) | 8,00,000

Check: Dr 8,00,000 = Cr 1,00,000 + 7,00,000 ✓. Premium per share = 50 + 30 − 10 = 70 ✓.

Variation (forfeiture before vesting): If 1,000 options lapse in Year 2 before vesting, the expense for them is reversed. ESOP Outstanding Dr 30,000 (1,000 × 30), To Employee Compensation Expense 30,000, and the remaining expense is spread over the remaining 9,000 options.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 32, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c32-q-1", "text": "ESOP: alternative formulations (multi-year vesting and forfeiture).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c32-a-1", "text": "Question: On 1 April 2022 a company grants 10,000 options at ₹50 (market price ₹80, FV ₹10). Vesting period is 2 years. All options are exercised in the third year. Pass the entries.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c32-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Steps:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c32-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Intrinsic value = 80 − 50 = ₹30. Total = 10,000 × 30 = ₹3,00,000.", "Annual expense = 3,00,000 ÷ 2 = ₹1,50,000 per year."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c32-a-4", "rows": [["Time", "Entry", "Amount (₹)"], ["End of Year 1", "Employee Compensation Expense Dr, To ESOP Outstanding. Then P&L Dr, To Employee Compensation Expense", "1,50,000"], ["End of Year 2", "Same entries as Year 1", "1,50,000"], ["Exercise", "Bank Dr 5,00,000 (10,000 × 50) + ESOP Outstanding Dr 3,00,000, To Equity Share Capital 1,00,000 (10,000 × 10), To Securities Premium 7,00,000 (10,000 × 70)", "8,00,000"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c32-a-5", "text": "Check: Dr 8,00,000 = Cr 1,00,000 + 7,00,000 ✓. Premium per share = 50 + 30 − 10 = 70 ✓.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c32-a-6", "text": "Variation (forfeiture before vesting): If 1,000 options lapse in Year 2 before vesting, the expense for them is reversed. ESOP Outstanding Dr 30,000 (1,000 × 30), To Employee Compensation Expense 30,000, and the remaining expense is spread over the remaining 9,000 options.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('8fdffc09-50d9-4602-acbf-2e917ecd3be7', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Sweat Equity numerical: rules, limits and procedure.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Numerical on Sweat Equity" (not yet asked, so prepare from rules)', 'Formulas:

Fair value of sweat equity = Shares issued × Fair value per share (given or valued).
Equity Share Capital = Shares × FV. Securities Premium = Shares × (Fair value − FV).
Employee compensation/discount = Shares × (Fair value − Price paid).

Entries:

(a) Issued for consideration other than cash (know-how/services): Employee Compensation Expense A/c (or Know-how/Intangible Asset A/c) Dr, To Equity Share Capital, To Securities Premium.
(b) Issued at a discount for cash: Bank A/c Dr (cash paid) + Employee Compensation Expense A/c Dr (discount), To Equity Share Capital, To Securities Premium.

Limit check (Rule 8; verify):

Per-year limit = higher of 15% of paid-up equity capital and ₹5 crore.
Overall limit = 25% of paid-up equity capital at any time.
Example: Paid-up equity ₹60 crore. Per-year = higher of ₹9 crore (15%) and ₹5 crore = ₹9 crore. Overall = 25% = ₹15 crore. If ₹8 crore of sweat equity is already outstanding, the maximum that can be issued now = 15 − 8 = ₹7 crore (the overall cap is binding).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 33, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c33-q-1", "text": "Sweat Equity numerical: rules, limits and procedure.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c33-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Formulas:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c33-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Fair value of sweat equity = Shares issued × Fair value per share (given or valued).", "Equity Share Capital = Shares × FV. Securities Premium = Shares × (Fair value − FV).", "Employee compensation/discount = Shares × (Fair value − Price paid)."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c33-a-3", "bold": true, "text": "Entries:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c33-a-4", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["(a) Issued for consideration other than cash (know-how/services): Employee Compensation Expense A/c (or Know-how/Intangible Asset A/c) Dr, To Equity Share Capital, To Securities Premium.", "(b) Issued at a discount for cash: Bank A/c Dr (cash paid) + Employee Compensation Expense A/c Dr (discount), To Equity Share Capital, To Securities Premium."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c33-a-5", "bold": true, "text": "Limit check (Rule 8; verify):", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c33-a-6", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Per-year limit = higher of 15% of paid-up equity capital and ₹5 crore.", "Overall limit = 25% of paid-up equity capital at any time.", "Example: Paid-up equity ₹60 crore. Per-year = higher of ₹9 crore (15%) and ₹5 crore = ₹9 crore. Overall = 25% = ₹15 crore. If ₹8 crore of sweat equity is already outstanding, the maximum that can be issued now = 15 − 8 = ₹7 crore (the overall cap is binding)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('057e111f-c492-4386-ba2c-5b59476b6151', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Sweat Equity: worked examples.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Example 1 (know-how, no cash). 10,000 sweat equity shares of ₹10 each are issued to directors for know-how. Fair value per share = ₹25.

Fair value = 10,000 × 25 = ₹2,50,000. Capital = 10,000 × 10 = ₹1,00,000. Premium = 10,000 × 15 = ₹1,50,000.

Particulars |  | Dr (₹) | Cr (₹)
Employee Compensation Expense A/c (or Know-how A/c) | Dr | 2,50,000 | 
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c |  |  | 1,00,000
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c |  |  | 1,50,000

Check: 1,00,000 + 1,50,000 = 2,50,000 ✓.

Example 2 (issued at a discount for cash). 5,000 sweat equity shares of FV ₹10 are issued to employees at ₹25 each. Fair value = ₹40.

Cash = 5,000 × 25 = ₹1,25,000. Fair value = 5,000 × 40 = ₹2,00,000. Discount = 5,000 × 15 = ₹75,000. Capital = ₹50,000. Premium = 5,000 × 30 = ₹1,50,000.

Particulars |  | Dr (₹) | Cr (₹)
Bank A/c | Dr | 1,25,000 | 
Employee Compensation Expense A/c | Dr | 75,000 | 
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c |  |  | 50,000
&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c |  |  | 1,50,000

Check: Dr 1,25,000 + 75,000 = 2,00,000. Cr 50,000 + 1,50,000 = 2,00,000 ✓.

Exam tip: Always state the accounting treatment you assume (expense vs intangible asset), and mention the 3-year lock-in.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 34, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c34-q-1", "text": "Sweat Equity: worked examples.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-1", "text": "Example 1 (know-how, no cash). 10,000 sweat equity shares of ₹10 each are issued to directors for know-how. Fair value per share = ₹25.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Fair value = 10,000 × 25 = ₹2,50,000. Capital = 10,000 × 10 = ₹1,00,000. Premium = 10,000 × 15 = ₹1,50,000."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-3", "rows": [["Particulars", "", "Dr (₹)", "Cr (₹)"], ["Employee Compensation Expense A/c (or Know-how A/c)", "Dr", "2,50,000", ""], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c", "", "", "1,00,000"], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c", "", "", "1,50,000"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-4", "text": "Check: 1,00,000 + 1,50,000 = 2,50,000 ✓.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-5", "text": "Example 2 (issued at a discount for cash). 5,000 sweat equity shares of FV ₹10 are issued to employees at ₹25 each. Fair value = ₹40.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-6", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Cash = 5,000 × 25 = ₹1,25,000. Fair value = 5,000 × 40 = ₹2,00,000. Discount = 5,000 × 15 = ₹75,000. Capital = ₹50,000. Premium = 5,000 × 30 = ₹1,50,000."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-7", "rows": [["Particulars", "", "Dr (₹)", "Cr (₹)"], ["Bank A/c", "Dr", "1,25,000", ""], ["Employee Compensation Expense A/c", "Dr", "75,000", ""], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Equity Share Capital A/c", "", "", "50,000"], ["&nbsp;&nbsp;To Securities Premium A/c", "", "", "1,50,000"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-8", "text": "Check: Dr 1,25,000 + 75,000 = 2,00,000. Cr 50,000 + 1,50,000 = 2,00,000 ✓.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c34-a-9", "text": "Exam tip: Always state the accounting treatment you assume (expense vs intangible asset), and mention the 3-year lock-in.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('39444741-ba8f-4a73-9e5a-0dfe174ccb59', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'IPO numerical 1: Fixed Price Issue: pro-rata allotment and refund.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "IPO — methods followed (simple numerical)"', 'Question: A company issues 10,00,000 shares (FV ₹10) at ₹50 (premium ₹40). Applications are received for 25,00,000 shares. Applicant A applied for 800 shares and B for 2,000 shares. Find the subscription ratio, allotment and refund, and the amounts raised.

Formula: Subscription multiple = Shares applied ÷ Shares offered. Allotment = Shares applied ÷ Subscription multiple. Refund = (Applied − Allotted) × Issue price.

Steps:

Multiple = 25,00,000 ÷ 10,00,000 = 2.5 times (allotment ratio 40%).
A: 800 ÷ 2.5 = 320 shares. Refund = 480 × 50 = ₹24,000.
B: 2,000 ÷ 2.5 = 800 shares. Refund = 1,200 × 50 = ₹60,000.
Total raised = 10,00,000 × 50 = ₹5,00,00,000. Capital = ₹1,00,00,000. Premium = ₹4,00,00,000.

Checks: A paid 800 × 50 = 40,000 = allotted 16,000 + refund 24,000 ✓. Total refund = (25,00,000 − 10,00,000) × 50 = ₹7,50,00,000 ✓. Exam tip: In practice, allotment is rounded to market lots, but simple problems use pure pro rata.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 35, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c35-q-1", "text": "IPO numerical 1: Fixed Price Issue: pro-rata allotment and refund.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c35-a-1", "text": "Question: A company issues 10,00,000 shares (FV ₹10) at ₹50 (premium ₹40). Applications are received for 25,00,000 shares. Applicant A applied for 800 shares and B for 2,000 shares. Find the subscription ratio, allotment and refund, and the amounts raised.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c35-a-2", "text": "Formula: Subscription multiple = Shares applied ÷ Shares offered. Allotment = Shares applied ÷ Subscription multiple. Refund = (Applied − Allotted) × Issue price.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c35-a-3", "bold": true, "text": "Steps:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c35-a-4", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Multiple = 25,00,000 ÷ 10,00,000 = 2.5 times (allotment ratio 40%).", "A: 800 ÷ 2.5 = 320 shares. Refund = 480 × 50 = ₹24,000.", "B: 2,000 ÷ 2.5 = 800 shares. Refund = 1,200 × 50 = ₹60,000.", "Total raised = 10,00,000 × 50 = ₹5,00,00,000. Capital = ₹1,00,00,000. Premium = ₹4,00,00,000."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c35-a-5", "text": "Checks: A paid 800 × 50 = 40,000 = allotted 16,000 + refund 24,000 ✓. Total refund = (25,00,000 − 10,00,000) × 50 = ₹7,50,00,000 ✓. Exam tip: In practice, allotment is rounded to market lots, but simple problems use pure pro rata.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('a2548579-e9dd-4bf1-a775-07333e2738b0', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'IPO numerical 3: category reservation and subscription multiples.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Question: Net offer = 10,00,000 shares (profit-track issuer). Reservation: QIB 50%, NII 15%, Retail 35%. Bids received: QIB 20,00,000; NII 6,00,000; Retail 7,00,000. Find each category''s subscription.

Steps:

QIB portion = 5,00,000. NII = 1,50,000. Retail = 3,50,000.
QIB subscription = 20,00,000 ÷ 5,00,000 = 4.0×.
NII subscription = 6,00,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = 4.0×.
Retail subscription = 7,00,000 ÷ 3,50,000 = 2.0×.
Overall = (20 + 6 + 7) lakh ÷ 10 lakh = 3.3×.

Check: Category portions add up to 10,00,000 (5,00,000 + 1,50,000 + 3,50,000) ✓.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 37, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c37-q-1", "text": "IPO numerical 3: category reservation and subscription multiples.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c37-a-1", "text": "Question: Net offer = 10,00,000 shares (profit-track issuer). Reservation: QIB 50%, NII 15%, Retail 35%. Bids received: QIB 20,00,000; NII 6,00,000; Retail 7,00,000. Find each category''s subscription.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c37-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "Steps:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c37-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["QIB portion = 5,00,000. NII = 1,50,000. Retail = 3,50,000.", "QIB subscription = 20,00,000 ÷ 5,00,000 = 4.0×.", "NII subscription = 6,00,000 ÷ 1,50,000 = 4.0×.", "Retail subscription = 7,00,000 ÷ 3,50,000 = 2.0×.", "Overall = (20 + 6 + 7) lakh ÷ 10 lakh = 3.3×."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c37-a-4", "text": "Check: Category portions add up to 10,00,000 (5,00,000 + 1,50,000 + 3,50,000) ✓.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('a2423630-4f64-444a-97b2-837e8304ab2d', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'IPO numerical 4: Pricing using the P/E method.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus (links to the merchant banker''s role in pricing)', 'Question: A company has a PAT of ₹30 crore and 3 crore shares. Listed peers trade at an average P/E of 20. It plans an IPO at a 10% discount to fair value and a fresh issue of 50 lakh shares (FV ₹10). Find the issue price and funds raised.

Formula: EPS = PAT ÷ Shares. Fair price = EPS × Industry P/E.

Steps:

EPS = 30 ÷ 3 = ₹10.
Fair price = 10 × 20 = ₹200.
Issue price after 10% discount = 200 × 0.90 = ₹180.
Funds raised = 0.5 crore × 180 = ₹90 crore (capital 0.5 × 10 = ₹5 crore + premium 0.5 × 170 = ₹85 crore ✓).
Post-issue shares = 3.5 crore. Market cap at the issue price = 3.5 × 180 = ₹630 crore.

Exam tip: Mention that the merchant banker also cross-checks P/E with other methods (DCF, NAV), and that the "IPO discount" leaves room for listing gains.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 38, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c38-q-1", "text": "IPO numerical 4: Pricing using the P/E method.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c38-a-1", "text": "Question: A company has a PAT of ₹30 crore and 3 crore shares. Listed peers trade at an average P/E of 20. It plans an IPO at a 10% discount to fair value and a fresh issue of 50 lakh shares (FV ₹10). Find the issue price and funds raised.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c38-a-2", "text": "Formula: EPS = PAT ÷ Shares. Fair price = EPS × Industry P/E.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c38-a-3", "bold": true, "text": "Steps:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c38-a-4", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["EPS = 30 ÷ 3 = ₹10.", "Fair price = 10 × 20 = ₹200.", "Issue price after 10% discount = 200 × 0.90 = ₹180.", "Funds raised = 0.5 crore × 180 = ₹90 crore (capital 0.5 × 10 = ₹5 crore + premium 0.5 × 170 = ₹85 crore ✓).", "Post-issue shares = 3.5 crore. Market cap at the issue price = 3.5 × 180 = ₹630 crore."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c38-a-5", "text": "Exam tip: Mention that the merchant banker also cross-checks P/E with other methods (DCF, NAV), and that the \"IPO discount\" leaves room for listing gains.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('8f2d2641-96e7-42f7-80aa-8c2a771d8751', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Common mistakes and how to verify any Unit 2 numerical.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Applies to Oct 2024 Q2B and 2025 Q2.3', 'Common mistakes

Using the total offered/granted shares instead of the subscribed/exercised shares.
Confusing face value with issue price (capital = FV only; the rest is premium).
In ESOP: using FV or exercise price for intrinsic value, instead of market price − exercise price.
Wrong securities premium in ESOP (correct = exercise price + intrinsic value − FV).
Forgetting the ESOP Outstanding A/c, lapse entry or year-end P&L transfer.
Passing entries for unsubscribed rights shares.
Missing the narration, or the "Dr/To" format.
Building the book-building table from the lowest price instead of the highest.
Not stating assumptions (vesting period, treatment of lapse).

Verification checklist

Total Dr = Total Cr in every entry.
Capital = shares × FV.
Cash + ESOP Outstanding (or discount) = Capital + Premium.
ESOP Outstanding closes to zero after exercise and lapse.
Subscription percentage matches the shares given.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 39, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', 'bd81773e-1a5e-4ef0-8417-167ad3e3dad3', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c39-q-1", "text": "Common mistakes and how to verify any Unit 2 numerical.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c39-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Common mistakes", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c39-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Using the total offered/granted shares instead of the subscribed/exercised shares.", "Confusing face value with issue price (capital = FV only; the rest is premium).", "In ESOP: using FV or exercise price for intrinsic value, instead of market price − exercise price.", "Wrong securities premium in ESOP (correct = exercise price + intrinsic value − FV).", "Forgetting the ESOP Outstanding A/c, lapse entry or year-end P&L transfer.", "Passing entries for unsubscribed rights shares.", "Missing the narration, or the \"Dr/To\" format.", "Building the book-building table from the lowest price instead of the highest.", "Not stating assumptions (vesting period, treatment of lapse)."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c39-a-3", "bold": true, "text": "Verification checklist", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c39-a-4", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Total Dr = Total Cr in every entry.", "Capital = shares × FV.", "Cash + ESOP Outstanding (or discount) = Capital + Premium.", "ESOP Outstanding closes to zero after exercise and lapse.", "Subscription percentage matches the shares given."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('629d71ab-2493-4f97-9c64-2b8533476d23', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Define Stock Exchange. Explain its functions.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Definition & Functions of Stock Exchanges"; foundation for 2025 Q2.1', 'Definition (SCRA 1956, Sec 2(j)): any body of individuals, whether incorporated or not, constituted for the purpose of assisting, regulating or controlling the business of buying, selling or dealing in securities.
Functions:

Continuous market and liquidity for securities.
Price discovery through demand and supply.
Capital mobilisation, by giving a listing venue for new issues.
Safety of transactions, through rules, margins and settlement guarantees.
Economic barometer (Sensex, Nifty).
Investor protection and grievance handling.
Promotion of savings and investment, and wealth creation.
Corporate governance, through listing and disclosure norms.
Facilitates disinvestment and PSU listings.
Hedging and risk management (derivatives).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 40, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c40-q-1", "text": "Define Stock Exchange. Explain its functions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c40-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Definition (SCRA 1956, Sec 2(j)): any body of individuals, whether incorporated or not, constituted for the purpose of assisting, regulating or controlling the business of buying, selling or dealing in securities.", "Functions:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c40-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Continuous market and liquidity for securities.", "Price discovery through demand and supply.", "Capital mobilisation, by giving a listing venue for new issues.", "Safety of transactions, through rules, margins and settlement guarantees.", "Economic barometer (Sensex, Nifty).", "Investor protection and grievance handling.", "Promotion of savings and investment, and wealth creation.", "Corporate governance, through listing and disclosure norms.", "Facilitates disinvestment and PSU listings.", "Hedging and risk management (derivatives)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('ebc17c46-fc94-4b8d-bf03-922546297992', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Evolution and growth of Stock Exchanges (global and India).', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Year | Event
1602 | Amsterdam Stock Exchange trades shares of the Dutch East India Company (first stock exchange)
1792 | NYSE origins (Buttonwood Agreement); London Stock Exchange formalised in 1801
1850s / 1875 | Brokers meet under a banyan tree in Mumbai; BSE (1875), Asia''s oldest
1894–1947 | Ahmedabad (1894), Calcutta (1908), Madras (1937), Delhi (1947) and other regional exchanges
1956 | SCRA passed; BSE recognised in 1957
1971 | NASDAQ, the first electronic exchange
1988–1992 | SEBI set up (1988), statutory in 1992; OTCEI (1992), NSE incorporated (1992)
1994–95 | NSE starts trading (1994), BOLT screen-based trading at BSE (1995)
2000 | Index derivatives launched
2005 | Demutualisation and corporatisation of exchanges (separation of ownership, management, trading rights)
2012–13 | SEBI''s exit policy for inactive regional exchanges
2017 | BSE lists on itself; India INX at GIFT City
2023 | T+1 settlement in all equities

Growth features: Move from floor trading to screen-based trading, national reach, derivatives, tighter regulation, more retail participation and international linkages.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 41, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c41-q-1", "text": "Evolution and growth of Stock Exchanges (global and India).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c41-a-1", "rows": [["Year", "Event"], ["1602", "Amsterdam Stock Exchange trades shares of the Dutch East India Company (first stock exchange)"], ["1792", "NYSE origins (Buttonwood Agreement); London Stock Exchange formalised in 1801"], ["1850s / 1875", "Brokers meet under a banyan tree in Mumbai; BSE (1875), Asia''s oldest"], ["1894–1947", "Ahmedabad (1894), Calcutta (1908), Madras (1937), Delhi (1947) and other regional exchanges"], ["1956", "SCRA passed; BSE recognised in 1957"], ["1971", "NASDAQ, the first electronic exchange"], ["1988–1992", "SEBI set up (1988), statutory in 1992; OTCEI (1992), NSE incorporated (1992)"], ["1994–95", "NSE starts trading (1994), BOLT screen-based trading at BSE (1995)"], ["2000", "Index derivatives launched"], ["2005", "Demutualisation and corporatisation of exchanges (separation of ownership, management, trading rights)"], ["2012–13", "SEBI''s exit policy for inactive regional exchanges"], ["2017", "BSE lists on itself; India INX at GIFT City"], ["2023", "T+1 settlement in all equities"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c41-a-2", "text": "Growth features: Move from floor trading to screen-based trading, national reach, derivatives, tighter regulation, more retail participation and international linkages.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('307f24be-9e89-4ab0-9973-8e7349142840', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Categorize the different types of Stock Exchanges in India.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q2.1 (7.5m)', 'A good answer classifies on more than one basis.

1. By geographical coverage

National exchanges: NSE, BSE, Metropolitan Stock Exchange (MSE).
Regional exchanges: older exchanges (Calcutta, Ahmedabad, Delhi, Madras, etc.). Most have exited or become inactive under SEBI''s exit policy.
International exchanges: India INX and NSE IX at GIFT City.

2. By products traded

Equity/cash market exchanges: NSE, BSE, MSE.
Derivatives (F&O) and currency segments on NSE, BSE and MSE.
Commodity exchanges: MCX, NCDEX (regulated by SEBI since 2015).
Debt segments (Wholesale Debt Market).
Special platforms: BSE SME, NSE Emerge, Innovators Growth Platform.

3. By ownership and structure

Demutualised/corporatised exchanges: ownership, management and trading rights are separated (NSE, BSE).
Older mutual (broker-owned) exchanges, run by member brokers.

4. By trading mechanism

Floor-based (open outcry) in the past.
Screen-based/electronic trading today. OTCEI was a counter-based over-the-counter model.

5. By regulatory status

Recognised by SEBI under SCRA (permanent or annual recognition).
Derecognised/exited exchanges.

Exam tip: Draw a short tree diagram, and give one example against each type.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 42, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c42-q-1", "text": "Categorize the different types of Stock Exchanges in India.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-1", "text": "A good answer classifies on more than one basis.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "1. By geographical coverage", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["National exchanges: NSE, BSE, Metropolitan Stock Exchange (MSE).", "Regional exchanges: older exchanges (Calcutta, Ahmedabad, Delhi, Madras, etc.). Most have exited or become inactive under SEBI''s exit policy.", "International exchanges: India INX and NSE IX at GIFT City."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "2. By products traded", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Equity/cash market exchanges: NSE, BSE, MSE.", "Derivatives (F&O) and currency segments on NSE, BSE and MSE.", "Commodity exchanges: MCX, NCDEX (regulated by SEBI since 2015).", "Debt segments (Wholesale Debt Market).", "Special platforms: BSE SME, NSE Emerge, Innovators Growth Platform."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-6", "bold": true, "text": "3. By ownership and structure", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-7", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Demutualised/corporatised exchanges: ownership, management and trading rights are separated (NSE, BSE).", "Older mutual (broker-owned) exchanges, run by member brokers."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-8", "bold": true, "text": "4. By trading mechanism", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-9", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Floor-based (open outcry) in the past.", "Screen-based/electronic trading today. OTCEI was a counter-based over-the-counter model."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-10", "bold": true, "text": "5. By regulatory status", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-11", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Recognised by SEBI under SCRA (permanent or annual recognition).", "Derecognised/exited exchanges."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c42-a-12", "text": "Exam tip: Draw a short tree diagram, and give one example against each type.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('8ae14900-3dca-4729-a558-719d90fdd66d', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Stock Exchanges in India: current position.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Stock Exchanges in India"; supports 2025 Q2.1', 'Nationwide equity exchanges recognised by SEBI: NSE, BSE and MSE (formerly MCX-SX; currency derivatives 2008, equity 2013).
International exchanges (GIFT City, Gandhinagar): India INX (BSE, 2017) and NSE IX.
Commodity exchanges: MCX, NCDEX and a few others.
Historical regional exchanges: Calcutta, Ahmedabad, Delhi, Madras, etc. Most have exited under SEBI''s exit policy. (Verify the latest list on SEBI''s website.)
Concentration: NSE and BSE account for nearly all equity trading.
Regulation: SEBI (SCRA 1956, SEBI Act 1992, and the SEBI (Stock Exchanges and Clearing Corporations) Regulations 2018).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 43, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c43-q-1", "text": "Stock Exchanges in India: current position.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c43-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Nationwide equity exchanges recognised by SEBI: NSE, BSE and MSE (formerly MCX-SX; currency derivatives 2008, equity 2013).", "International exchanges (GIFT City, Gandhinagar): India INX (BSE, 2017) and NSE IX.", "Commodity exchanges: MCX, NCDEX and a few others.", "Historical regional exchanges: Calcutta, Ahmedabad, Delhi, Madras, etc. Most have exited under SEBI''s exit policy. (Verify the latest list on SEBI''s website.)", "Concentration: NSE and BSE account for nearly all equity trading.", "Regulation: SEBI (SCRA 1956, SEBI Act 1992, and the SEBI (Stock Exchanges and Clearing Corporations) Regulations 2018)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('e4f4d71e-8c66-408b-8d56-4ef473a69e4e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'National Stock Exchange (NSE).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "NSE"', 'Incorporated: Nov 1992. Recognised as a stock exchange in 1993. Promoted by leading financial institutions (IDBI, ICICI, IFCI, LIC, GIC, SBI and others).
Operations began: Wholesale Debt Market (June 1994), Capital Market (Nov 1994). Derivatives in 2000.
Headquarters: Mumbai.
Features:

Screen-based, order-driven, nationwide trading (NEAT system), fully electronic.
Demutualised from inception.
Index: Nifty 50 (launched 1996), plus other indices.
Segments: Capital market, F&O, currency derivatives, debt, SME (NSE Emerge), IPO/listing, and NSE IX at GIFT City.
Clearing through NSE Clearing Ltd.
Among the world''s largest derivatives exchanges by number of contracts traded (verify latest WFE data).

Contribution: Transparency, technology, faster settlement and wider investor access.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 44, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c44-q-1", "text": "National Stock Exchange (NSE).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c44-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Incorporated: Nov 1992. Recognised as a stock exchange in 1993. Promoted by leading financial institutions (IDBI, ICICI, IFCI, LIC, GIC, SBI and others).", "Operations began: Wholesale Debt Market (June 1994), Capital Market (Nov 1994). Derivatives in 2000.", "Headquarters: Mumbai.", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c44-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Screen-based, order-driven, nationwide trading (NEAT system), fully electronic.", "Demutualised from inception.", "Index: Nifty 50 (launched 1996), plus other indices.", "Segments: Capital market, F&O, currency derivatives, debt, SME (NSE Emerge), IPO/listing, and NSE IX at GIFT City.", "Clearing through NSE Clearing Ltd.", "Among the world''s largest derivatives exchanges by number of contracts traded (verify latest WFE data)."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c44-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Contribution: Transparency, technology, faster settlement and wider investor access."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('97e7097f-b1d6-4a0f-a498-c3601073c1a7', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE).', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "BSE"', 'Established: 1875 as the Native Share & Stock Brokers'' Association. Asia''s oldest exchange. It was the first exchange to get permanent recognition under SCRA (1957).
Features:

BOLT screen-based trading from 1995.
Corporatised and demutualised in 2005 (BSE Ltd).
Listed on itself in 2017, the first listed exchange in India.
Index: S&P BSE Sensex (30 stocks, launched 1986, base year 1978–79 = 100).
Segments: Equity, derivatives, currency, debt, SME platform, mutual fund platform (BSE StAR MF) and India INX at GIFT City.
Has the largest number of listed companies (over 5,000, verify latest).
Clearing: Indian Clearing Corporation Ltd. (ICCL).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 45, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c45-q-1", "text": "Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c45-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Established: 1875 as the Native Share & Stock Brokers'' Association. Asia''s oldest exchange. It was the first exchange to get permanent recognition under SCRA (1957).", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c45-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["BOLT screen-based trading from 1995.", "Corporatised and demutualised in 2005 (BSE Ltd).", "Listed on itself in 2017, the first listed exchange in India.", "Index: S&P BSE Sensex (30 stocks, launched 1986, base year 1978–79 = 100).", "Segments: Equity, derivatives, currency, debt, SME platform, mutual fund platform (BSE StAR MF) and India INX at GIFT City.", "Has the largest number of listed companies (over 5,000, verify latest).", "Clearing: Indian Clearing Corporation Ltd. (ICCL)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('697e1711-e394-4fd0-8433-661fa114446e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Demat Trading: process and features.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Demat Trading"', 'Meaning: Buying and selling of shares held in dematerialised (electronic) form. Trading of listed securities is compulsory in demat form.
Requirements: Bank account + trading account (broker) + demat account (DP with NSDL/CDSL) + KYC.
Process (buy):

Place an order through the broker (online/mobile/phone).
The exchange matches the order, and the broker sends a trade confirmation/contract note.
The clearing corporation determines obligations.
Pay-in/pay-out on T+1: funds are settled, and shares are credited to the demat account.

Process (sell): Place the order → delivery through the e-DIS/DIS authorisation → shares debited and funds credited on T+1.
Costs: Brokerage, STT, GST, stamp duty, exchange charges, SEBI turnover fees and DP charges.
Advantages: No risk of theft or forgery, faster settlement, easy transfer and lower costs.
Timings: Pre-open session 9:00–9:15 am and normal market 9:15 am–3:30 pm (verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 52, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c52-q-1", "text": "Demat Trading: process and features.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c52-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Buying and selling of shares held in dematerialised (electronic) form. Trading of listed securities is compulsory in demat form.", "Requirements: Bank account + trading account (broker) + demat account (DP with NSDL/CDSL) + KYC.", "Process (buy):"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c52-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Place an order through the broker (online/mobile/phone).", "The exchange matches the order, and the broker sends a trade confirmation/contract note.", "The clearing corporation determines obligations.", "Pay-in/pay-out on T+1: funds are settled, and shares are credited to the demat account."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c52-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Process (sell): Place the order → delivery through the e-DIS/DIS authorisation → shares debited and funds credited on T+1.", "Costs: Brokerage, STT, GST, stamp duty, exchange charges, SEBI turnover fees and DP charges.", "Advantages: No risk of theft or forgery, faster settlement, easy transfer and lower costs.", "Timings: Pre-open session 9:00–9:15 am and normal market 9:15 am–3:30 pm (verify)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('d0177424-9f4d-4993-9682-69300396ff43', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between NSE and BSE.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus (comparison form is possible)', 'Basis | NSE | BSE
Established | 1992 (trading 1994) | 1875
Legal status | Demutualised from start | Corporatised and demutualised in 2005
Trading system | NEAT | BOLT (BOLT+)
Benchmark index | Nifty 50 (50 stocks) | Sensex (30 stocks)
Listed companies | Fewer (approx. 2,000+) | More (5,000+)
Turnover | Highest, especially in F&O | Lower
Recognition | Recognised 1993 | Permanent recognition 1957
Clearing corporation | NSE Clearing | ICCL
SME platform | NSE Emerge | BSE SME
International arm | NSE IX | India INX

(Figures such as the count of listed companies change; verify.)', NULL, NULL, NULL, 46, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c46-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between NSE and BSE.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c46-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "NSE", "BSE"], ["Established", "1992 (trading 1994)", "1875"], ["Legal status", "Demutualised from start", "Corporatised and demutualised in 2005"], ["Trading system", "NEAT", "BOLT (BOLT+)"], ["Benchmark index", "Nifty 50 (50 stocks)", "Sensex (30 stocks)"], ["Listed companies", "Fewer (approx. 2,000+)", "More (5,000+)"], ["Turnover", "Highest, especially in F&O", "Lower"], ["Recognition", "Recognised 1993", "Permanent recognition 1957"], ["Clearing corporation", "NSE Clearing", "ICCL"], ["SME platform", "NSE Emerge", "BSE SME"], ["International arm", "NSE IX", "India INX"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c46-a-2", "text": "(Figures such as the count of listed companies change; verify.)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('cfb8e92f-7005-452e-8b81-6efb3f9523bf', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'OTCEI (Over the Counter Exchange of India).', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "OTCEI"', 'Meaning: An exchange that trades securities over the counter, without a central trading floor. Modelled on the US NASDAQ.
Set up: Incorporated 1990, operations started in 1992 in Mumbai. Promoted by institutions such as UTI, ICICI, IDBI, IFCI, SBI Capital Markets, LIC and GIC.
Objective: To help small and medium companies raise capital and to provide liquidity to investors.
Features:

Sponsor member (a member who sponsors and does the initial appraisal of the company).
Market makers quote two-way prices (buy and sell), providing liquidity.
Counter receipts and trading through counters at member offices in different cities.
Computerised network for price display.
Lower listing costs.

Failure: Low volumes and liquidity, lack of investor interest, and competition from screen-based NSE and BSE. It became inactive and was later wound up/de-recognised (mid-2010s, verify).
Legacy: Its ideas live on in SME platforms and market-making.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 47, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c47-q-1", "text": "OTCEI (Over the Counter Exchange of India).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c47-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: An exchange that trades securities over the counter, without a central trading floor. Modelled on the US NASDAQ.", "Set up: Incorporated 1990, operations started in 1992 in Mumbai. Promoted by institutions such as UTI, ICICI, IDBI, IFCI, SBI Capital Markets, LIC and GIC.", "Objective: To help small and medium companies raise capital and to provide liquidity to investors.", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c47-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Sponsor member (a member who sponsors and does the initial appraisal of the company).", "Market makers quote two-way prices (buy and sell), providing liquidity.", "Counter receipts and trading through counters at member offices in different cities.", "Computerised network for price display.", "Lower listing costs."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c47-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Failure: Low volumes and liquidity, lack of investor interest, and competition from screen-based NSE and BSE. It became inactive and was later wound up/de-recognised (mid-2010s, verify).", "Legacy: Its ideas live on in SME platforms and market-making."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('c8f1dbd9-4752-4183-8633-87fc88ca95d0', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Overseas stock exchanges.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Overseas Stock Exchanges"', 'Exchange | Country | Key facts
NYSE | USA | Origins 1792; the world''s largest by market capitalisation; part of ICE; index: Dow Jones Industrial Average (30 stocks, price-weighted) and S&P 500
NASDAQ | USA | 1971; first electronic exchange; tech-heavy; NASDAQ Composite
London Stock Exchange (LSE) | UK | Formalised 1801; part of LSE Group; FTSE 100
Tokyo Stock Exchange (TSE) | Japan | 1878; part of Japan Exchange Group; Nikkei 225
Shanghai Stock Exchange (SSE) | China | 1990; SSE Composite; STAR Market
Hong Kong Exchanges (HKEX) | Hong Kong | Hang Seng Index
Euronext, Deutsche Börse (Frankfurt), SGX, TSX | Europe / Singapore / Canada | Regional leaders

Link with India: Indian companies have raised funds abroad through ADRs/GDRs and FCCBs. Rules for direct listing of Indian companies on overseas exchanges were notified in 2024 (verify). GIFT City exchanges (India INX, NSE IX) provide an international listing and trading venue inside India.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 48, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c48-q-1", "text": "Overseas stock exchanges.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c48-a-1", "rows": [["Exchange", "Country", "Key facts"], ["NYSE", "USA", "Origins 1792; the world''s largest by market capitalisation; part of ICE; index: Dow Jones Industrial Average (30 stocks, price-weighted) and S&P 500"], ["NASDAQ", "USA", "1971; first electronic exchange; tech-heavy; NASDAQ Composite"], ["London Stock Exchange (LSE)", "UK", "Formalised 1801; part of LSE Group; FTSE 100"], ["Tokyo Stock Exchange (TSE)", "Japan", "1878; part of Japan Exchange Group; Nikkei 225"], ["Shanghai Stock Exchange (SSE)", "China", "1990; SSE Composite; STAR Market"], ["Hong Kong Exchanges (HKEX)", "Hong Kong", "Hang Seng Index"], ["Euronext, Deutsche Börse (Frankfurt), SGX, TSX", "Europe / Singapore / Canada", "Regional leaders"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c48-a-2", "text": "Link with India: Indian companies have raised funds abroad through ADRs/GDRs and FCCBs. Rules for direct listing of Indian companies on overseas exchanges were notified in 2024 (verify). GIFT City exchanges (India INX, NSE IX) provide an international listing and trading venue inside India.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('fa859280-8cbf-482d-a4a6-29d48b840944', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Recent developments in Stock Exchanges.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Recent Developments in Stock Exchanges"', 'T+1 settlement was fully implemented in Jan 2023, and an optional T+0 (beta) was introduced in March 2024.
IPO listing in T+3 days (2023), and UPI-based ASBA for applications.
SEBI''s F&O measures (2024) after a SEBI study found heavy losses among retail F&O traders: higher contract sizes, fewer weekly expiries and upfront premium collection (verify details).
GIFT City exchanges (India INX, NSE IX), and GIFT Nifty replacing SGX Nifty (2023).
Growth of the SME IPO segment, with SEBI tightening the norms.
Record retail participation: demat accounts have crossed roughly 19–20 crore (verify) and there is strong SIP inflow.
SEBI SCORES 2.0 (2024) and the Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) platform (2023).
Regulation of finfluencers and a framework for retail algorithmic trading (2025; verify status).
Rules enabling direct listing of Indian companies abroad (2024; verify).
Tech developments include mobile apps, AI-based surveillance and stronger cyber-security norms for brokers.

Exam tip: Pick 5–6 developments and give one line each. Avoid quoting exact numbers unless sure.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 49, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c49-q-1", "text": "Recent developments in Stock Exchanges.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c49-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["T+1 settlement was fully implemented in Jan 2023, and an optional T+0 (beta) was introduced in March 2024.", "IPO listing in T+3 days (2023), and UPI-based ASBA for applications.", "SEBI''s F&O measures (2024) after a SEBI study found heavy losses among retail F&O traders: higher contract sizes, fewer weekly expiries and upfront premium collection (verify details).", "GIFT City exchanges (India INX, NSE IX), and GIFT Nifty replacing SGX Nifty (2023).", "Growth of the SME IPO segment, with SEBI tightening the norms.", "Record retail participation: demat accounts have crossed roughly 19–20 crore (verify) and there is strong SIP inflow.", "SEBI SCORES 2.0 (2024) and the Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) platform (2023).", "Regulation of finfluencers and a framework for retail algorithmic trading (2025; verify status).", "Rules enabling direct listing of Indian companies abroad (2024; verify).", "Tech developments include mobile apps, AI-based surveillance and stronger cyber-security norms for brokers."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c49-a-2", "text": "Exam tip: Pick 5–6 developments and give one line each. Avoid quoting exact numbers unless sure.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('05bed295-5a22-48df-bd50-5a75257f491b', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Stock Market Indices: meaning, methods and uses.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Stock Market Indices"', 'Meaning: A stock market index is a statistical measure of the price movement of a group of selected shares, showing the overall market or sector trend.
Methods of construction:

Price-weighted (Dow Jones, Nikkei 225): index depends on share prices.
Market-capitalisation weighted: weight = market cap.
Free-float market-cap weighted (Sensex since 2003, Nifty since 2009): only shares available for public trading count.
Equal-weighted.

Indian indices: Sensex, Nifty 50, Nifty Next 50, Nifty Bank, BSE 500, BSE Midcap/Smallcap, sectoral indices and India VIX (volatility).
Uses: Economic barometer, benchmark for fund performance, underlying for index funds, ETFs and derivatives, and sentiment indicator.
Limitations: Limited stock coverage, changes in composition and sensitivity to a few large stocks.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 50, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c50-q-1", "text": "Stock Market Indices: meaning, methods and uses.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c50-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: A stock market index is a statistical measure of the price movement of a group of selected shares, showing the overall market or sector trend.", "Methods of construction:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c50-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Price-weighted (Dow Jones, Nikkei 225): index depends on share prices.", "Market-capitalisation weighted: weight = market cap.", "Free-float market-cap weighted (Sensex since 2003, Nifty since 2009): only shares available for public trading count.", "Equal-weighted."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c50-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Indian indices: Sensex, Nifty 50, Nifty Next 50, Nifty Bank, BSE 500, BSE Midcap/Smallcap, sectoral indices and India VIX (volatility).", "Uses: Economic barometer, benchmark for fund performance, underlying for index funds, ETFs and derivatives, and sentiment indicator.", "Limitations: Limited stock coverage, changes in composition and sensitivity to a few large stocks."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('c0cfe321-6261-49b3-82bb-2160ee5eff71', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Mobile-based Investing.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Mobile-based Investing"', 'Meaning: Investing and trading through smartphone apps of brokers and fintech platforms.
Features:

Instant e-KYC (Aadhaar/DigiLocker) and paperless account opening.
UPI payments and IPO applications.
SIPs in stocks and mutual funds with small amounts.
Push notifications and price alerts.
Low-cost or zero-brokerage delivery trades.

Impact: Democratised access, so many first-time investors from smaller towns joined the market after 2020.
Risks: Excess trading in F&O, fake apps and scams, "finfluencer" tips and data-security risks.
Regulatory response: SEBI rules on finfluencers, verified-app guidance (SEBI Check) and investor education (SEBI Saarthi app).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 54, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c54-q-1", "text": "Mobile-based Investing.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c54-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Investing and trading through smartphone apps of brokers and fintech platforms.", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c54-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Instant e-KYC (Aadhaar/DigiLocker) and paperless account opening.", "UPI payments and IPO applications.", "SIPs in stocks and mutual funds with small amounts.", "Push notifications and price alerts.", "Low-cost or zero-brokerage delivery trades."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c54-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Impact: Democratised access, so many first-time investors from smaller towns joined the market after 2020.", "Risks: Excess trading in F&O, fake apps and scams, \"finfluencer\" tips and data-security risks.", "Regulatory response: SEBI rules on finfluencers, verified-app guidance (SEBI Check) and investor education (SEBI Saarthi app)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('3aec49b2-3a41-41e4-af76-a9a85c23cd34', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Investor Protection Measures.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Investor Protection Measures"', 'Legal framework: SEBI Act 1992, SCRA 1956, Companies Act 2013, Depositories Act 1996, PFUTP and Insider Trading Regulations, and the Consumer Protection Act.
Regulation of intermediaries: SEBI registration, net-worth norms and codes of conduct for brokers, merchant bankers, DPs and registrars.
Disclosure norms: offer-document vetting, periodic results, corporate governance and LODR.
Market safeguards: margins, circuit breakers (index-based at 10%, 15%, 20% and stock price bands), surveillance (ASM/GSM frameworks) and Settlement Guarantee Fund.
Investor Protection Fund (IPF) at every exchange, which compensates investors in case of member default (limit: up to ₹25 lakh, verify).
IEPF (Investor Education and Protection Fund, Companies Act Sec 125) for unclaimed dividends and shares.
Safe holding and settlement: demat, segregation of client funds and UCC.
Investor education and awareness: SEBI/exchange programmes and the SEBI Investor Charter.
Redressal channels (Card 56).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 55, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c55-q-1", "text": "Investor Protection Measures.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c55-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Legal framework: SEBI Act 1992, SCRA 1956, Companies Act 2013, Depositories Act 1996, PFUTP and Insider Trading Regulations, and the Consumer Protection Act.", "Regulation of intermediaries: SEBI registration, net-worth norms and codes of conduct for brokers, merchant bankers, DPs and registrars.", "Disclosure norms: offer-document vetting, periodic results, corporate governance and LODR.", "Market safeguards: margins, circuit breakers (index-based at 10%, 15%, 20% and stock price bands), surveillance (ASM/GSM frameworks) and Settlement Guarantee Fund.", "Investor Protection Fund (IPF) at every exchange, which compensates investors in case of member default (limit: up to ₹25 lakh, verify).", "IEPF (Investor Education and Protection Fund, Companies Act Sec 125) for unclaimed dividends and shares.", "Safe holding and settlement: demat, segregation of client funds and UCC.", "Investor education and awareness: SEBI/exchange programmes and the SEBI Investor Charter.", "Redressal channels (Card 56)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('4aa431ed-e9a8-4817-b971-dc9ed980d121', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Grievance Redressal Mechanism.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Grievance Redressal Mechanism"', 'Step-by-step escalation ladder:

Approach the intermediary/company first (broker, DP, listed company or RTA), and get a complaint number.
Exchange/depository grievance cell: file with the exchange''s Investor Services Cell or the depository (NSDL/CDSL), against a member or listed company.
SEBI SCORES (SEBI Complaints Redress System; launched 2011, SCORES 2.0 in 2024): a web-based, centralised platform for complaints against listed companies and registered intermediaries. Resolution is time-bound (about 21 days, verify).
Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) (2023): online conciliation, then arbitration.
Arbitration through the exchange (for disputes with brokers) and an appellate arbitration.
Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT) for appeals against SEBI orders, then the Supreme Court.
Other forums: Consumer Commissions, NCLT (company matters) and civil courts.
Compensation: Investor Protection Fund (exchange).

Exam tip: A flow diagram (Investor → Broker/Company → Exchange → SCORES → ODR → SAT) scores well.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 56, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c56-q-1", "text": "Grievance Redressal Mechanism.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c56-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Step-by-step escalation ladder:", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u2-c56-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Approach the intermediary/company first (broker, DP, listed company or RTA), and get a complaint number.", "Exchange/depository grievance cell: file with the exchange''s Investor Services Cell or the depository (NSDL/CDSL), against a member or listed company.", "SEBI SCORES (SEBI Complaints Redress System; launched 2011, SCORES 2.0 in 2024): a web-based, centralised platform for complaints against listed companies and registered intermediaries. Resolution is time-bound (about 21 days, verify).", "Online Dispute Resolution (ODR) (2023): online conciliation, then arbitration.", "Arbitration through the exchange (for disputes with brokers) and an appellate arbitration.", "Securities Appellate Tribunal (SAT) for appeals against SEBI orders, then the Supreme Court.", "Other forums: Consumer Commissions, NCLT (company matters) and civil courts.", "Compensation: Investor Protection Fund (exchange)."]}, {"id": "edm-u2-c56-a-3", "text": "Exam tip: A flow diagram (Investor → Broker/Company → Exchange → SCORES → ODR → SAT) scores well.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('3a441cdb-1d63-4936-9530-e586dfa8335f', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Trading mechanics: quick facts.', 'Priority: Tier 4 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus (supports demat/online trading)', 'Settlement cycle: T+1 (trade day + 1 working day).
Trading hours: pre-open 9:00–9:15; normal 9:15–15:30 (verify).
Order types: Market, limit, stop-loss, IOC, day and after-market orders.
Circuit breakers: market-wide at 10% / 15% / 20% of index movement, and stock-specific price bands (2%, 5%, 10%, 20%).
Clearing: by the clearing corporation acting as the central counterparty (NSE Clearing, ICCL).
Depositories: NSDL and CDSL.
Charges: brokerage, STT, GST, stamp duty, exchange and SEBI fees, DP charges.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 57, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '63666abc-79f8-449c-8f76-d44ee795b369', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c57-q-1", "text": "Trading mechanics: quick facts.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c57-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Settlement cycle: T+1 (trade day + 1 working day).", "Trading hours: pre-open 9:00–9:15; normal 9:15–15:30 (verify).", "Order types: Market, limit, stop-loss, IOC, day and after-market orders.", "Circuit breakers: market-wide at 10% / 15% / 20% of index movement, and stock-specific price bands (2%, 5%, 10%, 20%).", "Clearing: by the clearing corporation acting as the central counterparty (NSE Clearing, ICCL).", "Depositories: NSDL and CDSL.", "Charges: brokerage, STT, GST, stamp duty, exchange and SEBI fees, DP charges."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('4af78440-a831-4cec-984c-d74c29e96f58', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'One-liners and key figures.', 'Priority: Tier 4', 'BSE: 1875. NSE: incorporated 1992, trading 1994. SCRA: 1956. SEBI statutory: 1992. OTCEI: 1992 operations. BOLT: 1995. Demutualisation: 2005. BSE listing: 2017. T+1: 2023.
Sensex: 30 stocks, base 1978–79 = 100. Nifty 50: 50 stocks, base Nov 1995 = 1000.
Cap price ≤ 120% of floor price. IPO open: 3–10 working days. Listing: T+3. Min subscription: 90%. Retail limit: ₹2 lakh. Anchor: ₹10 crore minimum. Green shoe: up to 15%.
Reservation (Reg 6(1)): QIB ≤ 50%, NII ≥ 15%, retail ≥ 35%. Reg 6(2): QIB ≥ 75%.
Sweat equity: 15% per year or ₹5 crore (higher), 25% overall, 3-year lock-in. ESOP min vesting: 1 year.
Full forms: IPO, FPO, OFS, DRHP, RHP, BRLM, QIB, NII, RII, ASBA, GSO, ESOP, DP, RoC, SCORES, ODR, SAT, IPF, IEPF, ICDR, SBEB, LODR, RTA, UCC.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 58, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '807338b8-94b8-4bdc-a611-86f93ab997aa', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c58-q-1", "text": "One-liners and key figures.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c58-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["BSE: 1875. NSE: incorporated 1992, trading 1994. SCRA: 1956. SEBI statutory: 1992. OTCEI: 1992 operations. BOLT: 1995. Demutualisation: 2005. BSE listing: 2017. T+1: 2023.", "Sensex: 30 stocks, base 1978–79 = 100. Nifty 50: 50 stocks, base Nov 1995 = 1000.", "Cap price ≤ 120% of floor price. IPO open: 3–10 working days. Listing: T+3. Min subscription: 90%. Retail limit: ₹2 lakh. Anchor: ₹10 crore minimum. Green shoe: up to 15%.", "Reservation (Reg 6(1)): QIB ≤ 50%, NII ≥ 15%, retail ≥ 35%. Reg 6(2): QIB ≥ 75%.", "Sweat equity: 15% per year or ₹5 crore (higher), 25% overall, 3-year lock-in. ESOP min vesting: 1 year.", "Full forms: IPO, FPO, OFS, DRHP, RHP, BRLM, QIB, NII, RII, ASBA, GSO, ESOP, DP, RoC, SCORES, ODR, SAT, IPF, IEPF, ICDR, SBEB, LODR, RTA, UCC."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('90150432-6f2a-4c8f-adc7-98dcb87cae4f', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Numerical formula sheet (one page).', 'Priority: Tier 4', 'Topic | Formula
Share issue (any) | Capital = shares × FV; Premium = shares × (issue price − FV)
Rights (ratio) | Rights shares = existing shares × ratio
Rights part-subscription | Use subscribed shares only; unsubscribed = no entry
TERP | (N × Pm + n × Ps) ÷ (N + n)
Value of a right | TERP − Ps (per new share); Pm − TERP (per existing share)
ESOP intrinsic value | Market price at grant − Exercise price
ESOP total expense | Options × intrinsic value (spread over vesting period)
ESOP premium | Exercise price + intrinsic value − FV
Sweat equity (non-cash) | Expense = shares × fair value; premium = shares × (fair value − FV)
Sweat equity (discount) | Discount = shares × (fair value − price paid)
Subscription multiple | Shares applied ÷ shares offered
Pro-rata allotment | Applied ÷ multiple; refund = (applied − allotted) × price
Cut-off price | Highest price where cumulative demand ≥ issue size
P/E pricing | EPS = PAT ÷ shares; price = EPS × P/E
Index | (FF market cap ÷ base market cap) × base value', NULL, NULL, NULL, 59, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, 'adc29a92-1496-4a49-9e94-09ce4fd912da', '807338b8-94b8-4bdc-a611-86f93ab997aa', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c59-q-1", "text": "Numerical formula sheet (one page).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u2-c59-a-1", "rows": [["Topic", "Formula"], ["Share issue (any)", "Capital = shares × FV; Premium = shares × (issue price − FV)"], ["Rights (ratio)", "Rights shares = existing shares × ratio"], ["Rights part-subscription", "Use subscribed shares only; unsubscribed = no entry"], ["TERP", "(N × Pm + n × Ps) ÷ (N + n)"], ["Value of a right", "TERP − Ps (per new share); Pm − TERP (per existing share)"], ["ESOP intrinsic value", "Market price at grant − Exercise price"], ["ESOP total expense", "Options × intrinsic value (spread over vesting period)"], ["ESOP premium", "Exercise price + intrinsic value − FV"], ["Sweat equity (non-cash)", "Expense = shares × fair value; premium = shares × (fair value − FV)"], ["Sweat equity (discount)", "Discount = shares × (fair value − price paid)"], ["Subscription multiple", "Shares applied ÷ shares offered"], ["Pro-rata allotment", "Applied ÷ multiple; refund = (applied − allotted) × price"], ["Cut-off price", "Highest price where cumulative demand ≥ issue size"], ["P/E pricing", "EPS = PAT ÷ shares; price = EPS × P/E"], ["Index", "(FF market cap ÷ base market cap) × base value"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:19.465361+00'),
('89be28b3-d7c8-47b9-80be-5fb1c02d1348', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Define Government Securities. Explain their meaning and features.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3Q ("Define Government Securities"); 2025 Q3.1', 'Definition: Government Securities (G-secs) are tradable debt instruments issued by the Central or State Governments to borrow money. They acknowledge the government''s obligation to pay interest and repay the principal. They are also called gilt-edged securities (gilts) because of their safety.
Classification by tenor: Less than 1 year = Treasury Bills / Cash Management Bills (money market). One year or more = dated securities / government bonds (capital market).
Legal basis and manager: Government Securities Act 2006 (in force from 2007) and the RBI Act 1934. The RBI is the debt manager of the Centre and the States. It conducts the auctions and maintains the records.
Features:

Sovereign safety: virtually no default risk.
Fixed income: regular coupon (usually half-yearly) and repayment of face value on maturity.
Held in electronic form: SGL accounts with the RBI, or demat accounts.
Tradable: actively traded on the RBI''s NDS-OM platform and over the counter.
SLR-eligible: banks use them to meet the Statutory Liquidity Ratio.
Issued through auctions (some on tap or for special purposes).
Benchmark: G-sec yields set the base rate for pricing other debt.

Purpose: To finance the fiscal deficit, infrastructure and government expenditure, and to help monetary policy.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c1-q-1", "text": "Define Government Securities. Explain their meaning and features.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c1-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Definition: Government Securities (G-secs) are tradable debt instruments issued by the Central or State Governments to borrow money. They acknowledge the government''s obligation to pay interest and repay the principal. They are also called gilt-edged securities (gilts) because of their safety.", "Classification by tenor: Less than 1 year = Treasury Bills / Cash Management Bills (money market). One year or more = dated securities / government bonds (capital market).", "Legal basis and manager: Government Securities Act 2006 (in force from 2007) and the RBI Act 1934. The RBI is the debt manager of the Centre and the States. It conducts the auctions and maintains the records.", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c1-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Sovereign safety: virtually no default risk.", "Fixed income: regular coupon (usually half-yearly) and repayment of face value on maturity.", "Held in electronic form: SGL accounts with the RBI, or demat accounts.", "Tradable: actively traded on the RBI''s NDS-OM platform and over the counter.", "SLR-eligible: banks use them to meet the Statutory Liquidity Ratio.", "Issued through auctions (some on tap or for special purposes).", "Benchmark: G-sec yields set the base rate for pricing other debt."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c1-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Purpose: To finance the fiscal deficit, infrastructure and government expenditure, and to help monetary policy."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('ed229236-d1be-4a2b-a0c9-ad07fcca6b62', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Illustrate the different types of Government Securities offered by the RBI.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.1 (7.5m); Oct 2024 Q3Q ("Types")', 'G-secs are classified by issuer and tenor.

A. Central Government securities

Type | Tenor | Coupon | Key point
Treasury Bills (T-bills) | 91, 182, 364 days | Zero coupon (issued at a discount, redeemed at par) | Money market instrument
Cash Management Bills (CMBs) | Less than 91 days | Zero coupon | Since 2010, to meet temporary cash-flow mismatches
Dated Securities: Fixed Rate Bonds | Generally 5–40 years | Fixed, paid half-yearly | Most common G-sec
Floating Rate Bonds (FRBs) | Long term | Coupon resets periodically against a benchmark | Protects investors when rates rise
Zero Coupon Bonds | Long term | None (issued at a discount) | Rarely issued now
Capital / Inflation Indexed Bonds | Long term | Linked to an inflation index | Protects real value
Bonds with Call/Put options | Long term | Fixed | Issuer may redeem early (call) or holder may exit early (put)
Special Securities | Long term | Fixed | Issued to entities such as oil-marketing or fertiliser companies in lieu of cash subsidies
STRIPS | Derived | Zero coupon | Coupons and principal of an existing G-sec are separated and traded independently
Sovereign Green Bonds | 5–10 years (2023 issues) | Fixed | Proceeds for green projects

B. State Government securities

State Development Loans (SDLs): dated securities issued by State Governments through RBI auctions. States do not issue T-bills.

C. Retail-oriented issues: Floating Rate Savings Bonds (Taxable) and Sovereign Gold Bonds (issued by the RBI on behalf of the Government).

Exam tip: Draw a tree with Central (short-term, long-term) and State (SDL). Add a one-line description for each type.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 2, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c2-q-1", "text": "Illustrate the different types of Government Securities offered by the RBI.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c2-a-1", "text": "G-secs are classified by issuer and tenor.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c2-a-2", "bold": true, "text": "A. Central Government securities", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c2-a-3", "rows": [["Type", "Tenor", "Coupon", "Key point"], ["Treasury Bills (T-bills)", "91, 182, 364 days", "Zero coupon (issued at a discount, redeemed at par)", "Money market instrument"], ["Cash Management Bills (CMBs)", "Less than 91 days", "Zero coupon", "Since 2010, to meet temporary cash-flow mismatches"], ["Dated Securities: Fixed Rate Bonds", "Generally 5–40 years", "Fixed, paid half-yearly", "Most common G-sec"], ["Floating Rate Bonds (FRBs)", "Long term", "Coupon resets periodically against a benchmark", "Protects investors when rates rise"], ["Zero Coupon Bonds", "Long term", "None (issued at a discount)", "Rarely issued now"], ["Capital / Inflation Indexed Bonds", "Long term", "Linked to an inflation index", "Protects real value"], ["Bonds with Call/Put options", "Long term", "Fixed", "Issuer may redeem early (call) or holder may exit early (put)"], ["Special Securities", "Long term", "Fixed", "Issued to entities such as oil-marketing or fertiliser companies in lieu of cash subsidies"], ["STRIPS", "Derived", "Zero coupon", "Coupons and principal of an existing G-sec are separated and traded independently"], ["Sovereign Green Bonds", "5–10 years (2023 issues)", "Fixed", "Proceeds for green projects"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c2-a-4", "bold": true, "text": "B. State Government securities", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c2-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["State Development Loans (SDLs): dated securities issued by State Governments through RBI auctions. States do not issue T-bills."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c2-a-6", "text": "C. Retail-oriented issues: Floating Rate Savings Bonds (Taxable) and Sovereign Gold Bonds (issued by the RBI on behalf of the Government).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c2-a-7", "text": "Exam tip: Draw a tree with Central (short-term, long-term) and State (SDL). Add a one-line description for each type.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('2894260d-33ee-44af-af1b-5f8f9fea93e7', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain Treasury Bills and Cash Management Bills.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.1 (sub-part)', 'Treasury Bills: Short-term money market instruments issued by the Central Government.
Tenors: 91-day, 182-day and 364-day. The 91-day bill is auctioned weekly and the others on alternate weeks (verify the current calendar).
Zero coupon: issued at a discount to face value and redeemed at par. The investor''s return is the difference.
Minimum bid: ₹25,000 and multiples.
Use: Meets short-term government financing needs, and banks and corporates use them to park surplus funds.
Example: Face value ₹100, issued at ₹98.50 for 91 days. Discount = ₹1.50. Annualised yield ≈ (1.50 ÷ 98.50) × (365 ÷ 91) ≈ 6.11%.
Cash Management Bills: Introduced in 2010. Maturity is less than 91 days. They have the same features as T-bills (discount issue, par redemption), but they are issued only when the Government has a temporary cash-flow mismatch. They are not part of the regular borrowing calendar.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 3, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c3-q-1", "text": "Explain Treasury Bills and Cash Management Bills.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c3-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Treasury Bills: Short-term money market instruments issued by the Central Government.", "Tenors: 91-day, 182-day and 364-day. The 91-day bill is auctioned weekly and the others on alternate weeks (verify the current calendar).", "Zero coupon: issued at a discount to face value and redeemed at par. The investor''s return is the difference.", "Minimum bid: ₹25,000 and multiples.", "Use: Meets short-term government financing needs, and banks and corporates use them to park surplus funds.", "Example: Face value ₹100, issued at ₹98.50 for 91 days. Discount = ₹1.50. Annualised yield ≈ (1.50 ÷ 98.50) × (365 ÷ 91) ≈ 6.11%.", "Cash Management Bills: Introduced in 2010. Maturity is less than 91 days. They have the same features as T-bills (discount issue, par redemption), but they are issued only when the Government has a temporary cash-flow mismatch. They are not part of the regular borrowing calendar."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('5c812ebe-170f-4d25-bfb1-dbba364bf93c', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain Dated Government Securities and their types.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.1 (sub-part)', 'Meaning: Long-term securities (one year or more) issued by the Central Government with a fixed maturity date (hence "dated"). They pay a coupon half-yearly on the face value, and the principal is repaid at maturity. Tenor is usually 5 to 40 years.
Naming: A security is named by its coupon and maturity year, e.g. "7.18% GS 2037" (7.18% coupon, maturing 2037).
Types:

Fixed Rate Bonds: the coupon is fixed for the life of the bond.
Floating Rate Bonds (FRBs): the coupon resets at intervals against a benchmark (for example, the average of recent 182-day T-bill auctions).
Zero Coupon Bonds: no coupon, issued at a discount.
Capital / Inflation Indexed Bonds: principal or interest is adjusted for inflation.
Bonds with Call/Put options: early redemption rights for the issuer or the holder, on interest dates.
Special Securities: issued outside the market to entities, in lieu of cash subsidies.
STRIPS: Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities.
Sovereign Green Bonds: proceeds are tied to green projects.

Minimum bid at auction: ₹10,000 (verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 4, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c4-q-1", "text": "Explain Dated Government Securities and their types.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c4-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Long-term securities (one year or more) issued by the Central Government with a fixed maturity date (hence \"dated\"). They pay a coupon half-yearly on the face value, and the principal is repaid at maturity. Tenor is usually 5 to 40 years.", "Naming: A security is named by its coupon and maturity year, e.g. \"7.18% GS 2037\" (7.18% coupon, maturing 2037).", "Types:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c4-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Fixed Rate Bonds: the coupon is fixed for the life of the bond.", "Floating Rate Bonds (FRBs): the coupon resets at intervals against a benchmark (for example, the average of recent 182-day T-bill auctions).", "Zero Coupon Bonds: no coupon, issued at a discount.", "Capital / Inflation Indexed Bonds: principal or interest is adjusted for inflation.", "Bonds with Call/Put options: early redemption rights for the issuer or the holder, on interest dates.", "Special Securities: issued outside the market to entities, in lieu of cash subsidies.", "STRIPS: Separate Trading of Registered Interest and Principal of Securities.", "Sovereign Green Bonds: proceeds are tied to green projects."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c4-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Minimum bid at auction: ₹10,000 (verify)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('3454d20a-5430-4491-a9b3-9b14fb47a07b', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain State Development Loans, Savings Bonds and Sovereign Gold Bonds.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.1 (types)', 'State Development Loans (SDLs):
Dated securities issued by State Governments to fund their fiscal deficits, through auctions conducted by the RBI.
Interest half-yearly, principal on maturity. They are SLR-eligible and can be used as collateral in repo.
They usually offer a slightly higher yield than Central G-secs, because of lower liquidity and state-specific risk.
States issue only SDLs (no T-bills).
Floating Rate Savings Bonds (Taxable): Offered to individuals and HUFs. 7-year tenor. The rate resets half-yearly against a benchmark (linked to the NSC rate). Not tradable.
Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGBs): Issued by the RBI on behalf of the Government. Denominated in grams of gold, with a fixed annual interest (2.5%) and redemption at the prevailing gold price. Issuance has been stopped or reduced recently (verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 5, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c5-q-1", "text": "Explain State Development Loans, Savings Bonds and Sovereign Gold Bonds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c5-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["State Development Loans (SDLs):", "Dated securities issued by State Governments to fund their fiscal deficits, through auctions conducted by the RBI.", "Interest half-yearly, principal on maturity. They are SLR-eligible and can be used as collateral in repo.", "They usually offer a slightly higher yield than Central G-secs, because of lower liquidity and state-specific risk.", "States issue only SDLs (no T-bills).", "Floating Rate Savings Bonds (Taxable): Offered to individuals and HUFs. 7-year tenor. The rate resets half-yearly against a benchmark (linked to the NSC rate). Not tradable.", "Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGBs): Issued by the RBI on behalf of the Government. Denominated in grams of gold, with a fixed annual interest (2.5%) and redemption at the prevailing gold price. Issuance has been stopped or reduced recently (verify)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('f1a16222-9bb9-4908-96f9-47a75cdaf9af', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'How are Government Securities issued and traded? (working)', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3Q; supports 2025 Q3.1', 'Borrowing calendar: The Government announces its borrowing programme (half-yearly), and the RBI announces each auction (security, notified amount, method).
Auction methods: Yield-based or price-based, with uniform-price or multiple-price allotment.
Bidding: Members (banks, Primary Dealers, insurers, provident funds) bid electronically on the RBI''s e-Kuber platform. Others bid through banks or PDs.
Non-competitive bidding: A window lets small investors bid without quoting a price (allotted at the weighted average cut-off).
Cut-off and allotment: The RBI decides the cut-off yield/price and allots securities.
Settlement: Securities are credited to the investor''s SGL/CSGL or demat account (T+1 after the auction).
Secondary market: Trading on NDS-OM (Negotiated Dealing System – Order Matching) and OTC. CCIL clears and settles the trades.
Retail access: RBI Retail Direct (2021) lets individuals open a Gilt Account with the RBI. The stock exchanges also offer G-sec platforms.
Income and redemption: Half-yearly coupon, and redemption at par on maturity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 6, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c6-q-1", "text": "How are Government Securities issued and traded? (working)", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c6-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Borrowing calendar: The Government announces its borrowing programme (half-yearly), and the RBI announces each auction (security, notified amount, method).", "Auction methods: Yield-based or price-based, with uniform-price or multiple-price allotment.", "Bidding: Members (banks, Primary Dealers, insurers, provident funds) bid electronically on the RBI''s e-Kuber platform. Others bid through banks or PDs.", "Non-competitive bidding: A window lets small investors bid without quoting a price (allotted at the weighted average cut-off).", "Cut-off and allotment: The RBI decides the cut-off yield/price and allots securities.", "Settlement: Securities are credited to the investor''s SGL/CSGL or demat account (T+1 after the auction).", "Secondary market: Trading on NDS-OM (Negotiated Dealing System – Order Matching) and OTC. CCIL clears and settles the trades.", "Retail access: RBI Retail Direct (2021) lets individuals open a Gilt Account with the RBI. The stock exchanges also offer G-sec platforms.", "Income and redemption: Half-yearly coupon, and redemption at par on maturity."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('df25ff21-a107-4a4e-b9a4-5721438c19ea', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the Merits and Demerits of Government Securities.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3Q ("Merits, Demerits")', 'Merits | Demerits
Safest: sovereign backing, no default risk | Lower return than corporate bonds and equity
Assured income: fixed coupon and principal | Interest rate risk: prices fall when rates rise
Liquidity: active secondary market, and can be used in repo | Inflation risk: fixed returns lose purchasing power
SLR-eligible for banks | Reinvestment risk: coupons may be reinvested at lower rates
Benchmark for pricing other securities | Interest is taxable (no tax benefits on most G-secs)
Portfolio diversification and a stable core holding | Long lock-in in long-dated securities if held to maturity
Wide range of tenors (91 days to 40 years) | Thin trading in less liquid securities
Easy access through RBI Retail Direct | No capital appreciation unless yields fall
Zero risk weight for banks (capital-efficient) | Retail participation is still low', NULL, NULL, NULL, 7, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c7-q-1", "text": "Discuss the Merits and Demerits of Government Securities.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c7-a-1", "rows": [["Merits", "Demerits"], ["Safest: sovereign backing, no default risk", "Lower return than corporate bonds and equity"], ["Assured income: fixed coupon and principal", "Interest rate risk: prices fall when rates rise"], ["Liquidity: active secondary market, and can be used in repo", "Inflation risk: fixed returns lose purchasing power"], ["SLR-eligible for banks", "Reinvestment risk: coupons may be reinvested at lower rates"], ["Benchmark for pricing other securities", "Interest is taxable (no tax benefits on most G-secs)"], ["Portfolio diversification and a stable core holding", "Long lock-in in long-dated securities if held to maturity"], ["Wide range of tenors (91 days to 40 years)", "Thin trading in less liquid securities"], ["Easy access through RBI Retail Direct", "No capital appreciation unless yields fall"], ["Zero risk weight for banks (capital-efficient)", "Retail participation is still low"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('155cb15a-bff2-4532-a28d-1943d729a8ba', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the factors affecting Government Securities investment decisions.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3Q ("Factors affecting Government Securities Decisions")', 'A. Macroeconomic and market factors

Interest rates and monetary policy: Expected repo rate changes move yields. Bond prices and yields move in opposite directions.
Inflation: High inflation erodes fixed returns and pushes yields up.
Fiscal deficit and government borrowing: More supply of G-secs pushes yields up.
Liquidity conditions: OMOs, CRR/SLR changes and system liquidity.
Global factors: US Treasury yields, foreign portfolio flows and index inclusion.
Economic growth and currency outlook.

B. Security-specific factors

Coupon and yield compared with alternatives.
Maturity/tenor and duration (longer bonds are more price-sensitive).
Liquidity of the particular security.
SLR-eligibility and tax treatment.

C. Investor-specific factors

Investment objective and risk appetite (safety vs return).
Investment horizon and asset-liability matching (insurers, pension funds).
Regulatory requirements (SLR, IRDAI/PFRDA norms).
Availability of alternatives (corporate bonds, deposits, equity).

Decision rule: If interest rates are expected to fall, investors prefer long-dated bonds (prices rise). If rates are expected to rise, they prefer short-dated securities or floating rate bonds.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 8, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c8-q-1", "text": "Explain the factors affecting Government Securities investment decisions.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c8-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "A. Macroeconomic and market factors", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c8-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Interest rates and monetary policy: Expected repo rate changes move yields. Bond prices and yields move in opposite directions.", "Inflation: High inflation erodes fixed returns and pushes yields up.", "Fiscal deficit and government borrowing: More supply of G-secs pushes yields up.", "Liquidity conditions: OMOs, CRR/SLR changes and system liquidity.", "Global factors: US Treasury yields, foreign portfolio flows and index inclusion.", "Economic growth and currency outlook."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c8-a-3", "bold": true, "text": "B. Security-specific factors", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c8-a-4", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Coupon and yield compared with alternatives.", "Maturity/tenor and duration (longer bonds are more price-sensitive).", "Liquidity of the particular security.", "SLR-eligibility and tax treatment."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c8-a-5", "bold": true, "text": "C. Investor-specific factors", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c8-a-6", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Investment objective and risk appetite (safety vs return).", "Investment horizon and asset-liability matching (insurers, pension funds).", "Regulatory requirements (SLR, IRDAI/PFRDA norms).", "Availability of alternatives (corporate bonds, deposits, equity)."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c8-a-7", "text": "Decision rule: If interest rates are expected to fall, investors prefer long-dated bonds (prices rise). If rates are expected to rise, they prefer short-dated securities or floating rate bonds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('a797d760-0f6b-4f91-b6e4-61f203b4a29e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between Treasury Bills and Dated Government Securities.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Supports 2025 Q3.1', 'Basis | Treasury Bills | Dated Government Securities
Maturity | Up to 1 year (91/182/364 days) | 1 year and above (5–40 years typical)
Market | Money market | Capital / debt market
Interest | No coupon; issued at a discount | Periodic (half-yearly) coupon
Return source | Discount to face value | Coupon plus any price change
Issued by | Central Government only | Central and State Governments (SDLs)
Price risk | Very low | Higher (longer duration)
Minimum bid | ₹25,000 | ₹10,000 (verify)
Use | Short-term financing, liquidity management | Long-term financing of the deficit', NULL, NULL, NULL, 9, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '3c5dfa5e-b596-4d2d-a881-ab9be6e60104', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c9-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between Treasury Bills and Dated Government Securities.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c9-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Treasury Bills", "Dated Government Securities"], ["Maturity", "Up to 1 year (91/182/364 days)", "1 year and above (5–40 years typical)"], ["Market", "Money market", "Capital / debt market"], ["Interest", "No coupon; issued at a discount", "Periodic (half-yearly) coupon"], ["Return source", "Discount to face value", "Coupon plus any price change"], ["Issued by", "Central Government only", "Central and State Governments (SDLs)"], ["Price risk", "Very low", "Higher (longer duration)"], ["Minimum bid", "₹25,000", "₹10,000 (verify)"], ["Use", "Short-term financing, liquidity management", "Long-term financing of the deficit"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('a81e91b5-950e-4583-86c1-26e5b4d0a3fa', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Discuss the importance and limitations of the Corporate Bond Market in India.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3P (Corporate Bonds); 2025 Q3.2', 'Importance:

Alternative to bank credit for long-term project funding.
Matches long-term funds to long-term needs (avoids asset-liability mismatch in banks).
Cheaper than equity for the issuer, and interest is tax-deductible.
Diversified investor base (insurers, pension funds, mutual funds).
Investors earn higher yields than G-secs.
Improves price discovery and risk pricing through ratings.
Supports infrastructure and financial stability by reducing bank dependence.

Limitations in India:

Private placements dominate, and public issues are few.
Shallow secondary market and low liquidity.
Concentration in AAA/AA issuers. Lower-rated issuers find it hard to raise funds.
Low retail participation.
Default and rating events (for example, IL&FS in 2018) hurt confidence.

Reforms (verify): SEBI''s EBP and RFQ platforms, reduced face value for privately placed bonds, online bond platform regulation, and the Corporate Debt Market Development Fund.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 14, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '90cac77e-9df9-475c-bba8-eec925fde44c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c14-q-1", "text": "Discuss the importance and limitations of the Corporate Bond Market in India.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c14-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Importance:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c14-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Alternative to bank credit for long-term project funding.", "Matches long-term funds to long-term needs (avoids asset-liability mismatch in banks).", "Cheaper than equity for the issuer, and interest is tax-deductible.", "Diversified investor base (insurers, pension funds, mutual funds).", "Investors earn higher yields than G-secs.", "Improves price discovery and risk pricing through ratings.", "Supports infrastructure and financial stability by reducing bank dependence."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c14-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Limitations in India:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c14-a-4", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Private placements dominate, and public issues are few.", "Shallow secondary market and low liquidity.", "Concentration in AAA/AA issuers. Lower-rated issuers find it hard to raise funds.", "Low retail participation.", "Default and rating events (for example, IL&FS in 2018) hurt confidence."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c14-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Reforms (verify): SEBI''s EBP and RFQ platforms, reduced face value for privately placed bonds, online bond platform regulation, and the Corporate Debt Market Development Fund."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('9fcb7bac-b44f-4a8b-955d-57740c17f6af', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain Public Sector Bonds (PSU Bonds): meaning, features and types.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Public Sector Bonds"; not asked in these two papers', 'Meaning: Bonds issued by government-owned enterprises (PSUs), such as NHAI, REC, PFC, IRFC, NABARD, HUDCO, NTPC and Power Grid, and public sector banks. They are not government securities: the bond is the PSU''s own liability, not the Government''s.
Features:

Government ownership gives investors comfort, so most are highly rated (often AAA).
Yield is slightly above G-secs and below private corporates of similar tenor.
Issued through public issues or private placements.
Mostly used to fund infrastructure, power, railways and housing.
Listed and traded on the exchanges and OTC.
Regulated by SEBI (listing and disclosure), the Companies Act, and the RBI (for bank-issued bonds).

Types:
Taxable bonds.
Tax-free bonds: interest exempt under the Income Tax Act. They were issued mainly in 2011–2016 (verify).
Capital gains bonds (Section 54EC): issued by NHAI, REC, PFC and IRFC.
Infrastructure bonds and bank capital bonds (AT1, Tier 2) issued by PSU banks.
Government-guaranteed and non-guaranteed bonds.
Importance: They finance national infrastructure, and act as a benchmark for corporate bonds (the "PSU spread").', NULL, NULL, NULL, 10, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '90cac77e-9df9-475c-bba8-eec925fde44c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c10-q-1", "text": "Explain Public Sector Bonds (PSU Bonds): meaning, features and types.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c10-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Bonds issued by government-owned enterprises (PSUs), such as NHAI, REC, PFC, IRFC, NABARD, HUDCO, NTPC and Power Grid, and public sector banks. They are not government securities: the bond is the PSU''s own liability, not the Government''s.", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c10-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Government ownership gives investors comfort, so most are highly rated (often AAA).", "Yield is slightly above G-secs and below private corporates of similar tenor.", "Issued through public issues or private placements.", "Mostly used to fund infrastructure, power, railways and housing.", "Listed and traded on the exchanges and OTC.", "Regulated by SEBI (listing and disclosure), the Companies Act, and the RBI (for bank-issued bonds)."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c10-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Types:", "Taxable bonds.", "Tax-free bonds: interest exempt under the Income Tax Act. They were issued mainly in 2011–2016 (verify).", "Capital gains bonds (Section 54EC): issued by NHAI, REC, PFC and IRFC.", "Infrastructure bonds and bank capital bonds (AT1, Tier 2) issued by PSU banks.", "Government-guaranteed and non-guaranteed bonds.", "Importance: They finance national infrastructure, and act as a benchmark for corporate bonds (the \"PSU spread\")."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('edc6ddb8-db92-4e98-a5d7-4658bfa49abc', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain Corporate Bonds: meaning and features.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3P (Corporate Bonds); 2025 Q3.2', 'Meaning: Corporate bonds are debt securities issued by companies (private or public, NBFCs, banks and financial institutions) to raise long-term funds. The investor lends money and receives interest and principal, and does not become an owner. Under the Companies Act, bonds issued by companies are also referred to as debentures.
Features:

Fixed or floating coupon, higher than G-secs to compensate for credit risk (credit spread).
Fixed maturity, generally 1–10 years or more.
Credit rated by a SEBI-registered agency, so the rating drives the price.
Secured or unsecured. Secured bonds have a charge on the issuer''s assets.
Priority over shareholders in repayment.
Listed or unlisted; mostly traded over the counter.
Issued through public issue or private placement (private placement dominates in India).
Debenture trustee protects investors'' interest.
Optionality is possible: convertibility, call and put options.
Interest is tax-deductible for the issuer.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 11, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '90cac77e-9df9-475c-bba8-eec925fde44c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c11-q-1", "text": "Explain Corporate Bonds: meaning and features.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c11-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Corporate bonds are debt securities issued by companies (private or public, NBFCs, banks and financial institutions) to raise long-term funds. The investor lends money and receives interest and principal, and does not become an owner. Under the Companies Act, bonds issued by companies are also referred to as debentures.", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c11-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Fixed or floating coupon, higher than G-secs to compensate for credit risk (credit spread).", "Fixed maturity, generally 1–10 years or more.", "Credit rated by a SEBI-registered agency, so the rating drives the price.", "Secured or unsecured. Secured bonds have a charge on the issuer''s assets.", "Priority over shareholders in repayment.", "Listed or unlisted; mostly traded over the counter.", "Issued through public issue or private placement (private placement dominates in India).", "Debenture trustee protects investors'' interest.", "Optionality is possible: convertibility, call and put options.", "Interest is tax-deductible for the issuer."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('4b65a7da-6731-406d-a5c5-33cd1b817324', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Types of Corporate Bonds.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.2 (7.5m, "types and working")', 'Basis | Types | Explanation
Security | Secured / Unsecured | Secured bonds are backed by a charge on assets. Unsecured bonds rely on the issuer''s credit
Convertibility | Convertible (fully / partly) / Non-Convertible (NCD) | Convertible bonds can turn into equity shares. NCDs cannot
Coupon | Fixed / Floating / Zero-coupon / Deep-discount / Step-up | Interest fixed, linked to a benchmark, or absent (issued at a discount)
Redemption | Redeemable (bullet or amortising) / Callable / Putable / Perpetual | Callable: issuer may redeem early. Putable: investor may exit early. Perpetual: no maturity (e.g. bank AT1 bonds)
Seniority | Senior / Subordinated (Tier 2) / Junior | Ranking in the event of liquidation
Purpose / theme | Infrastructure bonds, Green, Social, Sustainability, Sustainability-linked bonds | Proceeds tied to a defined use
Currency / market | Rupee bonds / Foreign-currency bonds / Masala bonds | Masala bonds are rupee-denominated bonds issued overseas
Structured | Securitised debt (PTCs) | Backed by a pool of receivables
Short-term corporate debt | Commercial Paper (CP) | Money-market instrument (up to 1 year), not a bond

Exam tip: A classification table with 5–6 bases and one line for each type is enough for 7.5 marks.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 12, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '90cac77e-9df9-475c-bba8-eec925fde44c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c12-q-1", "text": "Explain the Types of Corporate Bonds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c12-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Types", "Explanation"], ["Security", "Secured / Unsecured", "Secured bonds are backed by a charge on assets. Unsecured bonds rely on the issuer''s credit"], ["Convertibility", "Convertible (fully / partly) / Non-Convertible (NCD)", "Convertible bonds can turn into equity shares. NCDs cannot"], ["Coupon", "Fixed / Floating / Zero-coupon / Deep-discount / Step-up", "Interest fixed, linked to a benchmark, or absent (issued at a discount)"], ["Redemption", "Redeemable (bullet or amortising) / Callable / Putable / Perpetual", "Callable: issuer may redeem early. Putable: investor may exit early. Perpetual: no maturity (e.g. bank AT1 bonds)"], ["Seniority", "Senior / Subordinated (Tier 2) / Junior", "Ranking in the event of liquidation"], ["Purpose / theme", "Infrastructure bonds, Green, Social, Sustainability, Sustainability-linked bonds", "Proceeds tied to a defined use"], ["Currency / market", "Rupee bonds / Foreign-currency bonds / Masala bonds", "Masala bonds are rupee-denominated bonds issued overseas"], ["Structured", "Securitised debt (PTCs)", "Backed by a pool of receivables"], ["Short-term corporate debt", "Commercial Paper (CP)", "Money-market instrument (up to 1 year), not a bond"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c12-a-2", "text": "Exam tip: A classification table with 5–6 bases and one line for each type is enough for 7.5 marks.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('e48a284f-c2a1-4d90-8a09-473c68de7c7c', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the working of Corporate Bonds (issue to redemption).', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.2 ("working"); Oct 2024 Q3P', 'Decision: The Board decides to raise debt. Shareholder approval is needed for limits under the Companies Act.
Structuring: Choose the type (secured/unsecured, tenor, coupon, put/call options).
Credit rating: The issuer obtains a rating from a SEBI-registered agency (at least investment grade for a public issue).
Appointments: Merchant banker/arranger, debenture trustee, registrar, legal counsel and auditors.
Mode of issue:

Public issue: offer document filed under the SEBI NCS Regulations 2021, open to all investors.
Private placement: offered to a limited number of institutions and HNIs. Above the threshold (₹20 crore, verify) it is done on an Electronic Book Provider (EBP) platform.

Pricing: Coupon = benchmark G-sec yield + credit spread (based on rating and market conditions).

Illustration (hypothetical): 5-year G-sec yield 6.9% + spread 2.1% = 9.0% coupon.

Allotment and listing: Bonds are allotted in demat form and are listed on the exchange debt segment.
Servicing: Interest is paid on the due dates through the paying agent. The trustee monitors covenants and security.
Secondary trading: Mostly OTC and on exchange/RFQ platforms. Trades are reported and settled through the clearing corporations.
Redemption: The principal is repaid at maturity (or earlier for callable/putable bonds). In case of default, the trustee enforces the security.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 13, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '90cac77e-9df9-475c-bba8-eec925fde44c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c13-q-1", "text": "Explain the working of Corporate Bonds (issue to redemption).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c13-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Decision: The Board decides to raise debt. Shareholder approval is needed for limits under the Companies Act.", "Structuring: Choose the type (secured/unsecured, tenor, coupon, put/call options).", "Credit rating: The issuer obtains a rating from a SEBI-registered agency (at least investment grade for a public issue).", "Appointments: Merchant banker/arranger, debenture trustee, registrar, legal counsel and auditors.", "Mode of issue:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c13-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Public issue: offer document filed under the SEBI NCS Regulations 2021, open to all investors.", "Private placement: offered to a limited number of institutions and HNIs. Above the threshold (₹20 crore, verify) it is done on an Electronic Book Provider (EBP) platform."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c13-a-3", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Pricing: Coupon = benchmark G-sec yield + credit spread (based on rating and market conditions)."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c13-a-4", "text": "Illustration (hypothetical): 5-year G-sec yield 6.9% + spread 2.1% = 9.0% coupon.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c13-a-5", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Allotment and listing: Bonds are allotted in demat form and are listed on the exchange debt segment.", "Servicing: Interest is paid on the due dates through the paying agent. The trustee monitors covenants and security.", "Secondary trading: Mostly OTC and on exchange/RFQ platforms. Trades are reported and settled through the clearing corporations.", "Redemption: The principal is repaid at maturity (or earlier for callable/putable bonds). In case of default, the trustee enforces the security."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('b4bff54a-daa1-40d0-b0ad-fc0b45b21af8', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the role of Insurance Companies in the Debt Market.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Regulation: IRDAI (Investment) Regulations prescribe minimum holdings of G-secs and approved investments, and limits on lower-rated papers (verify current percentages).
Role:

Long-term investors: They have long-dated liabilities (life policies), so they buy long-tenor G-secs, SDLs and bonds and hold to maturity.
Asset-liability matching drives their demand at the long end of the yield curve.
Stable, buy-and-hold demand reduces market volatility.
Finance infrastructure through PSU and infrastructure bonds.
Credit quality preference: mostly AAA/AA-rated bonds.
Life Insurance Corporation (LIC) is among the largest institutional investors in India''s debt market.

General insurers: Shorter horizons, so they hold more short and medium-dated securities.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 30, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '66f8af16-45e3-4cfa-ba06-990bbe5b0c55', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c30-q-1", "text": "Explain the role of Insurance Companies in the Debt Market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c30-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Regulation: IRDAI (Investment) Regulations prescribe minimum holdings of G-secs and approved investments, and limits on lower-rated papers (verify current percentages).", "Role:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c30-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Long-term investors: They have long-dated liabilities (life policies), so they buy long-tenor G-secs, SDLs and bonds and hold to maturity.", "Asset-liability matching drives their demand at the long end of the yield curve.", "Stable, buy-and-hold demand reduces market volatility.", "Finance infrastructure through PSU and infrastructure bonds.", "Credit quality preference: mostly AAA/AA-rated bonds.", "Life Insurance Corporation (LIC) is among the largest institutional investors in India''s debt market."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c30-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["General insurers: Shorter horizons, so they hold more short and medium-dated securities."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('ebb80c16-c63c-4bce-bb81-ffea340cb932', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Regulatory framework for Corporate Bonds (short note).', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Supports Card 13', 'Companies Act 2013: debentures (Sec 71), private placement (Sec 42), definition of debenture (Sec 2(30)).
SEBI (Issue and Listing of Non-Convertible Securities) Regulations 2021: public issue, private placement and listing of debt.
SEBI LODR Regulations: ongoing disclosures. SEBI Debenture Trustees and CRA Regulations.
RBI: banks'' investment norms, FPI limits and rules for bank-issued bonds.
Market infrastructure: exchange debt segments, EBP and RFQ platforms, clearing corporations, depositories.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 15, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '90cac77e-9df9-475c-bba8-eec925fde44c', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c15-q-1", "text": "Regulatory framework for Corporate Bonds (short note).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c15-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Companies Act 2013: debentures (Sec 71), private placement (Sec 42), definition of debenture (Sec 2(30)).", "SEBI (Issue and Listing of Non-Convertible Securities) Regulations 2021: public issue, private placement and listing of debt.", "SEBI LODR Regulations: ongoing disclosures. SEBI Debenture Trustees and CRA Regulations.", "RBI: banks'' investment norms, FPI limits and rules for bank-issued bonds.", "Market infrastructure: exchange debt segments, EBP and RFQ platforms, clearing corporations, depositories."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('2f8b0c63-bd48-4543-8bab-da422207a9e3', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'What are Open Market Operations (OMO)? Explain their meaning and purpose.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3A (10m, with Primary Dealers)', 'Meaning: OMO means the purchase and sale of government securities by the RBI in the open market to regulate liquidity, money supply and interest rates. OMO is a quantitative tool of monetary policy.
Purposes:

Manage durable (long-term) liquidity in the banking system.
Control money supply and inflation.
Influence yields and interest rates (stabilise or shape the yield curve).
Support the government''s borrowing programme by keeping the market orderly.
Sterilise foreign-exchange intervention. When the RBI buys dollars it injects rupees, which OMO sales can absorb.
Signal the monetary policy stance to the market.
Complement CRR, SLR and the repo rate.

Role in the debt market: OMO creates a large and regular demand/supply of G-secs, improves price discovery, deepens liquidity and links monetary policy to bond yields.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 16, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', 'fc5cbdc2-02a8-4974-8356-ddc7d4147a34', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c16-q-1", "text": "What are Open Market Operations (OMO)? Explain their meaning and purpose.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c16-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: OMO means the purchase and sale of government securities by the RBI in the open market to regulate liquidity, money supply and interest rates. OMO is a quantitative tool of monetary policy.", "Purposes:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c16-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Manage durable (long-term) liquidity in the banking system.", "Control money supply and inflation.", "Influence yields and interest rates (stabilise or shape the yield curve).", "Support the government''s borrowing programme by keeping the market orderly.", "Sterilise foreign-exchange intervention. When the RBI buys dollars it injects rupees, which OMO sales can absorb.", "Signal the monetary policy stance to the market.", "Complement CRR, SLR and the repo rate."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c16-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Role in the debt market: OMO creates a large and regular demand/supply of G-secs, improves price discovery, deepens liquidity and links monetary policy to bond yields."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('5b8e0720-3814-4d64-b252-6bd95e2b50b8', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the process and types of Open Market Operations.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3A', 'Types

Outright OMO purchase: the RBI buys G-secs and injects liquidity.
Outright OMO sale: the RBI sells G-secs and absorbs liquidity.
Simultaneous purchase and sale ("Operation Twist"): buys long-dated and sells short-dated securities, to flatten the yield curve without changing liquidity.
OMO in State Development Loans (since 2020).

Process (numbered)

The RBI reviews liquidity and decides to inject or absorb.
It announces the auction: securities, notified amount, date and method (usually a multi-security, multiple-price auction).
Banks, Primary Dealers and other eligible participants submit offers (to sell) or bids (to buy) electronically.
The RBI accepts or rejects offers at its discretion and finalises cut-off prices.
Settlement: securities and funds are exchanged through the RBI''s accounts and CCIL.
The RBI''s holdings and system liquidity change.

Effects

Purchase: RBI pays banks, so bank reserves and liquidity rise. Yields fall (prices rise), and lending rates ease.
Sale: liquidity is withdrawn. Yields rise (prices fall), and credit becomes costlier.

Illustration: In Dec 2025–Jan 2026 the RBI announced OMO purchases of ₹2 lakh crore in four tranches of ₹50,000 crore each, to counter a liquidity deficit (verify).

Limitations: Depends on the RBI''s stock of securities and market depth. Its effect on yields can be temporary. It can conflict with the government''s borrowing needs.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 17, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', 'fc5cbdc2-02a8-4974-8356-ddc7d4147a34', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c17-q-1", "text": "Explain the process and types of Open Market Operations.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c17-a-1", "bold": true, "text": "Types", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c17-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Outright OMO purchase: the RBI buys G-secs and injects liquidity.", "Outright OMO sale: the RBI sells G-secs and absorbs liquidity.", "Simultaneous purchase and sale (\"Operation Twist\"): buys long-dated and sells short-dated securities, to flatten the yield curve without changing liquidity.", "OMO in State Development Loans (since 2020)."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c17-a-3", "bold": true, "text": "Process (numbered)", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c17-a-4", "type": "numberList", "items": ["The RBI reviews liquidity and decides to inject or absorb.", "It announces the auction: securities, notified amount, date and method (usually a multi-security, multiple-price auction).", "Banks, Primary Dealers and other eligible participants submit offers (to sell) or bids (to buy) electronically.", "The RBI accepts or rejects offers at its discretion and finalises cut-off prices.", "Settlement: securities and funds are exchanged through the RBI''s accounts and CCIL.", "The RBI''s holdings and system liquidity change."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c17-a-5", "bold": true, "text": "Effects", "type": "text", "style": "heading"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c17-a-6", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Purchase: RBI pays banks, so bank reserves and liquidity rise. Yields fall (prices rise), and lending rates ease.", "Sale: liquidity is withdrawn. Yields rise (prices fall), and credit becomes costlier."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c17-a-7", "text": "Illustration: In Dec 2025–Jan 2026 the RBI announced OMO purchases of ₹2 lakh crore in four tranches of ₹50,000 crore each, to counter a liquidity deficit (verify).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c17-a-8", "text": "Limitations: Depends on the RBI''s stock of securities and market depth. Its effect on yields can be temporary. It can conflict with the government''s borrowing needs.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('22751bd9-aba4-4c1e-81d8-91b1181a8819', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between OMO and LAF.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Supports Oct 2024 Q3A', 'Basis | Open Market Operations | Liquidity Adjustment Facility (LAF)
Nature | Outright purchase/sale of G-secs (permanent) | Repo/reverse repo (temporary, collateralised)
Liquidity type | Durable / structural | Short-term / transient
Initiative | RBI announces auctions | Banks approach the RBI''s window
Tenor | Permanent until maturity or resale | Overnight to a few weeks
Price | Market yield discovered in the auction | Policy rate (repo, and SDF as the floor)
Use | Manage durable liquidity and yields | Day-to-day liquidity adjustment', NULL, NULL, NULL, 18, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', 'fc5cbdc2-02a8-4974-8356-ddc7d4147a34', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c18-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between OMO and LAF.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c18-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Open Market Operations", "Liquidity Adjustment Facility (LAF)"], ["Nature", "Outright purchase/sale of G-secs (permanent)", "Repo/reverse repo (temporary, collateralised)"], ["Liquidity type", "Durable / structural", "Short-term / transient"], ["Initiative", "RBI announces auctions", "Banks approach the RBI''s window"], ["Tenor", "Permanent until maturity or resale", "Overnight to a few weeks"], ["Price", "Market yield discovered in the auction", "Policy rate (repo, and SDF as the floor)"], ["Use", "Manage durable liquidity and yields", "Day-to-day liquidity adjustment"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('5e23d4a7-75b2-4f63-829f-a16f4980a69b', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the role of Mutual Funds in the Debt Market.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Regulation: SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations 1996.
Role:

Pool retail and HNI savings and invest them in debt securities, offering diversification and professional management.
Major buyers of corporate bonds, CPs, CDs, T-bills and G-secs.
Provide liquidity in the corporate bond market, especially for AAA and AA papers.
Improve price discovery through frequent trading and valuation.
Provide investors a route to the bond market with small amounts (SIPs).

Debt fund categories (examples): Liquid, Money market, Short duration, Corporate bond funds (mostly high-rated bonds), Gilt funds (mostly G-secs), Dynamic bond, Credit risk and Banking & PSU funds.
Safeguards: Exposure limits, rating norms, standardised valuation by approved valuation agencies, and risk-o-meter disclosure.
Lesson on credit risk: The closure of six debt schemes by Franklin Templeton in April 2020 showed the importance of credit and liquidity risk.
Taxation: Since April 2023, gains on specified debt funds are taxed at the investor''s slab rate (verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 29, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '66f8af16-45e3-4cfa-ba06-990bbe5b0c55', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c29-q-1", "text": "Explain the role of Mutual Funds in the Debt Market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c29-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Regulation: SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations 1996.", "Role:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c29-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Pool retail and HNI savings and invest them in debt securities, offering diversification and professional management.", "Major buyers of corporate bonds, CPs, CDs, T-bills and G-secs.", "Provide liquidity in the corporate bond market, especially for AAA and AA papers.", "Improve price discovery through frequent trading and valuation.", "Provide investors a route to the bond market with small amounts (SIPs)."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c29-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Debt fund categories (examples): Liquid, Money market, Short duration, Corporate bond funds (mostly high-rated bonds), Gilt funds (mostly G-secs), Dynamic bond, Credit risk and Banking & PSU funds.", "Safeguards: Exposure limits, rating norms, standardised valuation by approved valuation agencies, and risk-o-meter disclosure.", "Lesson on credit risk: The closure of six debt schemes by Franklin Templeton in April 2020 showed the importance of credit and liquidity risk.", "Taxation: Since April 2023, gains on specified debt funds are taxed at the investor''s slab rate (verify)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('2eb8ecad-41ea-44bd-b37e-dd8c9ea1d0a4', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain STCI (Securities Trading Corporation of India): concept, evolution and role.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3P (10m, with Corporate Bonds); 2025 Q3.3', 'Concept: STCI was set up in May 1994 as a company promoted by the RBI with the objective of fostering an active secondary market in Government of India securities and public sector bonds. It began as a subsidiary of the RBI (the RBI held a majority stake, which was later divested).
Evolution:
1994: started operations as a market maker in G-secs and PSU bonds.
1996: accredited by the RBI as one of the first Primary Dealers in India.
June 2007: the primary dealership business was hived off into a wholly owned subsidiary, STCI Primary Dealer Ltd, in line with RBI guidelines on diversification by PDs.
24 Oct 2011: renamed STCI Finance Limited, to reflect its lending and financing business.
Role in the debt market (original mandate): market making, providing liquidity, trading in G-secs, T-bills, PSU bonds and money market instruments, and supporting the RBI''s efforts to develop the secondary market.
Today: STCI Finance is a systemically important non-deposit taking NBFC, with two subsidiaries: STCI Primary Dealer Ltd and STCI Commodities Ltd (Card 23).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 19, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '7a682bcc-5792-4d05-ae77-7e8b6c760085', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c19-q-1", "text": "Explain STCI (Securities Trading Corporation of India): concept, evolution and role.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c19-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Concept: STCI was set up in May 1994 as a company promoted by the RBI with the objective of fostering an active secondary market in Government of India securities and public sector bonds. It began as a subsidiary of the RBI (the RBI held a majority stake, which was later divested).", "Evolution:", "1994: started operations as a market maker in G-secs and PSU bonds.", "1996: accredited by the RBI as one of the first Primary Dealers in India.", "June 2007: the primary dealership business was hived off into a wholly owned subsidiary, STCI Primary Dealer Ltd, in line with RBI guidelines on diversification by PDs.", "24 Oct 2011: renamed STCI Finance Limited, to reflect its lending and financing business.", "Role in the debt market (original mandate): market making, providing liquidity, trading in G-secs, T-bills, PSU bonds and money market instruments, and supporting the RBI''s efforts to develop the secondary market.", "Today: STCI Finance is a systemically important non-deposit taking NBFC, with two subsidiaries: STCI Primary Dealer Ltd and STCI Commodities Ltd (Card 23)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('9aef34e4-d263-43c2-8991-90667fee3071', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain STCI Finance Limited.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.3 (one of the three sub-parts)', 'Status: The parent company of the group and the successor to the original STCI. It is a systemically important non-deposit taking NBFC (NBFC-ND-SI) registered with the RBI, classified as a loan NBFC.
History: Formerly Securities Trading Corporation of India Ltd. Renamed STCI Finance Ltd with effect from 24 October 2011.
Business: Since 2007 it has focused on lending and investment activities:

Loan against securities / promoter funding.
Corporate and other loans, including real estate lending, structured loans, bridge finance and loans against lease rentals.
Investments and treasury operations.

Group role: Holds the two subsidiaries (STCI Primary Dealer and STCI Commodities).
Significance: It represents the shift from a market-making institution to a diversified financing company.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 20, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '7a682bcc-5792-4d05-ae77-7e8b6c760085', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c20-q-1", "text": "Explain STCI Finance Limited.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c20-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Status: The parent company of the group and the successor to the original STCI. It is a systemically important non-deposit taking NBFC (NBFC-ND-SI) registered with the RBI, classified as a loan NBFC.", "History: Formerly Securities Trading Corporation of India Ltd. Renamed STCI Finance Ltd with effect from 24 October 2011.", "Business: Since 2007 it has focused on lending and investment activities:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c20-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Loan against securities / promoter funding.", "Corporate and other loans, including real estate lending, structured loans, bridge finance and loans against lease rentals.", "Investments and treasury operations."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c20-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Group role: Holds the two subsidiaries (STCI Primary Dealer and STCI Commodities).", "Significance: It represents the shift from a market-making institution to a diversified financing company."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('c908f265-4712-4ab9-9dcf-c04ad948231e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain STCI Primary Dealer Limited.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.3', 'Status: A wholly owned subsidiary of STCI Finance Ltd. Incorporated in 2006 and commenced operations on 25 June 2007, after the primary dealership business was hived off from the parent.
Nature: An RBI-authorised Standalone Primary Dealer (SPD), a non-bank entity registered as an NBFC, and one of the larger PDs in India.
Activities:

Underwriting and bidding in G-sec auctions (G-secs, T-bills and CMBs).
Market making (two-way quotes) in G-secs and other debt.
Trading in G-secs, SDLs, T-bills, corporate bonds and money market instruments.
Interest rate derivatives (IRS) for hedging and trading.
Proprietary trading in equity cash and derivatives.
Fee-based activities: portfolio management services and mutual fund distribution.

Significance: Supports liquidity, price discovery and the RBI''s operations in the G-sec market.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 21, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '7a682bcc-5792-4d05-ae77-7e8b6c760085', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c21-q-1", "text": "Explain STCI Primary Dealer Limited.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c21-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Status: A wholly owned subsidiary of STCI Finance Ltd. Incorporated in 2006 and commenced operations on 25 June 2007, after the primary dealership business was hived off from the parent.", "Nature: An RBI-authorised Standalone Primary Dealer (SPD), a non-bank entity registered as an NBFC, and one of the larger PDs in India.", "Activities:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c21-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Underwriting and bidding in G-sec auctions (G-secs, T-bills and CMBs).", "Market making (two-way quotes) in G-secs and other debt.", "Trading in G-secs, SDLs, T-bills, corporate bonds and money market instruments.", "Interest rate derivatives (IRS) for hedging and trading.", "Proprietary trading in equity cash and derivatives.", "Fee-based activities: portfolio management services and mutual fund distribution."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c21-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Significance: Supports liquidity, price discovery and the RBI''s operations in the G-sec market."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('0ea282a2-a0ae-4198-a641-6100b2ae3c69', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain STCI Commodities Limited.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.3', 'Status: A wholly owned subsidiary of STCI Finance Ltd.
Purpose (concept): It was created to provide brokerage, investment and advisory services in the Indian commodity markets.
Present position: Recent disclosures state that it discontinued commodity broking in 2011 and is under voluntary liquidation (verify).
Significance in the group: It shows STCI''s diversification beyond debt. It is not part of the debt-market activities, which sit with STCI Primary Dealer and the parent.
Exam tip: In an exam, describe its business as brokerage, investment and advisory in commodities, and add a one-line note on the discontinued operations if you are confident.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 22, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '7a682bcc-5792-4d05-ae77-7e8b6c760085', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c22-q-1", "text": "Explain STCI Commodities Limited.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c22-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Status: A wholly owned subsidiary of STCI Finance Ltd.", "Purpose (concept): It was created to provide brokerage, investment and advisory services in the Indian commodity markets.", "Present position: Recent disclosures state that it discontinued commodity broking in 2011 and is under voluntary liquidation (verify).", "Significance in the group: It shows STCI''s diversification beyond debt. It is not part of the debt-market activities, which sit with STCI Primary Dealer and the parent.", "Exam tip: In an exam, describe its business as brokerage, investment and advisory in commodities, and add a one-line note on the discontinued operations if you are confident."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('7d4ec89f-4a77-4ae6-b5d9-e1aac1b387b9', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'STCI group at a glance, and how to answer the 2025 Q3.3 pattern.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: 2025 Q3.3 (7.5m); Oct 2024 Q3P', 'Entity | Nature | Core business | Link to debt market
STCI Finance Ltd | Parent; NBFC-ND-SI; formerly Securities Trading Corporation of India | Lending (loans against securities, corporate loans) and investments | Indirect (holds the PD)
STCI Primary Dealer Ltd | 100% subsidiary; RBI-authorised standalone PD | Underwriting, market making and trading in G-secs, bonds and money market | Direct
STCI Commodities Ltd | 100% subsidiary | Commodity brokerage, investment and advisory (broking discontinued in 2011) | None

Answer plan for 7.5 marks: Short intro on STCI (2 lines) + 2–3 marks per entity (nature, business, significance). Answer plan for a 10-mark "STCI + Corporate Bonds" question: STCI (concept, evolution, group, role) for about 5 marks + Corporate Bonds (meaning, features, types, working) for about 5 marks.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 23, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '7a682bcc-5792-4d05-ae77-7e8b6c760085', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c23-q-1", "text": "STCI group at a glance, and how to answer the 2025 Q3.3 pattern.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c23-a-1", "rows": [["Entity", "Nature", "Core business", "Link to debt market"], ["STCI Finance Ltd", "Parent; NBFC-ND-SI; formerly Securities Trading Corporation of India", "Lending (loans against securities, corporate loans) and investments", "Indirect (holds the PD)"], ["STCI Primary Dealer Ltd", "100% subsidiary; RBI-authorised standalone PD", "Underwriting, market making and trading in G-secs, bonds and money market", "Direct"], ["STCI Commodities Ltd", "100% subsidiary", "Commodity brokerage, investment and advisory (broking discontinued in 2011)", "None"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c23-a-2", "text": "Answer plan for 7.5 marks: Short intro on STCI (2 lines) + 2–3 marks per entity (nature, business, significance). Answer plan for a 10-mark \"STCI + Corporate Bonds\" question: STCI (concept, evolution, group, role) for about 5 marks + Corporate Bonds (meaning, features, types, working) for about 5 marks.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('5e566865-4017-42b0-abf5-fc3366b55c2e', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'What are Primary Dealers? Explain their meaning, objectives and types.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3A (10m, with OMO)', 'Meaning: Primary Dealers (PDs) are specialised financial institutions authorised by the RBI to underwrite and bid in the primary auctions of G-secs, make markets in the secondary market, and support the RBI in OMO. They are sometimes called merchant bankers to the Government.
Introduced: The RBI introduced the PD system in 1995, and the first PDs began operating in 1996.
Objectives (as stated by the RBI):

Strengthen the infrastructure of the G-sec market.
Improve the secondary market trading system, to help price discovery, liquidity and turnover.
Encourage voluntary holding of G-secs among a wider investor base.
Make PDs an effective conduit for OMO.

Types:

Standalone PDs: non-bank entities registered as NBFCs (for example, PNB Gilts, ICICI Securities PD, SBI DFHI, STCI PD).
Bank PDs: banks doing the PD business departmentally (permitted from 2006–07).

There are roughly 7 standalone and 14 bank PDs (verify current numbers).
Eligibility (summary): minimum net owned fund (₹50 crore for applicants), suitable track record, and standalone PDs registered as NBFCs for at least one year. The RBI decides based on market needs.
Regulation: RBI (a minimum capital adequacy of 15% for standalone PDs, under its 2025 Directions; verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 24, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '4bd73fbc-48d3-4336-b1dc-4d73379d0db2', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c24-q-1", "text": "What are Primary Dealers? Explain their meaning, objectives and types.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c24-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Primary Dealers (PDs) are specialised financial institutions authorised by the RBI to underwrite and bid in the primary auctions of G-secs, make markets in the secondary market, and support the RBI in OMO. They are sometimes called merchant bankers to the Government.", "Introduced: The RBI introduced the PD system in 1995, and the first PDs began operating in 1996.", "Objectives (as stated by the RBI):"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c24-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Strengthen the infrastructure of the G-sec market.", "Improve the secondary market trading system, to help price discovery, liquidity and turnover.", "Encourage voluntary holding of G-secs among a wider investor base.", "Make PDs an effective conduit for OMO."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c24-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Types:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c24-a-4", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Standalone PDs: non-bank entities registered as NBFCs (for example, PNB Gilts, ICICI Securities PD, SBI DFHI, STCI PD).", "Bank PDs: banks doing the PD business departmentally (permitted from 2006–07)."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c24-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["There are roughly 7 standalone and 14 bank PDs (verify current numbers).", "Eligibility (summary): minimum net owned fund (₹50 crore for applicants), suitable track record, and standalone PDs registered as NBFCs for at least one year. The RBI decides based on market needs.", "Regulation: RBI (a minimum capital adequacy of 15% for standalone PDs, under its 2025 Directions; verify)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('de9014fb-8a7e-4218-88a0-5f8eab4ea4af', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the functions and role of Primary Dealers in the Government Securities market.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3A', 'Underwriting: PDs commit to bid for and underwrite G-sec, T-bill and CMB issues, ensuring that the Government''s borrowing is fully subscribed.
Participation in auctions: They must meet bidding commitments and success ratios prescribed by the RBI (verify current norms).
Market making: They quote two-way (buy and sell) prices in the secondary market, providing liquidity.
Price discovery: Their active quotes help form the yield curve.
Distribution: They sell securities to retail investors, corporates and institutions, and widen the investor base.
Conduit for OMO: They are the RBI''s key counterparties in OMO and in liquidity operations.
Advisory role: They advise the Government/RBI on debt management and market development.
Trading in related markets: Corporate bonds, money market instruments and interest rate derivatives (where permitted).
Access to facilities: They can borrow and lend in the call/repo market and use the RBI''s repo window.
Obligations: Capital adequacy, risk management, reporting to the RBI and turnover targets.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 25, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '4bd73fbc-48d3-4336-b1dc-4d73379d0db2', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c25-q-1", "text": "Explain the functions and role of Primary Dealers in the Government Securities market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c25-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Underwriting: PDs commit to bid for and underwrite G-sec, T-bill and CMB issues, ensuring that the Government''s borrowing is fully subscribed.", "Participation in auctions: They must meet bidding commitments and success ratios prescribed by the RBI (verify current norms).", "Market making: They quote two-way (buy and sell) prices in the secondary market, providing liquidity.", "Price discovery: Their active quotes help form the yield curve.", "Distribution: They sell securities to retail investors, corporates and institutions, and widen the investor base.", "Conduit for OMO: They are the RBI''s key counterparties in OMO and in liquidity operations.", "Advisory role: They advise the Government/RBI on debt management and market development.", "Trading in related markets: Corporate bonds, money market instruments and interest rate derivatives (where permitted).", "Access to facilities: They can borrow and lend in the call/repo market and use the RBI''s repo window.", "Obligations: Capital adequacy, risk management, reporting to the RBI and turnover targets."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('82f5c4cf-1b80-437f-893c-c0d9e9ffc658', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Answer plan for "Elaborate in detail Open Market Operations and Primary Dealers" (10 marks).', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3A (10m)', 'Marks split: OMO ≈ 5 marks and Primary Dealers ≈ 5 marks.
OMO (5 marks): definition (1) + purposes, 4 points (1.5) + types and process (1.5) + effect on liquidity and yields (1).
PDs (5 marks): definition and introduction in 1995 (1) + objectives (1) + types (1) + functions, 5–6 points (2).
Link line (bonus): "PDs are the RBI''s main counterparties in OMO auctions. Together they deepen the G-sec market and help transmit monetary policy."
Diagram idea: RBI → OMO auction → PDs/banks → investors, with arrows showing liquidity in and out.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 26, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '4bd73fbc-48d3-4336-b1dc-4d73379d0db2', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c26-q-1", "text": "Answer plan for \"Elaborate in detail Open Market Operations and Primary Dealers\" (10 marks).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c26-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Marks split: OMO ≈ 5 marks and Primary Dealers ≈ 5 marks.", "OMO (5 marks): definition (1) + purposes, 4 points (1.5) + types and process (1.5) + effect on liquidity and yields (1).", "PDs (5 marks): definition and introduction in 1995 (1) + objectives (1) + types (1) + functions, 5–6 points (2).", "Link line (bonus): \"PDs are the RBI''s main counterparties in OMO auctions. Together they deepen the G-sec market and help transmit monetary policy.\"", "Diagram idea: RBI → OMO auction → PDs/banks → investors, with arrows showing liquidity in and out."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('5c2506a9-5fb7-4c4e-89e8-f9fa7225b54f', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the role of Institutional Investors in the Debt Market.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Role of Institutional Investors"; not asked in these two papers', 'Meaning: Institutional investors are large organisations that pool funds and invest them in securities. In the debt market they are banks, mutual funds, insurance companies and pension funds.
Common roles:

Anchor demand for G-secs, SDLs, PSU and corporate bonds.
Liquidity and depth: large, regular trading improves secondary market liquidity.
Price discovery: their bids set yields and credit spreads.
Long-term capital: they fund infrastructure and government borrowing.
Stabilise the market: long-horizon investors (insurers, pension funds) reduce volatility.
Credit discipline: they insist on ratings and disclosures.
Channel savings: they bring household savings into the bond market.

Key differences: Banks (regulatory SLR holding, liquidity), mutual funds (pooling retail savings, credit and duration strategies), insurers (long-term liability matching) and pension funds (very long horizon). See Cards 28–32.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 27, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '66f8af16-45e3-4cfa-ba06-990bbe5b0c55', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c27-q-1", "text": "Explain the role of Institutional Investors in the Debt Market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c27-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Institutional investors are large organisations that pool funds and invest them in securities. In the debt market they are banks, mutual funds, insurance companies and pension funds.", "Common roles:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c27-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Anchor demand for G-secs, SDLs, PSU and corporate bonds.", "Liquidity and depth: large, regular trading improves secondary market liquidity.", "Price discovery: their bids set yields and credit spreads.", "Long-term capital: they fund infrastructure and government borrowing.", "Stabilise the market: long-horizon investors (insurers, pension funds) reduce volatility.", "Credit discipline: they insist on ratings and disclosures.", "Channel savings: they bring household savings into the bond market."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c27-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Key differences: Banks (regulatory SLR holding, liquidity), mutual funds (pooling retail savings, credit and duration strategies), insurers (long-term liability matching) and pension funds (very long horizon). See Cards 28–32."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('2df1b0af-d7b6-495f-a0b3-de20ce1121d4', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the role of Banks in the Debt Market.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Largest holders of G-secs and SDLs, because banks must hold the Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR) in government securities (currently 18% of net demand and time liabilities, verify).
Primary subscribers in G-sec and T-bill auctions.
Market makers: banks act as Bank Primary Dealers and quote prices in the market.
Liquidity management: They use G-secs as collateral in repo/LAF and in the call money market.
Investors in PSU and corporate bonds, as well as CPs and CDs, for yield and diversification.
Issuers: They raise funds through infrastructure bonds, Tier 2 and AT1 bonds.
Asset-liability management: They hold securities to match liabilities, subject to RBI classification and valuation norms.

Risk to note: Banks face interest-rate risk on their bond portfolios when yields rise.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 28, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '66f8af16-45e3-4cfa-ba06-990bbe5b0c55', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c28-q-1", "text": "Explain the role of Banks in the Debt Market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c28-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Largest holders of G-secs and SDLs, because banks must hold the Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR) in government securities (currently 18% of net demand and time liabilities, verify).", "Primary subscribers in G-sec and T-bill auctions.", "Market makers: banks act as Bank Primary Dealers and quote prices in the market.", "Liquidity management: They use G-secs as collateral in repo/LAF and in the call money market.", "Investors in PSU and corporate bonds, as well as CPs and CDs, for yield and diversification.", "Issuers: They raise funds through infrastructure bonds, Tier 2 and AT1 bonds.", "Asset-liability management: They hold securities to match liabilities, subject to RBI classification and valuation norms."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c28-a-2", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Risk to note: Banks face interest-rate risk on their bond portfolios when yields rise."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('865dbb9b-267a-4830-b4ae-0fe38dce8a1d', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the role of Pension Funds in the Debt Market.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Who: EPFO (provident and pension funds), NPS (managed by Pension Fund Managers under PFRDA), and superannuation and gratuity funds.
Role:

Very long-term investors, with predictable contributions and payouts.
Major buyers of G-secs, SDLs and high-rated PSU/corporate bonds.
Deepen the long end of the yield curve and provide steady demand.
Stability: They rarely trade actively, so they dampen volatility.
Infrastructure finance: They invest in infrastructure and PSU bonds.

Investment norms: EPFO invests mainly in debt, with a limited equity exposure through ETFs. NPS follows PFRDA guidelines across asset classes (G = government securities, C = corporate debt) (verify limits).
Preference: Safety and credit quality (AAA/AA), with a long holding horizon.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 31, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '66f8af16-45e3-4cfa-ba06-990bbe5b0c55', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c31-q-1", "text": "Explain the role of Pension Funds in the Debt Market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c31-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Who: EPFO (provident and pension funds), NPS (managed by Pension Fund Managers under PFRDA), and superannuation and gratuity funds.", "Role:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c31-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Very long-term investors, with predictable contributions and payouts.", "Major buyers of G-secs, SDLs and high-rated PSU/corporate bonds.", "Deepen the long end of the yield curve and provide steady demand.", "Stability: They rarely trade actively, so they dampen volatility.", "Infrastructure finance: They invest in infrastructure and PSU bonds."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c31-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Investment norms: EPFO invests mainly in debt, with a limited equity exposure through ETFs. NPS follows PFRDA guidelines across asset classes (G = government securities, C = corporate debt) (verify limits).", "Preference: Safety and credit quality (AAA/AA), with a long holding horizon."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('2548f72c-8017-40c9-9fef-b9d2f1b93f6f', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Compare the four Institutional Investors in the Debt Market.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus', 'Basis | Banks | Mutual Funds | Insurance Companies | Pension Funds
Regulator | RBI | SEBI | IRDAI | PFRDA / EPFO norms
Source of funds | Deposits | Investors'' units | Premiums | Contributions
Horizon | Short to medium | Varies by scheme | Long | Very long
Main motive | SLR compliance, liquidity, yield | Returns for unit-holders | Match long-term liabilities | Safety and stable returns
Favourite instruments | G-secs, SDLs, CPs/CDs, bonds | CPs, CDs, corporate bonds, G-secs | Long G-secs, SDLs, AAA bonds | G-secs, SDLs, AAA PSU bonds
Unique role | Largest G-sec holders; PDs | Bring retail savings and liquidity in corporate bonds | Long-tenor anchor buyers | Deepen the long end', NULL, NULL, NULL, 32, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '66f8af16-45e3-4cfa-ba06-990bbe5b0c55', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c32-q-1", "text": "Compare the four Institutional Investors in the Debt Market.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c32-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Banks", "Mutual Funds", "Insurance Companies", "Pension Funds"], ["Regulator", "RBI", "SEBI", "IRDAI", "PFRDA / EPFO norms"], ["Source of funds", "Deposits", "Investors'' units", "Premiums", "Contributions"], ["Horizon", "Short to medium", "Varies by scheme", "Long", "Very long"], ["Main motive", "SLR compliance, liquidity, yield", "Returns for unit-holders", "Match long-term liabilities", "Safety and stable returns"], ["Favourite instruments", "G-secs, SDLs, CPs/CDs, bonds", "CPs, CDs, corporate bonds, G-secs", "Long G-secs, SDLs, AAA bonds", "G-secs, SDLs, AAA PSU bonds"], ["Unique role", "Largest G-sec holders; PDs", "Bring retail savings and liquidity in corporate bonds", "Long-tenor anchor buyers", "Deepen the long end"]], "type": "table"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('115f7873-6a77-4e0c-aad3-4adf4744a19f', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Define Bonds. Explain their meaning and importance.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3B ("Define Bonds")', 'Definition: A bond is a debt security (fixed-income instrument) in which the issuer borrows money from the investor for a fixed period and promises to pay periodic interest (coupon) and to repay the principal (face value) on the maturity date. The bondholder is a creditor, not an owner.
Issuers: Governments, public sector undertakings, companies, financial institutions and municipalities.
Importance:

To issuers: long-term funds without dilution of ownership.
To investors: regular income, capital safety (if high-rated) and diversification.
To the economy: mobilises savings, finances infrastructure, transmits monetary policy and provides a benchmark yield curve.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 33, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '14e8f4fd-28a5-4bb2-8bda-c6509ea49c90', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c33-q-1", "text": "Define Bonds. Explain their meaning and importance.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c33-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Definition: A bond is a debt security (fixed-income instrument) in which the issuer borrows money from the investor for a fixed period and promises to pay periodic interest (coupon) and to repay the principal (face value) on the maturity date. The bondholder is a creditor, not an owner.", "Issuers: Governments, public sector undertakings, companies, financial institutions and municipalities.", "Importance:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c33-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["To issuers: long-term funds without dilution of ownership.", "To investors: regular income, capital safety (if high-rated) and diversification.", "To the economy: mobilises savings, finances infrastructure, transmits monetary policy and provides a benchmark yield curve."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('1c7bc3ce-838e-447c-9ae5-e1792cdd606d', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Features of Bonds.', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3B ("Features")', 'Face (par) value: the amount repaid at maturity (for example, ₹100 or ₹1,000).
Coupon rate: the interest rate on face value. It can be fixed, floating or zero. Payment is periodic (half-yearly for G-secs).
Maturity: the date on which principal is repaid.
Issue price: at par, at a discount or at a premium.
Issuer and credit quality: shown through a credit rating.
Security: secured (charge on assets) or unsecured.
Priority of claim: bondholders are paid before shareholders.
Negotiability and trading: bonds are tradable in demat form, and prices move with interest rates.
Optionality: call, put or convertibility features may be included.
Yield: current yield and yield to maturity measure the return. Bond prices and yields move inversely.
Contractual obligation: governed by a trust deed / offer document. A debenture trustee looks after corporate bondholders.
No ownership or voting rights.
Risks: interest-rate risk, credit risk, reinvestment risk, inflation risk and liquidity risk.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 34, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '14e8f4fd-28a5-4bb2-8bda-c6509ea49c90', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c34-q-1", "text": "Explain the Features of Bonds.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c34-a-1", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Face (par) value: the amount repaid at maturity (for example, ₹100 or ₹1,000).", "Coupon rate: the interest rate on face value. It can be fixed, floating or zero. Payment is periodic (half-yearly for G-secs).", "Maturity: the date on which principal is repaid.", "Issue price: at par, at a discount or at a premium.", "Issuer and credit quality: shown through a credit rating.", "Security: secured (charge on assets) or unsecured.", "Priority of claim: bondholders are paid before shareholders.", "Negotiability and trading: bonds are tradable in demat form, and prices move with interest rates.", "Optionality: call, put or convertibility features may be included.", "Yield: current yield and yield to maturity measure the return. Bond prices and yields move inversely.", "Contractual obligation: governed by a trust deed / offer document. A debenture trustee looks after corporate bondholders.", "No ownership or voting rights.", "Risks: interest-rate risk, credit risk, reinvestment risk, inflation risk and liquidity risk."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('9e638a9b-27a0-4446-a4c3-619ae6473b92', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain the Types of Bonds (classification).', 'Priority: Tier 1 | PYQ Connection: Oct 2024 Q3B ("Types"); 2025 Q3.1 and Q3.2 (types of G-secs and corporate bonds)', 'Basis | Types
Issuer | Government securities, PSU bonds, Corporate bonds, Municipal bonds, Supranational bonds (World Bank, ADB), Foreign bonds
Coupon | Fixed rate, Floating rate, Zero-coupon / Deep-discount, Step-up / Step-down, Inflation-indexed
Maturity | Short-term (up to about 3 years), Medium-term, Long-term, Perpetual (no maturity)
Security | Secured, Unsecured
Convertibility | Convertible (fully / partly), Non-convertible
Redemption features | Bullet, Amortising, Callable, Putable, Sinking-fund bonds
Seniority | Senior, Subordinated (Tier 2), AT1 / Junior
Tax treatment | Taxable, Tax-free
Currency / market | Rupee bonds, Foreign-currency bonds, Masala bonds, Eurobonds
Purpose / theme | Green, Social, Sustainability, Infrastructure, Sovereign Gold, Savings and Capital-gains (54EC) bonds

Exam tip: For 9 marks, use 6–7 bases with one line of explanation each, and cross-refer to G-sec and corporate bond types.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 35, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '14e8f4fd-28a5-4bb2-8bda-c6509ea49c90', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c35-q-1", "text": "Explain the Types of Bonds (classification).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c35-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Types"], ["Issuer", "Government securities, PSU bonds, Corporate bonds, Municipal bonds, Supranational bonds (World Bank, ADB), Foreign bonds"], ["Coupon", "Fixed rate, Floating rate, Zero-coupon / Deep-discount, Step-up / Step-down, Inflation-indexed"], ["Maturity", "Short-term (up to about 3 years), Medium-term, Long-term, Perpetual (no maturity)"], ["Security", "Secured, Unsecured"], ["Convertibility", "Convertible (fully / partly), Non-convertible"], ["Redemption features", "Bullet, Amortising, Callable, Putable, Sinking-fund bonds"], ["Seniority", "Senior, Subordinated (Tier 2), AT1 / Junior"], ["Tax treatment", "Taxable, Tax-free"], ["Currency / market", "Rupee bonds, Foreign-currency bonds, Masala bonds, Eurobonds"], ["Purpose / theme", "Green, Social, Sustainability, Infrastructure, Sovereign Gold, Savings and Capital-gains (54EC) bonds"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c35-a-2", "text": "Exam tip: For 9 marks, use 6–7 bases with one line of explanation each, and cross-refer to G-sec and corporate bond types.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph", "italic": true}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('eda35801-590f-4e50-85e6-3d80c70b0b8a', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Key bond terms (glossary).', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Supports Oct 2024 Q3B', 'Par / face value: principal repaid at maturity. Coupon: interest rate on face value.
Discount bond / premium bond: trades below / above face value.
Current yield = annual coupon ÷ current price. Yield to maturity (YTM): total return if held to maturity (Unit 4).
Call / put option: issuer''s right to redeem early / holder''s right to sell back early.
Credit spread: yield of a bond over the risk-free (G-sec) yield.
Indenture / trust deed; debenture trustee: contract and guardian of bondholders.
ISIN: unique identification number of a security. Accrued interest: interest earned since the last coupon date.
Sinking fund: money set aside to repay the bond at maturity.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 36, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '14e8f4fd-28a5-4bb2-8bda-c6509ea49c90', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c36-q-1", "text": "Key bond terms (glossary).", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c36-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Par / face value: principal repaid at maturity. Coupon: interest rate on face value.", "Discount bond / premium bond: trades below / above face value.", "Current yield = annual coupon ÷ current price. Yield to maturity (YTM): total return if held to maturity (Unit 4).", "Call / put option: issuer''s right to redeem early / holder''s right to sell back early.", "Credit spread: yield of a bond over the risk-free (G-sec) yield.", "Indenture / trust deed; debenture trustee: contract and guardian of bondholders.", "ISIN: unique identification number of a security. Accrued interest: interest earned since the last coupon date.", "Sinking fund: money set aside to repay the bond at maturity."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('6befb54c-92bf-402b-8ad5-027323af295b', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Distinguish between Bonds and Equity Shares, and between Bonds and Debentures.', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Supports Oct 2024 Q3B', 'Basis | Bonds | Equity Shares
Nature | Debt (borrowing) | Ownership
Holder | Creditor | Owner
Return | Fixed coupon | Variable dividend and capital gain
Maturity | Fixed | No maturity
Risk | Lower | Higher
Claim in liquidation | Prior to shareholders | Residual
Voting rights | No | Yes
Cost to issuer | Interest is tax-deductible | Dividend is not

Bonds vs Debentures: In India the terms are used almost interchangeably (under the Companies Act a company''s debenture includes bonds). In practice, debentures are issued by companies and are often secured on assets, while bonds are issued by governments, PSUs and financial institutions and usually rely on the issuer''s credit.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 37, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '14e8f4fd-28a5-4bb2-8bda-c6509ea49c90', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c37-q-1", "text": "Distinguish between Bonds and Equity Shares, and between Bonds and Debentures.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c37-a-1", "rows": [["Basis", "Bonds", "Equity Shares"], ["Nature", "Debt (borrowing)", "Ownership"], ["Holder", "Creditor", "Owner"], ["Return", "Fixed coupon", "Variable dividend and capital gain"], ["Maturity", "Fixed", "No maturity"], ["Risk", "Lower", "Higher"], ["Claim in liquidation", "Prior to shareholders", "Residual"], ["Voting rights", "No", "Yes"], ["Cost to issuer", "Interest is tax-deductible", "Dividend is not"]], "type": "table"}, {"id": "edm-u3-c37-a-2", "text": "Bonds vs Debentures: In India the terms are used almost interchangeably (under the Companies Act a company''s debenture includes bonds). In practice, debentures are issued by companies and are often secured on assets, while bonds are issued by governments, PSUs and financial institutions and usually rely on the issuer''s credit.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('fda4d501-8aae-427d-af28-cba02a279386', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'What are Green Bonds? Explain their meaning, purpose, features and significance.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Green Bonds"; not asked in these two papers', 'Meaning: A green bond is a debt instrument whose proceeds are used exclusively to finance or refinance environmentally beneficial projects. It works like a normal bond (coupon and principal), but the use of proceeds is ring-fenced for green purposes.
Purpose: To channel capital to renewable energy, energy efficiency, clean transport, sustainable water and waste management, green buildings, pollution control and climate adaptation.
Features (ICMA Green Bond Principles):

Use of proceeds clearly stated.
Process for project evaluation and selection.
Management of proceeds (tracked and ring-fenced).
Regular reporting on the use and impact.
External review / certification (second-party opinion) is common.

Who can issue: Governments, PSUs, companies and municipalities. A green bond is defined by its purpose, not by its issuer.
Significance:

Funds the climate transition and India''s sustainability goals.
Widens the investor base (ESG-focused funds).
Improves the issuer''s reputation and disclosure.
Ties debt markets to ESG finance.

Limitations: Greenwashing risk, reporting and certification costs, limited liquidity and a small market.
India:
Yes Bank (2015) issued the first green bond.
SEBI issued green debt securities guidelines in 2017 and revised them in 2023.
The Government issued its first Sovereign Green Bonds in Jan–Feb 2023 (₹16,000 crore across two tranches, verify).
Municipal green bonds: Lucknow (2021), Indore and Vadodara (2023).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 38, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '52953b6e-aec4-4e2f-900e-bb2718655601', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c38-q-1", "text": "What are Green Bonds? Explain their meaning, purpose, features and significance.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c38-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: A green bond is a debt instrument whose proceeds are used exclusively to finance or refinance environmentally beneficial projects. It works like a normal bond (coupon and principal), but the use of proceeds is ring-fenced for green purposes.", "Purpose: To channel capital to renewable energy, energy efficiency, clean transport, sustainable water and waste management, green buildings, pollution control and climate adaptation.", "Features (ICMA Green Bond Principles):"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c38-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Use of proceeds clearly stated.", "Process for project evaluation and selection.", "Management of proceeds (tracked and ring-fenced).", "Regular reporting on the use and impact.", "External review / certification (second-party opinion) is common."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c38-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Who can issue: Governments, PSUs, companies and municipalities. A green bond is defined by its purpose, not by its issuer.", "Significance:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c38-a-4", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Funds the climate transition and India''s sustainability goals.", "Widens the investor base (ESG-focused funds).", "Improves the issuer''s reputation and disclosure.", "Ties debt markets to ESG finance."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c38-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Limitations: Greenwashing risk, reporting and certification costs, limited liquidity and a small market.", "India:", "Yes Bank (2015) issued the first green bond.", "SEBI issued green debt securities guidelines in 2017 and revised them in 2023.", "The Government issued its first Sovereign Green Bonds in Jan–Feb 2023 (₹16,000 crore across two tranches, verify).", "Municipal green bonds: Lucknow (2021), Indore and Vadodara (2023)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('bbd9f5ba-2d09-4288-a961-499da0540882', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'What are ESG debt securities? How do Green Bonds relate to sustainable finance?', 'Priority: Tier 3 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Green Bonds"; links to ESG in Unit 1', 'ESG debt securities is SEBI''s umbrella term (introduced through a December 2024 amendment to the NCS Regulations) covering:

Green debt securities: proceeds for environmental projects.
Social bonds: proceeds for social outcomes (affordable housing, health, education, financial inclusion).
Sustainability bonds: a mix of green and social projects.
Sustainability-linked bonds (SLBs): proceeds are general-purpose, but the coupon is linked to sustainability targets (for example, emission reduction).

SEBI''s framework: Green debt rules date from 2017 (revised 2023). A separate framework for ESG debt securities other than green was issued in June 2025, with third-party review and post-issue reporting (verify).
Relationship: Green bonds are the oldest and largest ESG debt category. The rest are newer and extend the same disclosure discipline to other sustainability goals.
Anti-greenwashing: SEBI has issued "do''s and don''ts" for green debt issuers.', NULL, NULL, NULL, 39, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '52953b6e-aec4-4e2f-900e-bb2718655601', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c39-q-1", "text": "What are ESG debt securities? How do Green Bonds relate to sustainable finance?", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c39-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["ESG debt securities is SEBI''s umbrella term (introduced through a December 2024 amendment to the NCS Regulations) covering:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c39-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Green debt securities: proceeds for environmental projects.", "Social bonds: proceeds for social outcomes (affordable housing, health, education, financial inclusion).", "Sustainability bonds: a mix of green and social projects.", "Sustainability-linked bonds (SLBs): proceeds are general-purpose, but the coupon is linked to sustainability targets (for example, emission reduction)."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c39-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["SEBI''s framework: Green debt rules date from 2017 (revised 2023). A separate framework for ESG debt securities other than green was issued in June 2025, with third-party review and post-issue reporting (verify).", "Relationship: Green bonds are the oldest and largest ESG debt category. The rest are newer and extend the same disclosure discipline to other sustainability goals.", "Anti-greenwashing: SEBI has issued \"do''s and don''ts\" for green debt issuers."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00'),
('bb8592e9-12fc-4e22-9eed-72f2ed41e752', '4942b243-3eda-4100-9200-942a29e98d11', 'flashcard', 'Explain Municipal Bonds: meaning, features, purpose and importance.', 'Priority: Tier 2 | PYQ Connection: Syllabus "Municipal Bonds"; not asked in these two papers', 'Meaning: Municipal bonds ("muni bonds") are debt securities issued by Urban Local Bodies (ULBs), such as municipal corporations, to finance urban infrastructure and public services (water supply, sewerage, roads, solid waste management and transport).
Features:

Issuer is a local government body (Art. 243Q of the Constitution).
Regulated by SEBI under the Issue and Listing of Municipal Debt Securities (ILMDS) Regulations 2015.
Credit rating is required, and eligibility and disclosure norms apply.
Repayment is backed by the ULB''s general revenues (general obligation) or a specific project''s revenues (revenue bonds), often with an escrow of receipts.
Issued by public issue or private placement, and listed.
Yield is generally above G-secs and SDLs, depending on the rating.

Purpose: To let cities raise funds from the market instead of depending only on grants and state or central transfers.
Importance:

Finances urban infrastructure, where funding needs are large.
Promotes fiscal discipline and transparency in city finances.
Develops a new asset class and widens the investor base.
Encourages decentralised financing.

India:
Bangalore Municipal Corporation issued the first municipal bond in 1997.
The SEBI ILMDS Regulations followed in 2015.
Pune Municipal Corporation raised ₹200 crore in 2017 for 24×7 water supply.
Central incentives exist (for example, under AMRUT, ₹13 crore per ₹100 crore raised, capped per ULB, verify).
Challenges: Weak ULB finances and ratings, limited own-revenue, small issue sizes and limited investor interest. SEBI has proposed further reforms (verify).', NULL, NULL, NULL, 40, true, 'University Exam', NULL, NULL, NULL, '3e54a28d-992c-471c-bd9a-f9778f1e0aec', '52953b6e-aec4-4e2f-900e-bb2718655601', '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c40-q-1", "text": "Explain Municipal Bonds: meaning, features, purpose and importance.", "type": "text", "style": "paragraph"}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '{"blocks": [{"id": "edm-u3-c40-a-1", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Meaning: Municipal bonds (\"muni bonds\") are debt securities issued by Urban Local Bodies (ULBs), such as municipal corporations, to finance urban infrastructure and public services (water supply, sewerage, roads, solid waste management and transport).", "Features:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c40-a-2", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Issuer is a local government body (Art. 243Q of the Constitution).", "Regulated by SEBI under the Issue and Listing of Municipal Debt Securities (ILMDS) Regulations 2015.", "Credit rating is required, and eligibility and disclosure norms apply.", "Repayment is backed by the ULB''s general revenues (general obligation) or a specific project''s revenues (revenue bonds), often with an escrow of receipts.", "Issued by public issue or private placement, and listed.", "Yield is generally above G-secs and SDLs, depending on the rating."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c40-a-3", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["Purpose: To let cities raise funds from the market instead of depending only on grants and state or central transfers.", "Importance:"]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c40-a-4", "type": "numberList", "items": ["Finances urban infrastructure, where funding needs are large.", "Promotes fiscal discipline and transparency in city finances.", "Develops a new asset class and widens the investor base.", "Encourages decentralised financing."]}, {"id": "edm-u3-c40-a-5", "type": "bulletList", "items": ["India:", "Bangalore Municipal Corporation issued the first municipal bond in 1997.", "The SEBI ILMDS Regulations followed in 2015.", "Pune Municipal Corporation raised ₹200 crore in 2017 for 24×7 water supply.", "Central incentives exist (for example, under AMRUT, ₹13 crore per ₹100 crore raised, capped per ULB, verify).", "Challenges: Weak ULB finances and ratings, limited own-revenue, small issue sizes and limited investor interest. SEBI has proposed further reforms (verify)."]}], "version": 1}'::jsonb, '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00', '2026-09-20 18:07:23.563655+00')
